regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:5886:The source allows a guest `CALL_PAL` to reach `swpctx`, but that alone does not prove the offset defect occurs during a boot: the overwritten root can lead to a failed walk. I found no successful Alpha boot documented in the allowed files. The decisive observation is a guest access reaching the successful walk return, with a nonzero offset, and then consuming the page-base address.
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6343:**VERDICT: UNSETTLED for an actual Alpha guest boot. The offset defect is real, but neither LIVE boot impact nor LATENT/unreachable status is established. The PTBR hinge contains another defect.**
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6349:CPU allocation initially zeroes it. Alpha machine setup supplies zero as the initial level-one-table PFN in `a1`. The only explicit runtime assignments to `apcb_ptbr` are in the implemented `PAL_OSF1_swpctx` handler:
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6355:There is no guest memory translation between those assignments. Consequently, the value available to subsequent walks is **the incoming PCB flags**, not its PTBR. [`swpctx` implementation](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:229)
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6359:| Incoming PCB flags | PTBR after `swpctx` | `(uint64_t)(PTBR << 13)` |
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6368:**Q1: Alpha incompleteness does not establish that the walk is unreachable.**
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6374:The missing evidence is whether an actual guest reaches `swpctx` with flags producing a nonzero base **and subsequently supplies a valid three-level walk**. Entering the walker and failing its first lookup would not demonstrate this offset defect.
regress\artifacts\alphapfnoffset\codex_reachability_20260907.txt:6397:- `swpctx` writes the old context into **`a0`**, although the supplied ABI wrapper returns **`v0`**. This independently contradicts the handler’s contract. [Handler](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:250), [ABI wrapper](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h:449)
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\HARNESS_review.md:60:`swpctx` calls later, `PCB_A+16` should hold the saved `apcb_ptbr` — `0` if the handler is correct,
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\codex_q3_20260908.txt:65:These establish an existing basis for testing, **not a completed `alphatbinegsel` differential**. I found no dedicated test in the searched regression sources and ran no before/after comparison. The defensible status is **source-confirmed functional defect; witness unvalidated**. Neither inherent unobservability nor absent paging support justifies closing it as documented-only.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:1:I'll treat this as a premise audit, not a ratification. First I'll confirm the live repo state, then read the PALcode and Alpha dispatch path myself.HEAD matches the checkpoint. Next I’ll read the swpctx handler and Alpha PAL dispatch myself instead of taking the brief’s excerpts as given.The assignment bug is in source. I’ll read the full handler, `call_pal` dispatch, and how Alpha machines enter PAL so Q1/Q2 rest on the code, not the excerpt.An Alpha harness probe already exists and names this defect. I’ll read it, then check translation and PAL gating against the source rather than the probe’s comments.**VERDICT: YES, a rung-3 witness is buildable on the three constructing Alpha machines — but the proposed “no MMU at all, two `swpctx`, read `A+16`” design is unsound as written if `A.flags` is nonzero. After the first switch, `apcb_ptbr` *is* the flags word, the identity map dies, and the second `call_pal` (and the save that is supposed to publish the bug) can fail to land or can `exit(1)` the host. Use K0SEG for code and both PCBs, or keep `flags==0` and accept a weaker oracle. The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous LHS.**
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:26:## Q1 — Can a rung-3 witness be built?
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:30:What makes it rung 3, from the source rather than from a boot story:
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:37:What is **not** established, and will red a witness if copied from the harness comments: **which addresses are RAM.** `-M 64` is `physical_max = 0x4000000`. `0x20000000` is 512 MB. The walk’s special case maps only `0x20000000`–`0x20001fff` onto `PROM_ARGSPACE_PADDR` (8 KB). Identity-mapped `0x20010000` is beyond RAM; `memory_rw` then either warns-and-fails or **accepts a write and stores nothing**. The harness’s own liveness row is the right check for that; a swpctx witness must not inherit `0x20010000`.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:43:## Q2 — Attack the two-`swpctx` design
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:54:Reset `ctx` is `0x10100`. First `swpctx(A)` saves the empty PCB there, then sets `ctx = A` and loads A (and then corrupts `apcb_ptbr`). Second `swpctx` saves to A. That part of the design is right. **Do not use `0x10100` as A or B** — the first save would clobber the block before the load.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:56:One `swpctx` is not enough for a memory oracle. The first save happens *before* the bad assignment, so `0x10100+16` gets the *old* PTBR (0), not the corrupted one. The host `pcb` is wrong after one call, but the debugger does not export `pcb.apcb_ptbr`. The second save is the guest-visible publication.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:66:`swpctx` ends with `invalidate_translation_caches(..., INVALIDATE_ALL)`. Every `run_instr` (including each debugger `step`) begins with `alpha_pc_to_pointers`. On a miss that function calls `translate_v2p`; if that returns 0, Alpha does **not** deliver an exception (`alphafaultclaim` in the source: the `#194` comment claims a fault, the instruction path just fails the access). `pc_to_pointers` then retries the same PC and, still failing, **`exit(1)`s the host** (`FATAL: could not find physical address of the exception handler`).
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:77:**`a0` is clobbered.** The comment says “Return old context in **v0**”; the code assigns **`ALPHA_A0` (r16), not `ALPHA_V0` (r0)**. After `swpctx(A)`, `a0` is `0x10100`. The local `a0` used for the load was captured at handler entry, so the *load* is still A; the *next* guest `call_pal` will use whatever is in r16. Reload `a0` with B before the second call. The `A+16` oracle actually depends on `ctx` still being A, not on `a0==B`; forgetting the reload still saves to A, then loads the dummy PCB. That is a mess, not a discriminator.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:102:6. **`call_pal` itself.** `PAL_whami` (`0x3c`) returns `cpu_id` in `v0` (0) with no PCB side effects. Separates “PAL dispatch is dead” from “swpctx is wrong.”
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:110:10. **Zeros are `call_pal 0` (`PAL_halt`), which is unimplemented and stops the CPU.** A `put` that did not store leaves a halt, which is not a swpctx miss.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:124:- Real OSF/1 `swpctx` (the NetBSD wrapper in the same header) takes a PCB pointer in `a0` and returns the old one in `v0`. It does not smuggle PTBR in flags.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:132:- **`swpctx` always `INVALIDATE_ALL`s.** That is why “the next instruction is already decoded” does not protect a `step` probe.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_witness_20260907.txt:136:- In-tree prior art: `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` is a controls-only Alpha rung-3 harness and names this defect in a comment. It does not yet exercise `swpctx`. Do not copy its “no gating” sentence or its `0x2001xxxx` addresses into a witness.
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\WITNESS_brief.md:1:# alphatbinegsel -- design the rung-3 witness
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\WITNESS_brief.md:44:* **rung 3** = a real guest instruction through real address decode and real `memory_rw`, on an
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\WITNESS_brief.md:56:**Q1. Is a rung-3 witness constructible for this defect, on `-E alpha -e alphabook1`?** Give the
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\WITNESS_brief.md:90:-- say so. And if you conclude no rung-3 witness is constructible, say that plainly with the reason;
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:1:I'll review the probe as code against the Alpha decoder, PAL handler, and debugger — starting with the file and the claimed facts, not the design.The probe is in hand. Next I’ll check the decoder, PAL handler, K0SEG translation, and debugger `put` against the claims.**VERDICT: DO NOT TRUST THE FIRST RUN.** The lda/ldah/ldq bootstrap and the swpctx SAVE/RESTORE walk are right. The encodings that this file actually emits match the decoder. `put d` is the right debugger command. What is wrong is the **address plan**, and it makes three rows able to go green while measuring “unmapped reads return 0”, not the handler.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:17:**`palalive` does not mean the CPU survived swpctx.** `alive` is “the pty is still readable”. `alpha_palcode`’s default arm does `cpu->running = 0` and stays in the debugger because `-V` is single-step. The emulator does not exit. `palalive` stays green.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:73:- PAL comments say swpctx clobbers `t0`. This emulator leaves registers alone. The second `LDQ(A0, 8, T0)` depends on that inaccuracy.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:96:| **`swpctx ptbr`** | Want `0x0`. Unmapped PCB load → flags=0 → even line 282 assigns 0 → unmapped readback 0. **Green on the committed defective build while swpctx never saw MARKER.** |
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:97:| **`swpctx notflags`** | `negate=True` against MARKER. Green for `0x0`, `"died"`, `"absent"`, anything except MARKER. Adds no protection against the zero path. |
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:99:| **`palalive` / `swpctx alive`** | Pty still up. `running = 0` does not kill it. Green after unimplemented palcode, and after a real swpctx. |
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:104:So a first run can look like: `live`/`exec` FAIL, `palgate`/`palalive`/`swpctx alive`/`swpctx ptbr`/`swpctx notflags` ok, `ALPHAHARNESS_RESULT=5/8` or similar, exit 1. The danger is reading `swpctx ptbr ok` as “the handler is correct.” It measured zeros from unmapped memory.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:114:- **Line 251 is a second product bug**, independent of 282: comment says v0, code writes a0. OSF/1 swpctx returns the old PCB in v0. The probe does not use that return, so Q3 is unhurt; any later row that prints `v0` for “old ctx” will be wrong.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_harness_20260907.txt:118:- **Initial `ctx = 0x10100` is in RAM.** The first SAVE of a real swpctx lands there. It does not collide with `0x200xxxxx`. It *would* collide with a PCB parked at `0x10100`.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:1:I'll read the PAL `swpctx` site, PCB fields, and the probe rather than take the brief's field-count and reader claims as given.The load-block count matches. Next I’ll enumerate every `apcb_flags` / `apcb_ptbr` reader and check whether the probe’s two red rows actually depend on flags staying stale.**VERDICT: RATIFY the one-token product fix; do not ratify it as the whole round.** `apcb_flags = new_flags` is the right edit and it does repair both in-cpu consequences. The witness then goes **9/9 while remaining blind to consequence 2**. No reader in this tree relies on the stale field. The overconfident sentence is S3’s “the row records only the first” plus Q1’s “whatever the previous context left.”
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:15:Q1’s “`apcb_flags` is currently whatever the previous context left” is the overconfident bit. `swpctx` has never loaded that field from any PCB. The in-cpu value is **last `wrfen` or 0 from the zeroed cpu struct**, not context N−1’s flags word.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:31:| `swpctx` SAVE `:243-244` | **read**, publish to outgoing PCB | No. Today this **propagates** the bug: the next SAVE writes stale 0 (or last `wrfen`) into the outgoing `+40`, wiping the flags the guest planted. After the fix it writes the flags that were actually loaded — which is the PAL contract. |
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:38:So: making the field track the loaded value changes one observable, the SAVE to `ctx+40`. Nothing in this emulator uses that word for FP enablement, ASN, translation, or the debugger (`CPU_SETTINGS_ADD_REGISTER64` exports `pc` and the 32 GPRs, not PCB fields). Sticky-FEN-across-`swpctx` is a PAL bug, not a behaviour a reader is entitled to.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:40:**What the token does not fix** (already filed or adjacent, not reasons to reject it): `alphaswpctxreg` (`A0` vs `v0`), `alphactxfail` (ignored `memory_rw` status), `wrfen` replacing the **entire** flags word rather than bit 0, `clrfen` unimplemented.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:46:Both red rows read **the same `got`**: `A+16` after the second `swpctx` SAVE. Neither reads `apcb_flags`. Neither reads `A+40`.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:50:1. First `swpctx` loads A: `apcb_ptbr = PTBR_VAL` and is **not** overwritten. `apcb_flags = FLAGS_VAL` (unobserved).
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:51:2. Second `swpctx` SAVE writes `PTBR_VAL` to `A+16`.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:52:3. `swpctx ptbr` wants `0xaaaa1111` → green. `swpctx notflags` wants not `0xbbbb2222` → green. `swpctx mapped` wants not `0` → still green.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:60:A flags-oracle that would pin it, from the same session, no new machine: after the two swaps, `A+40` is **0 on HEAD** (SAVE of never-loaded flags) and `FLAGS_VAL` after the fix. Same page as `A+16`, so `swpctx mapped` already proved the backing exists. That row is not in the file.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:68:**Interaction, and it is real:** without the load, FEN is process-global. `wrfen(1)` then `swpctx` to a PCB whose flags word is 0 leaves `apcb_flags == 1`. The SAVE of the *next* switch then publishes that 1 into the outgoing PCB. The fix makes FEN per-context: SAVE first (so the `wrfen` is preserved on the old PCB), then load the new PCB’s flags. That is the OSF order, not a regression.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:91:The assignment never fires. `:278` still sets PTBR correctly. **`swpctx ptbr` stays green. `swpctx notflags` stays green.** There is no third defect row to go red.
regress\artifacts\alphaswpctxptbr\grok_fix_20260907.txt:108:2. `r[A0]` is written at `:251` before the load (and the comment says `v0` — `alphaswpctxreg`).
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\VERIFY_brief.md:60:    alphaswpctxreg    swpctx stores the old context in a0; the ABI wrapper reads v0, and the
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\VERIFY_brief.md:62:    alphactxfail      swpctx's context save and load do not check whether the memory accesses
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:137:<tbody><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">b1w</span><span class="ttl">b1w  Wire selftest_absorb into the battery, run the census, name the 3 survivors</span><span class="tip">b1w  Wire selftest_absorb into the battery, run the census, name the 3 survivors — commits: 81506bd 4dc1ff8 — HOLD: all four phases done; PASS-WITH-GAPS — Statement of record, amended by the R2 census: the selftest is invoked by zero gates AND its helper returns body+tail so 27 of 29 rows compare the SUM - a mutant that only MOVES text between the halves is invisible. MEASURED against 22 mutants: the shipped test kills 17 and misses 5, every one of them non-equivalent with an executed witness. The strengthened design (nine added rows pinning the two halves separately, plus a hold-contract invariant checked on all 1216 calls) kills 22 of 22. Denominator note: the historical 13-of-18 figure belongs to GATE rows, not to this file. R2 RESEARCH CLOSURE: both designs adopted - the invariants catch the CLASS, the section-K rows catch the edges the invariants cannot (the hold bound still satisfies the length test; the regex-literal mutant never touches the tail). The contested conversion was settled by execution: 2,575 parser steps, zero violations of either invariant on the shipped parser. IMPLEMENTED (81506bd): 52 rows; census committed as absorb_census.py; 22/22 killed.</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="all four phases done; PASS-WITH-GAPS">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — It set the three completion criteria and found the 3-survivor gap that no other seat saw. — evidence: _scratchpad/adjudication_r420_fable.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — REAL - RAN the selftest: 29 rows, 0 failures, PASS (29 is a RUNTIME count; 22 static check( sites, the rest are loops - a static count will look like a discrepancy and is not one). NAMES A SURVIVOR: dropping the newline clause at drive_guest.py:186 passes all 29 rows; proven non-equivalent by execution. Cause: feed() returns body+tail and 27 rows compare the SUM, so a mutant that only MOVES text between them is invisible. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_batchA_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — the passing 29-row shipped-function selftest is absent from every battery gate — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — selftest_absorb.py exists in regress/ (159 lines, 29 test rows) but is called by zero regress/*.sh gate scripts. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — `selftest_absorb.py` exists and is invoked by no gate — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/grok_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — selftest_absorb.py (29 rows, shipped split_stream) is referenced by zero gates; run.sh&#x27;s 16-gate list omits it — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/kimi_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — REAL — unwired selftest is shelf-ware; &quot;13→3&quot; is honest enough without naming survivors — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/glm_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — UNKNOWN — UNKNOWN — cannot verify zero hits; if true, it is a test-harness gap, not a runtime bug. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/deepseek_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — REAL — a test that never runs in any gate is dead coverage; fix is one wiring line. (packet-only resend after the mixed-brief stall; P3 resolved) — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/assess/minimax_resend_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — Reframes the fix as TWO INVARIANTS rather than rows: the hold shape after every step, and finality (a newline-terminated input must leave the tail empty) - which lets every sum row convert to a pair, so THE BLIND IDIOM STOPS EXISTING. Its positive control is THE NAMED SURVIVOR MUTANT applied to copies, which proves the row KILLS the mutant where an in-process wrong-expectation row only proves the reporter can print FAIL. And it wrote THE DENOMINATOR RULE: attempted=killed+survived+faults, self-checked, every survivor named with a closed-vocabulary disposition, a crash is a FAULT never a detection, and no ratio may use a denominator other than attempted. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/fable5_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — CENSUS BUILT AND RUN, not designed on paper: 22 mutants across seven axes, applied to COPIES with each substitution asserted to apply exactly once (else scored MALFORMED, never a survivor). SHIPPED TEST: 17 killed, 5 SURVIVED, 0 malformed - and all five are NON-EQUIVALENT with executed witnesses, so none can be retired. Beyond the named one: dropping the i != -1 guard holds the last character for ever; &lt; -&gt; &lt;= at the hold bound (the existing row uses 400, far past the edge); rest.rfind -&gt; tail.rfind leaks a second record unstripped; dropping the &#x27; instrs&#x27; literal invents a count from &#x27;[ 100 frobs; p]&#x27;. STRENGTHENED DESIGN MEASURED: 38 rows, 22 of 22 killed, 88 ms. CORRECTS THE BRIEF: the historical 13-of-18 figure is about GATE rows, not this file - a different denominator that the closure and my brief both conflated. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/opus_census_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — Make gate 2 enforce a PARTITION-SENSITIVE, CANARY-BACKED 18-mutant parser selftest - it frames the whole round as one property (the partition between body and tail) rather than a list of rows. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — Pin body and tail separately on ALL newline-terminated and multi-chunk rows, not only the held-line ones. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — Pin body and tail separately on every hold/release DECISION; wire into gate 2 with a copy-applied drop-the-newline-clause mutant as the positive control. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/grok_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — Pin body and tail separately on every hold/release row; wire into gate_offline.sh with an IN-FILE mutant positive control; re-run the census from per-mutant scratch copies. Names its rejections: gate_hygiene (a static log grader - execution breaks its idiom), asserting body alone (a duplicate-to-both-halves mutant passes), converting all 27 sum rows (churn, zero extra kills), touching drive_guest.py (shipped behaviour is correct), folding into selftest_mutation.sh (couples two subjects to one gate token). AND THE RULE THAT MATTERS: the census must report 18 NAMED mutants, never &#x27;N of N killed&#x27; - no denominator reframing. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/kimi_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — packet: pin body and tail independently for held-line inputs; wire into gate_offline.sh with an in-process positive control. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/glm_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — packet: add separate body/tail assertions for the newline-after-bracket held-line cases; wire into gate_offline.sh with a positive control. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/deepseek_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — packet: strengthen selftest_absorb.py with body/tail-pinning rows; wire into gate_offline.sh with a positive-control substep. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/research/minimax_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c hold"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">⏸</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — Implemented and committed as 81506bd. MEASURED: the selftest is 52 rows / 0 failures on the shipped parser, and the committed census now reports 22 of 22 killed, 0 survived, 0 faults, with its attempted=killed+survived+faults identity self-checked. Gate 2 PASS at 131 checks (was 122). THE GATE CAUGHT ONE OF MY OWN ROWS: the control asserted the kill came from exactly one named row, but the mutant strands every bracket-carrying line and fails three - now a floor, with the reason recorded. Awaiting the panel&#x27;s review of the diff. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_closure/r2_research_closure_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — the author did this review (shown, not hidden) — GO-WITH-CHANGES (author-adjacent, badge A). MEASURED the destructive rmtree by losing a planted file; measured that a crash is scored FAULT and a planted FAIL line is scored SURVIVED (so the grading is sound); found the 255/256 off-by-one, the I2 row&#x27;s position being load-bearing and unpinned, a wrong-reason kill (HOLD_MAX=106 dies by a row about the constant&#x27;s value), the control&#x27;s logs being deleted exactly when it fails, and REC_EOF as an untested axis. Isolation verified clean. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/opus_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c bad"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✕</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — NO-GO - the census can delete a caller-supplied workdir. RIGHT, and reached independently of the measure seat. Fixed in 4dc1ff8. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — GO - invariants, edges and controls sound and non-vacuous. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — GO-WITH-CHANGES - independently found the leftover sum rows AND the rmtree hazard. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/grok_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — GO-WITH-CHANGES - verified the core by EXECUTION and then found the round&#x27;s own overstatements: the sum idiom is NOT gone (section B keeps it), the I2 prose says ~1200 calls where the row prints 685, the exact-3 presence row contradicts the round&#x27;s own floor rationale, and the census identity guard is structurally unreachable today. All corrected. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/kimi_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c bad"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✕</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — NO-GO - claimed the gate wants 3 body-landing rows while the selftest emits 2, so the PASS &#x27;cannot be true&#x27;. MEASURED WRONG: the file emits 3. It counted from a partial hunk - the honest limit of a seat that cannot open the file. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/glm_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c bad"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✕</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — NO-GO - same miscount as glm, same cause. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/deepseek_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c bad"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✕</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — NO-GO - same miscount. Three packet seats reached the same wrong conclusion from the same partial hunk, which is worth recording: packet seats fail TOGETHER when the packet is the thing at fault. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/review/minimax_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — PASS-WITH-GAPS. Ran the evidence rather than reading it: gate 2 PASS 131 checks twice, selftest 52 rows, invariant 685 steps, census 22/22 - no number differed from any claim. RE-KILLED BOTH R1 HALVES BY HAND (export deleted -&gt; no/no FAIL; driver re-hardcoded -&gt; no/no FAIL). Probed the census&#x27;s honesty: an unwritable workdir crashes visibly rather than degrading, and the identity check was negative-controlled to FAIL on an inflated count. Found independently, before my fix landed: the rmtree hazard, the identity tautology, the exact-count row and the false &#x27;sum idiom gone&#x27; claim. TWO GAPS REMAIN (now filed as rows): deleting gate 2&#x27;s whole absorb block is INVISIBLE because its guards live inside it, and NO CROSS-CONSUMER CONTROL EXISTS for the driver/grader seam. Also: an interrupted census leaves its workdir (cleanup not in a finally), and BOOT_WALL=0.0 from an instant-exit rig reds containment - a false red, only on an already-failing rig. Unverified until the 08-22 battery: gate 5&#x27;s new rows on real rigs, real BOOT_WALL magnitudes, gate 6 grading logs from the changed driver. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b1w/regress/fable5_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">b115</span><span class="ttl">#115  Read path: an unaligned overlay read takes bytes from the wrong layer; a short flat-CD read loses data</span><span class="tip">#115  Read path: an unaligned overlay read takes bytes from the wrong layer; a short flat-CD read loses data — commits: 00d546d — HOLD: R6 - its own round: the deepest data item — Statement of record: the overlay read loop neither clamps the read to the block remainder nor advances the buffer by the amount read, so an unaligned read takes bytes from the wrong layer AND duplicates them - measured, 1022 of 1024 bytes wrong - while returning the FULL length, so every caller check is bypassed. Overlays are the normal rig case. The CD half reports all-zeros as GOOD. CONFIRMED. Constraint: keep #416&#x27;s advertised-gap contract. — witness: detector:regress/diff_diskimage_io.c -- OWNER-ADOPTED TERM, 2026-08-27.  The fix shipped with a MEASURED detector and a kill table, and no guest-path witness.  `detector:` IS NOT A RUNG: it ranks below every access witness and does NOT discharge the rung-3 ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires.  Before this term the row had to be written `none`, which asserts DOCUMENTED-ONLY and was false about a round that shipped a fix.  *** THIS FIELD CITED `regress/diff_fb_clear.c`, WHICH DOES NOT EXIST AND NEVER DID. Corrected 2026-08-27 by a sibling grep. ***  The real detector is `regress/diff_diskimage_io.c` (its section is `section_overlay_unaligned`, tagged b115 in the file).  Found while correcting `gdromexitsix`, whose `probe:` field cited two scratchpad scripts that are also gone -- the adjudicating seat pointed out that the grep-for-siblings rule had been skipped, and doing it turned up these two.  **The `detector:` term was owner-adopted on 2026-08-27, used on three rows that day, and TWO of the three cited a file that does not exist** -- a plausible-looking filename written from intent rather than from the tree. — note: *** TWO CORRECTIONS OF MINE, BOTH SERIOUS, FROM THE OPUS PASS-2 SEAT. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:321:sentence sat in the probe comment AND here, and the measurement refuted both at once.*</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="R6 - its own round: the deepest data item">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL - both halves, plus an UNREPORTED byte-duplication defect on unaligned multi-block reads in fread_helper. The CD half zero-fills a short read and reports GOOD; a fix must keep #416&#x27;s advertised-gap contract. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/fable5_assess_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — REAL and WORSE than filed - MEASURED with a transcribed probe: off=511 len=1024 gives 1022 of 1024 bytes from the wrong layer, and the function RETURNS THE FULL LENGTH, so the caller&#x27;s zero-fill and short-read checks never fire. Rigs open R:, so an overlay is the NORMAL case; -d o100:img makes every read unaligned. The CD half reports all-zeros as GOOD. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_batchB_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] 112 KB. Answered but its verdict line is the echoed brief; body read for content.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] GO-WITH-CHANGES. Verified all three cited claims, then traced offset=511/len=1024 iteration by iteration and supplied the ALGEBRA nobody else did: curofs + remain IS (curofs | (BLOCK-1)) + 1, so the clamp is provably consistent with the loop&#x27;s own advance. Proved the aligned path byte-for-byte unchanged. NAMED THE ESCAPING MUTANT: clamp without the stride fix.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] 11.5 KB, substantive.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] TRANSPORT kimi-k3:cloud (SUBSTITUTE covering the walled CLI seat. 6.9 KB, substantive.)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] Named the same escaping mutant as agy, independently: &#x27;one critical mutant survives the detector&#x27;. That INDEPENDENT AGREEMENT is why the sixth row exists.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] Answered; walked the patched loop.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_024431] &#x27;correct, complete, and minimal&#x27; with a per-iteration table.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] ADJUDICATOR. VERDICT: HOLD -- roster incomplete, and the rule carries it rather than the kill table. Found FIVE record defects and ONE REAL BUILD-TREE DEFECT (the WSL arc tree was never propagated to, 2386 lines / 0 markers -- the no-VPATH hazard). Also: my filing justification was CIRCULAR, the witness field&#x27;s probe: prefix would have been scored rung-3 by the shape regex, and the short-read family had to be split out before b115 closes. Raised the witness-ladder eligibility question for the owner.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] FIRED and PENDING at close of this record -- adversarial pass-2 seat, launched via the Agent tool (leaves NO FILE in the panel dir, so any check counting files under-counts by two). Recorded as fired-not-answered so its absence is never read as agreement.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] 178 KB, still reading files at close of round; body not fully harvested. NOT graded as agreement -- recorded as answered-but-unharvested so it is not read as assent.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] VERDICT: GO. Re-verified the fwrite/fread stride targeting from source.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] 7.9 KB, substantive.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] TRANSPORT kimi-k3:cloud (SUBSTITUTE. THE SHARPEST ANSWER OF THE PASS: the signedness safety is CONDITIONAL on the same range invariant that proves termination (remain converts to unsigned in the comparison); identified buf += remain as a provably EQUIVALENT edit rather than debt; and found a THIRD escape class -- a scan-start optimisation that passes because the fixture pins ONE geometry.)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] Sound; PROVED no infinite-spin: inblock in [0,BLOCK-1] so remain in [1,BLOCK], so len strictly decreases. That was the one hazard that could have made the change worse than the defect.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] RATIFY. FOUND THE SECOND ESCAPE: buf += lentoread -&gt; buf += lenread, an identity outside a short read, so it passes every row. Filed as coverage debt.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g"></span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [_scratchpad/panel_20260827_025902] Answered.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">b126</span><span class="ttl">#126  A small ISO becomes a FLOPPY CD-ROM; d: does not apply to .cue</span><span class="tip">#126  A small ISO becomes a FLOPPY CD-ROM; d: does not apply to .cue — HOLD: R7 - disk parse round — Statement of record: a CD image of floppy size is typed FLOPPY because the size heuristic has no is_a_cdrom term and the type is still UNKNOWN when it runs; the result is invisible to every SCSI controller. The .cue path also lacks the prefix_d guard. CONFIRMED. NAMED MEASUREMENT for the round: suppressing FLOPPY-typing lands the image on the round-up geometry arm that #418 documents as harmful - measure it, do not assume. — witness: none yet -- half 1 is a host-side attach-path mis-typing with a guest-visible consequence (the CD-ROM is absent from the bus); a witness needs a build. Half 2 is latent. — note: *** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, and THE ROW COVERS TWO DEFECTS OF DIFFERENT SEVERITY -- which is the shape that buries one when the other closes. Split before working it. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:335:</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="R7 - disk parse round">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL - :232-236 has no is_a_cdrom term; the cue block (:1890-1904) has no prefix_d guard. Fix couples to TWO #418 artifacts: the record row parse.c:231 and the row-count guard :241. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/fable5_assess_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL - full chain confirmed, including the crux: type is still UNKNOWN at :1912 because the only earlier default sits inside the prefix_V block that returns at :1818. WARNING for the fix: suppressing FLOPPY-typing lands on the non-floppy round-up geometry arm that #418 documents as harmful, and #418 deferred THIS case at :1946-1948. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_batchA_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — small CDs are floppy-typed and d: still does not suppress CUE recognition — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — diskimage_recalc_size sets FLOPPY for 1.44M/1.2M without checking is_a_cdrom, and looks_like_cue ignores prefix_d. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — the size heuristic ignores `is_a_cdrom`; the `.cue` half is an asymmetry that should not be “fixed” into a disk — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/grok_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — heuristic diskimage.c:232-236 ignores is_a_cdrom; !prefix_d gates only .iso/.cdr (:1852), not .cue (:1890-1903) — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/kimi_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — REAL — measured on shipped binary; the one-liner guard is likely safe but unverified — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/glm_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — UNKNOWN — UNKNOWN — measured claim not independently verified; one-liner risk unassessed. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/deepseek_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — REAL — a size heuristic that overrides an explicit CD flag is a precedence bug; the third suffix is a coverage hole. (packet-only resend after the mixed-brief stall; P3 resolved) — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b126/assess/minimax_resend_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">b118</span><span class="ttl">b118o  Disk offset prefix: a 64-bit value is parsed by a 32-bit function</span><span class="tip">b118o  Disk offset prefix: a 64-bit value is parsed by a 32-bit function — HOLD: R7 - the o: half; the leak half is filed as b118L — Statement of record: the o: prefix parses a 64-bit offset through 32-bit atoi. Measured: the &lt;0 guard is FALSE COMFORT - offsets at or above 4 GiB near a multiple of 2^32 are silently accepted as 0 or 512 and the disk is read from the wrong place with no message, which is worse than a rejection. CONFIRMED. SPLIT: the failed-add leak is now b118L. — witness: none -- host-side only. Reachable from the `-d` argument and the config file, never by a guest, so no rung-3/4 witness can exist. Pinning the exact truncated value needs a build. — note: *** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: TRUE, host-side only -- AND IT IS HALF OF A FAMILY, which neither seat could see from its own row. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:355:</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="R7 - the trailing-garbage half only">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — PARTLY - trailing-garbage acceptance is real (:1693-1703); the no-detector half is ALREADY ADJUDICATED as an equivalent mutant (diff_diskimage_geom.c:69-76) - retire that half, do not work it. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/fable5_assess_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — PARTLY-FIXED - CORRECTS THE RECORD: the no-detector half is dispositioned at geom.c:69-77 as unreachable-in-tree defence-in-depth in the vacuity taxonomy, NOT as an equivalent mutant (that list is a separate paragraph at :63-67, and the guard is deliberately not in it). Half 1 measured REAL: both strtoll calls pass NULL endptr, so digits+garbage is accepted. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_batchA_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — geometry accepts trailing junk and the divisor guard has no named detector — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b122/assess/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — g prefix parser uses strtoll with NULL endptr accepting trailing non-digits, and bytespercyl &lt; 1 has no active detector. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/b122/assess/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — `g` still drops the `strtoll` end pointer; the `bytespercyl` guard is shipped with no detecto