<td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">seam</span><span class="ttl">seam  Nothing goes red if the driver and the log grader disagree about the path</span><span class="tip">seam  Nothing goes red if the driver and the log grader disagree about the path — HOLD: FILE: the R1 fix removed the cause, not the detector — Found by the flagship in the R1+R2 regression review, and it is the gap the R1 round itself named but did not close. Gate 5 grades the driver&#x27;s STDOUT only and never asserts that drive_&lt;rig&gt;.log landed anywhere; gate 6 answers a missing luna88k or landisk log with note + continue rather than degrade, so the coverage loss is SILENT (the pmax/arc half at least skips visibly). R1&#x27;s offline selftest covers the DRIVER side only, so a grader-side or invocation-side divergence still reds nothing. The fix removed the cause; this row is about the missing detector.</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="FILE: the R1 fix removed the cause, not the detector">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — REAL - no row anywhere goes red on a writer/grader path divergence. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/fable5_regress_r1r2_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: KEEP OPEN, NOT documented-only. The hold is exactly right at HEAD -- #422 removed the CAUSE and the DETECTOR is still missing, ASYMMETRICALLY. *** CAUSE GONE, verified both halves: lib.sh exports LOGDIR, drive_guest.py reads it with the same empty-is-unset semantics, and selftest_logdir.py points LOGDIR elsewhere via a BARE assignment in a child shell -- so it reds if lib.sh loses its export -- while asserting both filenames, pinning the spelling on the PRODUCER side. **THE SEAM IS ONE-SIDED AND THAT IS THE FINDING:** `drive_&lt;rig&gt;.log` is spelled twice independently -- drive_guest.py:250 (PINNED by the selftest) and gate_hygiene.sh:444 (pinned by NOTHING) -- and the grader&#x27;s response to a miss is `note` + `continue`. `note()` increments nothing and returns 0, **so two rigs&#x27; distress grading can silently not happen while gate 6 reports PASS.** Sibling parity fails against the pmax/arc half twelve lines up, where a missing log is `gate_skip` plus a size floor plus a uid=0 boot proof. **The newer, less-exercised rigs get the weaker treatment, which the block&#x27;s own comment says is backwards.** Third leg confirmed: gate 5 never asserts the log landed -- the string `drive_` does not appear in gate_crossfamily.sh at all. THE ROW IT PROPOSES, and it argued the placement rather than asserting it: put the check in gate 5 (the PRODUCER), because $LOGDIR PERSISTS BETWEEN RUNS -- gate_offline.sh names that hazard explicitly -- so the natural gate-6 discriminator (&#x27;rig_$rig.out present but drive_$rig.log absent&#x27;) would report a seam that is not there, or mask one that is. **Gate 5&#x27;s row has no staleness question at all.** Separately and on its own terms: upgrade gate_hygiene.sh:445 from `note` to `degrade`, which is the sibling-parity fix rather than the seam fix. NEGATIVE CONTROL, and it is the only form that tests the two spellings AGAINST EACH OTHER rather than each against a literal: extend selftest_logdir.py to read the grader&#x27;s pattern OUT OF gate_hygiene.sh by grep rather than re-typing it, then mutate the driver&#x27;s log_path and require RED. Honest limit stated: neither row catches an edit changing both spellings in one commit -- inherent to a seam, and it belongs in the row&#x27;s own comment. — evidence: Agent(model=opus) held-stage seat, batch A (harness rows). Read-only; GXEMUL-SEC only.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — MANUAL — WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.
