tools\pipeline\matrix.html:724:row&#x27;s own entries before starting new work on it.* — evidence: codex entry 2026-08-26 on this row; every manual citation re-verified by the main loop against _scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt, page numbers derived from the footers</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — *** TRUE, AND ITS &#x27;ZERO SEAT ENTRIES&#x27; IS A LEDGER ARTIFACT, NOT AN UNASSESSED ITEM. *** OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:2698+ ALREADY HOLDS THE FULL ANALYSIS -- verified independently by the main loop, which found the existing text naming the same mechanism. Re-verified the mechanism anyway: cpu_arm_instr.c:1550 fast path does p32[(addr &amp; 0xfff) &gt;&gt; 2] (the &gt;&gt;2 truncates addr[1:0], which IS the architectural answer) while :1563 slow path hands raw addr to memory_rw -- same instruction, two answers, selected by PAGE RESIDENCY. Thumb PUSH/POP inherits it (cpu_arm.c:1670,1677). AND IT RE-CHECKED THE ARM CITATIONS AGAINST THE REAL LOCAL MANUAL, all three holding: LDM(1) p.A4-37, STM(1) p.A4-190, Table A2-10 &#x27;Operation unaffected by Addr[1:0]&#x27; p.A2-40 -- its own first grep reported them unverifiable and that was ITS error, caught by re-grepping. Relabel: ANALYSED AND READY, not unassessed.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: `armbdt` IS REAL -- and it REFUTED MY BRIEF&#x27;S CITATION PREMISE, which I had planted from the carrier. VERIFIED AGAINST THE LOCAL MANUAL LINE BY LINE BEFORE RECORDING. *** My brief said the controlling text is LDR&#x27;s own Alignment note at p. A4-44. **That governs LDR&#x27;s rotate-the-aligned-word rule and does NOT govern block transfers.** The controlling text is LDM(1) p. A4-37 and STM(1) p. A4-190. Read from `_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt:7954`: *&#x27;For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0, the Load Multiple instructions ignore the least significant two bits of the address... For CP15_reg1_Ubit == 1, all non-word aligned accesses cause an alignment fault.&#x27;* STM says the same at `:14115`. **This is a citation read from the local manual, which is the standard the carrier demands after #357/#362/#365 shipped ARM page numbers that came from panel output.** And the seat&#x27;s own argument for why LDR cannot govern is the good one: if A4-44 applied, the fast path -- which aligns WITHOUT rotating -- would not be correct either. THE FINDING: with `U=0` and alignment checking off, the masked fast/warm path is RIGHT and the raw slow/cold path is WRONG (`memory_rw` uses the raw byte offset and copies four bytes from there, so a load can span two architectural words and an STM can overwrite part of the next). The generated optimised path confirms the intended shape independently: select the page from the raw address, mask the access offset with `addr &amp;= 0xffc`, update the base register from the UNMASKED value. *** THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS AND IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS: mask ONLY the addresses passed to the two slow `memory_rw` calls. Masking `addr` at function entry would CORRUPT WRITEBACK, because Addressing Mode 4 computes writeback from the raw `Rn` and the code writes its running raw `addr` back. *** AND IT QUALIFIED THE RECORD RATHER THAN RATIFYING IT: `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` is initialised ENABLED but never enforced by any memory access, so the record must NOT say unqualifiedly that the fast path is &#x27;the architectural answer&#x27; -- it is the answer for the emulator&#x27;s implemented A=0/U=0 behaviour, and at the advertised A=1 state **both paths are wrong** because an alignment exception is owed. RANKING: above `b120r`, and it noted `fable_queue.md` ALREADY ranks it first among the five triage rows -- so my brief&#x27;s framing that it sat behind boot-witnessed items was also wrong. It declined to generalise: this does not put it above every boot-witnessed durability-loss or host-death defect, which need individual harm comparisons. HONEST LIMIT RECORDED: it could not certify the rung-3 probe in a read-only session -- the ledger says that probe is private and uncommitted and no artifact exists under `regress/`. So its endorsement of the WITNESS is READ-from-record, while the manual-and-source finding stands on its own. — evidence: codex 5.6-SOL xhigh, fired against the row&#x27;s own `hold_question` (_scratchpad/holdq_briefs/holdq_armbdt.md; answer at scratchpad/holdq_out/armbdt.txt). NOTE: codex streams reasoning to STDERR here -- the .err files run 1-3 MB while the answer file is 2-5 KB, so a size check on the wrong stream would score these as stalls.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — Ranked 1st of five.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — RE-FIRE ANSWERED (6,523 b; the first attempt died at 927 b mid-analysis). Ranked it 1st of five, and the cheapest to witness.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — Ranked highly; cheapest to witness of the five.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — PACKET. Ranked 3rd.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — PACKET. Ranked 1st.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — PACKET. Ranked 3rd.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-19 — *** REPRODUCED, AND THE FIX IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. *** Probe built on the testarm/SA1110 cold debugger with a four-byte raw stub; every encoding re-verified through the emulator&#x27;s own unassemble before any row was believed. Three binaries in a private tree:
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:815:Corrected here, and the SOFT branch is now HARD for this shape, because the premise holds: `commits` is not closure-only. TWELVE OPEN ROWS CARRY IT (ovsync, carrier, n2, n4, b1w, a1a2 and others), so an open row with a CODE commit naming it and an empty field has no legitimate reading. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_003556/ (opus5 ninth-seat review)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mrwstore2</span><span class="ttl">mrwstore2  A straddling store whose tail faults makes an MMIO head see the write twice</span><span class="tip">mrwstore2  A straddling store whose tail faults makes an MMIO head see the write twice — commits: 0447c2e — HOLD: *** THE OLD BLOCKING REASON IS REFUTED AND WAS AN L4 -- corrected 2026-08-26. *** It read &#x27;the fix may itself DIVERGE from real hardware&#x27;. **A fix cannot diverge, because the behaviour is PERMITTED and never REQUIRED.** Four PowerPC manuals are now local in `_scratchpad/` (OEA Book III v2.01, VEA Book II v2.01, MPCFPE32B Rev 2 -- the 32-bit PEM covering the PPC750 this row&#x27;s witness uses -- and the 2005 32/64-bit PEM), fetched and extracted with the same pdftotext used for ddi0100i.txt. PEM 3.2.2 / Book II 2.1: a misaligned store is not atomic, so it falls in the &#x27;any other load or store instruction MAY be partially executed and then aborted... and then re-executed&#x27; bucket; 6.1.4 says &#x27;some bytes just before the boundary may have been updated&#x27;. **So an emulator that never partially executes is conformant under every one of them -- the INTERSECTION of both readings, not a divergence from either.** *** AND THE MANUALS DISAGREE ON THE CASE THIS ROW IS ACTUALLY ABOUT, recorded as an honest UNKNOWN rather than settled by the stronger document: *** Book III v2.01 4.2.4.1 says that for an access whose first byte is Caching-Inhibited AND Guarded -- exactly the MMIO-head shape here -- a DSI means &#x27;the portion of the storage operand that is in Caching Inhibited and Guarded storage is NOT accessed&#x27;, i.e. the device write must not happen. The PEM contains no such rule and its 6.1.4 explicitly permits the head bytes to have been updated. Whether a real PPC750 suppresses the device write is NOT established. THE REASONS THAT SURVIVE and on which this row stays filed: REACHABILITY (zero straddling stores measured across three real boots) and SEVERITY (a duplicated device write -- guest-visible, no host kill, no memory-safety consequence; both PowerPC and ARM put the burden on software). *** SCOPE CORRECTION THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE BLOCKER: &#x27;memory_rw is the choke point&#x27; is FALSE FOR THE CLASS. *** stmw/stswi/lmw/lswi and ARM&#x27;s bdt_store each loop and return on the first failing element, so they have the identical partial-completion-across-a-fault property at the INSTRUCTION level, entirely outside memory_rw -- and PEM 6.1.4&#x27;s first bullet is about exactly those. **A memory_rw fix would be a partial fix that reads as a complete one**, which is the argument for filing this properly rather than shipping a round a future reader mistakes for closure. TWO SIDE-EFFECT CAUTIONS FROM A PRIOR SEAT MEASURED ABSENT IN THIS TREE: FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS returns before every SPR write and before ppc_exception(), and there is NO R/C writeback on PPC at all -- so the recorded warning about PPC603 software reload describes hazards that do not exist here. FILED SEPARATELY, all from the same reading: `X(stvx)` does a raw 16-byte memory_rw with no `EA &amp; ~0xF` mask (a second straddling producer, and a divergence in its own right); diff_memory_rw.c has a coverage hole exactly at this row&#x27;s shape (device head + FAULTING tail); and memory_ppc.c:302-305 is an unlatched guest-drivable fatal(), one per translation fault -- the floodclass shape #464 just closed six of. — witness: none yet -- guest-reachable on PowerPC and witnessable at rung 3/4 (the observable is a side-effect COUNT, not a value), but a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids. — note: *** L4 CORRECTION 2026-08-27: MY OBSERVABLE IS WRONG AND MY RANKING WAS TOO HIGH. The mature ledger entry already measured three of this row&#x27;s claims FALSE, and I did not read it before writing a fresh note on top. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:879:mrwstore2_rung3.py is close to shippable as a regress detector; its controls already satisfy the liveness and device-signature clauses. — evidence: _scratchpad/ (mrwstore2_rung3.py, sig.py, probe_mrwstore2.c)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — *** THE RUNG-3 PROBE LEFT NO ARTEFACT, WHICH IS THE census_exits LESSON REPEATING. ***  0447c2e touched only memory_rw.c (a comment) and ledger.json. A content search for the witness&#x27;s own address 1201fffe across the whole project finds NOTHING. A rung-3 witness that cannot be re-run is one session away from being a remembered grep -- and [[pcchalt]] is already paying for it, since its cost estimate could be cut by reusing scaffolding that no longer exists.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:912:Corrected here, and the SOFT branch is now HARD for this shape, because the premise holds: `commits` is not closure-only. TWELVE OPEN ROWS CARRY IT (ovsync, carrier, n2, n4, b1w, a1a2 and others), so an open row with a CODE commit naming it and an empty field has no legitimate reading. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_003556/ (opus5 ninth-seat review)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">fbmode</span><span class="ttl">fbmode  footbridge ignores FREERUN vs PERIODIC, and three more datasheet-backed defects</span><span class="tip">fbmode  footbridge ignores FREERUN vs PERIODIC, and three more datasheet-backed defects — HOLD: Scope widened 2026-08-16 by the R6 pass-2 codex seat (read late): beyond the four listed defects it should cover external-event handling, reserved CONTROL-bit masking, and the retained/down-counter model. Still after the live-rig items -- nothing boots a footbridge, so this is correctness, not stability.</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="Scope widened 2026-08-16 by the R6 pass-2 codex seat (read late): beyond the four listed defects it should cover external-event handling, reserved CONTROL-bit masking, and the retained/down-counter model. Still after the live-rig items -- nothing boots a footbridge, so this is correctness, not stability.">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c hold"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">⏸</span><span class="tip">2026-08-16 — CONFIRMED — [RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY] This stage ran with 2 of 9 seats because the carrier&#x27;s rule at the time was ONE full panel per ROUND, with pass 2 restricted to codex and Opus -- written when Grok&#x27;s free tier was dead and Kimi was quota-dead. The owner replaced that on 2026-08-16 with FULL PANEL ON EVERY STAGE, stop-and-ask on any seat failure. Grandfathered FORWARD-ONLY by owner decision: not re-panelled, but marked so the dashboard does not read these blanks as seat failures. Missing: fable5, agy, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax. — evidence: policy annotation, not a review</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — UNBLOCKED: the 21285 datasheet was obtained (_scratchpad/dc21285.pdf, Intel 278115-001). Four defects are now specifiable. (1) SS7.3.41 bit 6: FREERUN wraps to FFFFFFh and keeps counting; PERIODIC reloads TimerNLoad; the clock source is bits 3:2 alone, identical in both. reload_timer_value() computes one rate and hands it to a repeating timer -- the PERIODIC cadence unconditionally -- so a FREERUN timer is too fast by up to 2^24/load. (2) TimerNValue is READ-ONLY (SS7.3.38/7.3.40) but the model accepts a guest write, and TIMER_1_CLEAR has NO writeflag test, so a guest READ of a write-only register reloads the counter and deasserts the IRQ. (3) Reset values diverge: hardware gives CONTROL 0x00 and LOAD 0, the model gives TIMER_MODE_PERIODIC and TIMER_MAX_VAL -- and this is the model bug #432&#x27;s first draft mistakenly cited as evidence for its own zero mapping. (4) The random() counter model is now fully specified by SS7.3.40 + SS7.3.41 bit 7: a 24-bit down-counter, readable any time, RETAINED across disable and resuming on re-enable. The author&#x27;s TODO is discharged; only a rig is missing. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260815_221232/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-16 — MANUAL — Expand the item: external-event handling, reserved CONTROL-bit masking, and the retained/down-counter model belong in it alongside the four already listed. Ordering it after i8253zero would also be defensible, since i8253zero has a direct datasheet answer. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260815_230954/codex.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">i8253zero</span><span class="ttl">i8253zero  A programmed count of 0 means 65536, not &#x27;disabled&#x27; -- now datasheet-backed</span><span class="tip">i8253zero  A programmed count of 0 means 65536, not &#x27;disabled&#x27; -- now datasheet-backed — HOLD: settled by document; one-line fix in the hz computation — CLOSE (assess), and PROMOTE as a small round</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="settled by document; one-line fix in the hz computation">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — CLOSE (assess), and PROMOTE as a small round. Confirmed in source: dev_8253.c:181-186 maps count 0 to hz=0 (&#x27;disabled&#x27;) where the datasheet -- a real local file -- says 65536. One-line fix; rung-3 witness constructible with the existing PIT probe pattern, and rung 3 is the ceiling here because no in-tree guest writes 0. Near-zero cost, PIT context still warm.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — SETTLED FROM A REAL DOCUMENT, which is the whole point of the item. i8253reg.h says nothing about a zero count (read in full, 102 lines), so the belief rested on memory -- exactly the trap this project names. The Intel 8254 datasheet (_scratchpad/i8254.pdf, 231164-005) states it twice on p.17: &#x27;The largest possible initial count is 0; this is equivalent to 2^16 for binary counting and 10^4 for BCD counting.&#x27; So dev_8253.c:125&#x27;s `else hz = 0` -- which the timer core then floor-clamps to 1e-8 Hz, i.e. never fires -- is wrong against the hardware convention. Cite it as the 8254, NOT the 8253: the count-0 convention was verified in the 8254 document only. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260815_221232/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — MANUAL — WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:916:CAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel&#x27;s own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a &#x27;+1&#x27; mutant that &#x27;admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green&#x27; -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today&#x27;s 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: &#x27;do tfreq first&#x27; (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 &#x27;CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store&#x27;), &#x27;mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness&#x27; (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and &#x27;drop constblind&#x27; (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/codex.txt.rerun</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — agy answered this panel. THIRD by the panel&#x27;s most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row&#x27;s main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — grok answered this panel. THIRD by the panel&#x27;s most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row&#x27;s main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — kimi answered this panel. THIRD by the panel&#x27;s most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row&#x27;s main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — 8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:929:FOR THIS ROW: THIRD by the panel&#x27;s most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row&#x27;s main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — deepseek answered this panel. THIRD by the panel&#x27;s most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row&#x27;s main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — minimax answered this panel. THIRD by the panel&#x27;s most reasoned sequence, not first -- and the reason is a dependency worth recording: pitflip MANUFACTURES count 0 from a guest that never asks for it, so closing pitflip removes this row&#x27;s main reachability route. agy alone would do it first, as a standalone round. It keeps its rung-3 witness either way. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — *** REPRODUCED AT RUNG 3, AND MY OWN PREMISE FOR THE TASK WAS WRONG. ***  I briefed the measure seat that NO rig reaches the 8253 -- only algor/cobalt/evbmips/iyonix via bus_isa -- and that a rung-4 witness therefore almost certainly did not exist. HALF FALSE, and the false half is what made rung 3 reachable. `bus_isa_init` has SIX callers outside src/machines/: bus_pci.c:476 and :1060, dev_eagle.c:160,172, dev_lca.c:376, dev_prep.c:80. My grep looked only at src/machines/ and bus_*.c and I reported the subset as the whole. **cats** reaches the 8253 through machine_cats.c -&gt; dev_footbridge.c:730 (bus_pci_add &quot;ali_m1543&quot;) -&gt; bus_pci.c:459/476, at isa_portbase 0x7c000000. Verified here independently.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:954:*** AND THIS ROW&#x27;S WITNESS IS NOT RE-RUNNABLE. ***  1ce189c (&#x27;i8253zero REPRODUCED at rung 3&#x27;) changed tools/pipeline/ledger.json ONLY -- no probe script -- and the row&#x27;s witness field is None. That rung-3 result exists as a transcript. Before the merged round ships, its D0-equivalent row must exist AS A FILE, or the fix lands with a witness nobody can reproduce: the fifth vacuity class in its hardest-to-see shape, because a transcript LOOKS like evidence.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:956:Harm if reached: emulated clock frozen -- hz=0 -&gt; timer_clamp_freq floors to TIMER_MIN_FREQUENCY = 1e-8 Hz, one tick per 3.17 years. — evidence: git show 1ce189c --stat; dev_8253.c I8253_TIMER_MSB arm</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">m8probe</span><span class="ttl">m8probe  m8820x PROBE_USER/PROBE_SUPER are dropped, so SSR answers a deterministic miss</span><span class="tip">m8probe  m8820x PROBE_USER/PROBE_SUPER are dropped, so SSR answers a deterministic miss — HOLD: *** RE-FILED LATENT 2026-08-26 ON THE `gdrompiooddlen` PRECEDENT -- REACHABILITY MEASURED NEGATIVE, WITH CONTROLS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. MUST NOT BE RANKED LIVE. *** A full OpenBSD 7.7/luna88k boot to `login:` (7,684,964,995 instructions) issues **seven distinct CMMU commands and ZERO probes**. Denominator stated: 0 of 7 distinct commands, not 0 of 0 -- and it independently replicates #433&#x27;s finding thirty rounds later. **The controls are what make this evidence:** a real guest supervisor store on a cold debugger, with the planted word DISASSEMBLED BEFORE STEPPING per the hand-assembled-encoding rule -- cmd 0x24 and 0x20 fire the detector, cmd 0x35 does not while still registering as M8CMD_FIRST. So the negative is real and not a broken grep. CORROBORATION that sharpens it past &#x27;this guest does not&#x27;: OpenBSD&#x27;s m88k CMMU driver issues CMMU_PROBE from exactly two places and **both are inside DDB display routines** -- a kernel-debugger feature, not an operating path, reachable only by a human at a DDB prompt. (Caveat recorded by the seat: that source is OpenBSD 2.2/mvme88k, the lineage, not the booting guest; the boot measurement is the evidence for the actual guest.) *** THE OLD COST NOTE WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE. *** &#x27;Needs the translation walk&#x27; implies a CAPABILITY gap. **The walk exists** -- m88k_translate_v2p() at memory_m88k.c:103 -- **and is already side-effect-free under FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS** (:344 guards the PG_U/PG_M writeback, :374 returns before any PFSR/PFAR write), its flags already cover the probe&#x27;s own selectors, and the SSR bit layout is in-tree. **And the driving probe SHAPE is already committed**: m8820x_sites_probe.py:159 ships a PROBE_SUPER row driven by one real guest instruction, ~3 s, no boot. *This is the `sh4dmacie` situation in reverse -- there the shape was missing and it was mis-read as a capability gap; here the shape was already there and nobody re-read the probe.* **WHAT IS ACTUALLY MISSING IS AN ORACLE, AND THE HOLD IS NOW CONDITIONAL ON IT:** translate_v2p exposes no BATC-vs-PATC-vs-walk distinction (needed for CMMU_SSR_BH) and none of the U/M/PROT/SO attributes SSR must carry; real probe semantics are supported only by DDB code reading those registers, which is evidence and not proof; and **there is no 88200/88204 manual in this project**. TRIGGER, on the SH4_VAL_NCLASS precedent: **if an 88200/88204 manual is obtained, this becomes a small round.** That converts an indefinite hold into a conditional one. *** AND THE CURRENT BEHAVIOUR IS THE BETTER ONE, which is the strongest reason not to rank it: *** a dropped probe leaves SSR zero with the V bit clear -- a deterministic, DEFINED &#x27;no valid translation&#x27;. Implementing without the manual would replace a defined conservative miss with an INVENTED answer that could steer the guest&#x27;s paging. **That is the same reasoning used to decline `sh4aaalias` before the SH manual arrived -- and that one turned out to be a wrong finding.** The only known consumer prints a line in a debugger display, so a cosmetically wrong DDB line for a human is the entire measured consequence. WORTH ADDING CHEAPLY: one row asserting SSR == 0 after a probe, to pin the DELIBERATE behaviour that today rests on prose in two comments and no oracle at all. — witness: none -- LATENT. The claim is true by reading, but the path is unreached by any operating guest OS, so no rung-3/4 witness can be built without first constructing a guest that probes. — note: *** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: the claim is TRUE and the path is COMPLETELY UNREACHED by operating guest OS paths. Latent. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:1114:</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="READ only -- needs its own reproduction before any edit.">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — CLOSE (assess). Disposition right: READ-only, reproduction first; init-order defects are witnessable at rung 2/3.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — READ, NOT MEASURED, and recorded at that grade deliberately. bus_isa.c:224-225 copies machine-&gt;isa_pic_data.pending_timer_interrupts at line 225, but dev_8253.c:248 -- the ONLY writer of that field -- runs from the device_add at line 244. machine.c&#x27;s memset(m, 0, ...) means the field is always NULL at the copy, so the timer-interrupt ack at bus_isa.c:134-138 can never run. LATENT TODAY only because nothing ever deasserts isa.0. The same shape sits at dev_v3.c:298. An ordering bug of this kind is exactly what a construction-time (rung 2) witness is for, since it is a defect in what construction itself does. — evidence: _scratchpad/t8253/ (pit8253_count0_probe.py, pit8253_site_probe.py)</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — MANUAL — WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:1118:CAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel&#x27;s own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a &#x27;+1&#x27; mutant that &#x27;admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green&#x27; -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today&#x27;s 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: &#x27;do tfreq first&#x27; (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 &#x27;CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store&#x27;), &#x27;mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness&#x27; (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and &#x27;drop constblind&#x27; (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/codex.txt.rerun</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — agy answered this panel. DROP AS A ROUND -- two seats explicitly, a third as &#x27;filed-only pending a reproduction&#x27;. It is the one item filed at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason: latent, unmeasured, different file. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held. If it survives, it needs its own reproduction first -- an ordering bug in what construction itself does is what a rung-2 witness is for. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — grok answered this panel. DROP AS A ROUND -- two seats explicitly, a third as &#x27;filed-only pending a reproduction&#x27;. It is the one item filed at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason: latent, unmeasured, different file. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held. If it survives, it needs its own reproduction first -- an ordering bug in what construction itself does is what a rung-2 witness is for. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — kimi answered this panel. DROP AS A ROUND -- two seats explicitly, a third as &#x27;filed-only pending a reproduction&#x27;. It is the one item filed at READ, NOT MEASURED, and the panel treated that grade as the reason: latent, unmeasured, different file. NOT EXECUTED: codex-held. If it survives, it needs its own reproduction first -- an ordering bug in what construction itself does is what a rung-2 witness is for. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_093038/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — 8253/8254 PIT CLUSTER: five MEASURED defects at one site, brought to the panel with the scope question promoted to Q0 -- the brief did not ask whether they are real (a rung-3 reproduction had settled that) but WHICH IS THE ROUND and how many may travel together.
