tools\pipeline\matrix.html:1804:CONSEQUENCE FOR #450&#x27;s record, stated there too: :751&#x27;s mis-link hazard is a counterfactual PARTLY BECAUSE this bug prevents the walk from ever reaching a node whose parent pointer would exercise it. Fixing this row may make that site live -- the coupling runs one way, like ctorabortclass before it.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:1812:2. A REAL SH7750 HARDWARE MANUAL was obtained mid-round by owner directive and immediately REFUTED a finding this round had filed (sh4aaalias). The loop argued the `aa` handlers ignoring bit 23 while `da1` treats it as an array selector meant one must be wrong; the P4 map says both are right, because the address windows are undivided 16 MB and the data windows hold arrays 1 AND 2 split at the 8 MB mark. The manual also confirms the entry is selected by bits [9:8] and that array 2 holds SA and TC -- DIFFERENT fields from array 1 -- which is exactly why declining is right and aliasing would have been wrong. — witness: probe:regress/sh4_tlbda2_witness.py (rung 3, 7 rows, unmodified -E landisk; 7/7 pre-fix and 4/7 post-fix, the three differences being exactly the three kill rows -- an ITLB load, a UTLB load, and an ITLB store, since the guard sits above the writeflag test). Its controls were STRENGTHENED mid-round: they asserted INEQUALITY against three wrong values, which any fourth wrong value passes, and a seat said so. They now assert EQUALITY with the value each device&#x27;s own mask folds 0xffffffff to -- 0x1ffffdfa and 0x1ffffdff, which DIFFER because the UTLB mask adds WT|D -- so neither row can be satisfied by the other device.</span></span><span class="state st-held" title="Fix and detector SHIPPED; gate 10 PASS at 62 checks run singly; build rc=0 zero warnings. Held open, not closed: both stages carry a deliberate fable5 omission and section H refuses a CLOSED row with a HELD stage. The question is in the fable5 cells.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:1818:2. A REAL SH7750 HARDWARE MANUAL was obtained mid-round by owner directive and immediately REFUTED a finding this round had filed (sh4aaalias). The loop argued the `aa` handlers ignoring bit 23 while `da1` treats it as an array selector meant one must be wrong; the P4 map says both are right, because the address windows are undivided 16 MB and the data windows hold arrays 1 AND 2 split at the 8 MB mark. The manual also confirms the entry is selected by bits [9:8] and that array 2 holds SA and TC -- DIFFERENT fields from array 1 -- which is exactly why declining is right and aliasing would have been wrong.">held</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: CONFIRMED, WITH ONE PREMISE IN MY BRIEF REFUTED -- the shipped decline is NOT silent. *** My question asked whether a silent decline owes an announce; it already carries one. dev_sh4.c:1059-1065 and :1231-1237 gate a fatal() through `sh4_val_first()`, an N=1-per-(class,instance,bit) latch, naming the absolute address and stating the semantics (&#x27;reads return 0, writes are ignored. (once)&#x27;), then zero-fill reads, drop stores, return 1. **Ceiling two lines per device lifetime, keyed on two explicit SITE BITS rather than the address** -- which a seat had flagged pre-edit as a collision risk, since the address is the same value at both sites. That is member 3 of the option set, in the device&#x27;s own idiom. *** IT READ THE MANUAL DIRECTLY RATHER THAN PARAPHRASING, and applied the extraction hazard to its own citations: *** sh7750_hardware.txt:10026/:10072 give ITLB data array 1 as H&#x27;F3000000-H&#x27;F37FFFFF and array 2 as H&#x27;F3800000-H&#x27;F3FFFFFF, with :10032/:10078 showing bits [31:23] as H&#x27;F30 vs H&#x27;F38 -- **the split is exactly bit 23, which is the shipped 0x800000 guard.** :10081/:10227 confirm array 2 holds SA[2:0] and TC[3], DIFFERENT fields from array 1&#x27;s PPN/V/SZ/PR/C/SH -- so aliasing would have been wrong and the commit&#x27;s stated ground for declining is true. Entry selectors match the code&#x27;s masks, neither containing bit 23. **It found the staggered-table hazard LIVE in this very section** (the map table pairs H&#x27;F6000000 with &#x27;Operand cache data array&#x27;, off by one row) and cited unstaggered PROSE paragraphs instead, with upstream sh4_mmu.h as an independent second source. One wrinkle it checked and deliberately did NOT file: the manual gives ITLB DA1&#x27;s PR as bit [6] while the handler masks 0x60, but bit 5 is &#x27;Reserved (0 write value, undefined read)&#x27; -- within the spec&#x27;s latitude, pre-existing, outside the diff. — evidence: Agent(model=fable) held-stage seat; both stages of this row were held for fable5 by a deliberate recorded omission. Liveness token FABLE_HOLDQ_20260826 plus a verbatim CHECKPOINT.md quote, both returned. Read-only, no builds.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: THE ROUND IS DISCHARGED. It confirms fable5 INDEPENDENTLY -- re-derived from the manual and the source rather than from fable5&#x27;s answer -- and MY BRIEF&#x27;S PREMISE (b) IS FALSE: the decline already carries a bounded announce. *** THE SPLIT CONFIRMED AT ALL FOUR WINDOWS by direct reading, each with a manual line number: ITLB address undivided at :9983; ITLB data arrays 1 AND 2 at :9052-9053, split DA1 F3000000-F37FFFFF (:10026, bits[31:23] = F30) / DA2 F3800000-F3FFFFFF (:10072, F38) -- **so the shipped `&amp; 0x800000` guard IS bit 23 exactly**; UTLB the same shape at :9061/:10113, :10171/:10218. Entry selectors match the code&#x27;s masks. `sh4aaalias` was correctly withdrawn. *** AND IT FOUND THE STAGGER HAZARD IS WORSE THAN THE CARRIER SAYS -- IT DEFEATS THE CARRIER&#x27;S OWN CALIBRATION RECIPE. Verified by reading :9014-9029 and CORRECTED IN THE CARRIER IN THE SAME COMMIT. *** Figure 3.4 has a MISSING NAME CELL at :9020, so the stagger BEGINS PARTWAY DOWN: the first three rows (F000/F100/F200) pair CORRECTLY and everything below is one row late. **A calibration point taken from above the gap certifies the table as sound.** A reader trusting the figure puts ITLB data at F4000000 and UTLB data at F8000000 -- and F8000000 is RESERVED. The figure would have condemned both shipped constants. Refinement adopted: calibrate BELOW the value cited, or on two rows STRADDLING it; one point is only valid for a UNIFORM stagger and you cannot tell which kind you have from one point. Prefer the unstaggered PROSE. SECOND CARRIER CORRECTION, with its denominator: the carrier calls `(further occurrences suppressed)` the house idiom. Censused across src/devices/*.c by MESSAGE TEXT: `(once)` 9, `(further occurrences suppressed)` 3, `(reported once)` 2. **`(once)` is the plurality.** The seat was careful about which quantity it disputed -- the carrier&#x27;s &#x27;roughly 34 sites&#x27; is probably a count of latch VARIABLES -- and claimed only what it counted. Corrected. *** ITS ONE DISAGREEMENT WITH fable5, and it is a real filed residual where fable5&#x27;s clause- (f) pass said &#x27;nothing owed&#x27;: the detector pins guard GROWTH into the entry-selector bits and does NOT pin the DECODE of those bits into an index. *** The A rows are same-path round-trips, so they are invariant under any injective re-map. Under `e -&gt; e + 1`, A1/A2/A3 round-trip through entry 1 and stay GREEN, while **A4i round-trips through `itlb_lo[4]` -- which IS `utlb_hi[0]`, verified: cpu_sh.h:133-134 declares them adjacent -- so the row stays green while a live UTLB entry&#x27;s VPN/ASID is overwritten.** Unlike the #463 exemplar there is no post-add mask, so the translation ESCAPES the array instead of wrapping. **Graded honestly as COVERAGE DEBT, not a floor breach and not a reopening**, with the reasoning for each clause given; one anchored row closes it (write via DA1 at entry N, read the VPN back via the ADDRESS array at entry N). IT ALSO BOUNDED fable5&#x27;s PROPOSED RULE: &#x27;...or a recorded reason&#x27; is a hatch with no floor -- the same shape as &#x27;weak earns its own round&#x27;, which this carrier already had to attach a floor to. Its fix needs no new mechanism: the recorded reason must be one of exactly TWO -- (i) the sites are textually identical in the mutated construct, so the dual is a compile-time equivalent, or (ii) the dual was BUILT and measured EQUIVALENT under the existing non-dying-mutant rule. Anything else is a dual owed. AND IT VERIFIED fable5&#x27;s REJECTION OF THE MECHANISATION RATHER THAN RATIFYING IT: the proposed string check is green on its own founding instance, because the witness is TEST-FIRST and so defines UTLB_DA1 by construction. **By the vacuity floor&#x27;s clause (a) applied to a harness check, it is vacuous.** ONE FALSE ALARM OF ITS OWN, recorded because the rule caught it: `memory_sh.c:122` reads `itlb_lo[i + SH_N_ITLB_ENTRIES]`, which reads as out-of-bounds until you read nine lines up where `i_start = -SH_N_ITLB_ENTRIES`. It nearly cited it. — evidence: Agent(model=opus) held-stage seat, batch D. BUILT AND MEASURED: two scratch trees (/tmp/gxm8b, /tmp/gxm8c), both cmp-verified byte-identical to SEC before removal, no .MUTANT sentinel left. Durable artifacts at _scratchpad/opus_holdq_20260826b/ -- m8sar_kill_table.log, 16 .raw run logs, and two composed-row prototypes. It disclosed contention unprompted (codex/agy/grok/ollama panel seats running throughout) and noted its own work is load-insensitive: gcc compiles of single-file offline harnesses, no wall-clock oracle, no gate, no rig, no emulator boot.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Prescribed the shipped design almost exactly: return 1, zero-fill reads, drop stores, and one class with two explicit SITE bits -- while naming the hazard that made the site bits necessary. `relative_addr &amp; 0x800000` is the SAME non-zero value at both sites, so a key derived from it lets whichever site complains first silence the other for the device lifetime. It also refused to assert what it could not check: it said plainly it had not verified the array bounds or the `aa` handler bodies, and named the exact checks required -- including the stricter form (check through r &lt; 0x01000000, and whether either entry mask contains 0x00800000) that the loop then ran.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Worked the whole thing from source with line citations and got the disposition right, but its most useful contribution was a prediction that turned out FALSE and was built anyway. Separately confirmed the registration lengths and the guard&#x27;s position above the writeflag test.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — *** READ THE DISPATCH ARMS INSTEAD OF THE SUMMARY, AND CORRECTED A CLAIM THE ROUND WAS ABOUT TO SHIP. *** The brief and an early comment draft said `return 0` raises an architecture-specific bus-error exception. It does not: memory_rw.c&#x27;s failure block has MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K arms ONLY, `MEM_SH` appears nowhere in the file, and cpu_sh_instr.c&#x27;s X(load_l_rm_rn) then returns WITHOUT assigning the destination register -- leaving it stale, under a comment of its own that claims an Exception. The conclusion (do not return 0) survived and got stronger; the reason was wrong. It also reached the #95 pre-zeroing conclusion by reading, which the loop reached by measuring, and prescribed the same disposition: write the zero anyway so the arm does not depend on a guarantee two files away.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Verified the C line by line and was the seat that questioned the WITNESS rather than the fix: its controls asserted INEQUALITY against three wrong values, which any fourth wrong value passes -- a mutated mask included. Correct, and the controls were rebuilt to assert EQUALITY with the value each device&#x27;s own mask folds 0xffffffff to. Also flagged the `(int)` cast feeding `%08x` as a principle-level nit that changes nothing observable, which is exactly how it was then dispositioned.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Packet-fed and explicit about it. Reached the return-1 / zero-fill / drop-stores answer independently and refused to guess about the `aa` handlers whose bodies it had not been given -- naming that as the check to run rather than inferring from window size.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Gave the cleanest statement of why `return 0` is wrong even before the SH-specific correction landed, and caught an overclaim in the BRIEF itself: &#x27;an unbounded key is a flood&#x27; is false, a full-byte key is bounded at 256; the objection is bounded-but-excessive amplification. Also refused to rule on the `aa` windows without their bodies.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Named the mutant three seats converged on -- dropping the declined read&#x27;s memory_writemax64 -- and was the first to state the reasoning precisely: `odata = 0` alone is not enough because the caller&#x27;s buffer is what the guest receives. The prediction was MEASURED FALSE (see the row&#x27;s hold), but the reasoning was sound C and the disposition it implied (write the zero explicitly) is what shipped.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: THE ENGINEERING IS DISCHARGED -- floor (a)-(f) carried by measured kills -- BUT THE ROW MAY NOT CLOSE: opus5 is unrecorded on BOTH stages, currently invisible only because the fable5 holds mask it. *** The moment these answers replace those holds, both stages become 8/9 post-cutoff and section H hard-fails naming opus5. **That is the checker&#x27;s own documented exitsweep hazard -- a hold naming one seat silently dropping another -- happening in the MARKER rather than in the code.** Disposition: fire opus5 once (it is the measuring seat and this round&#x27;s central artifacts are measurements), or an owner-recorded deliberate omission with the honest token. Not silent closure. FLOOR, CLAUSE BY CLAUSE against the 16-mutant table (15 dead, 1 equivalent): (a) V1/V2 die and the witness shows 7/7 pre-fix vs 4/7 post-fix with the three deltas being exactly the kill rows; (b) verbatim reinstatement at BOTH sites separately; (c) an identity row pins 15 rows and the gate requires the result line, exactly one PASS token and &gt;=10 rows, so there is no silent-skip path; **(d) carried in BOTH directions at BOTH sites** -- guard narrowed, guard grown (closed by all-bits-set accept offsets), repair rerouted with guard kept, announce nulled, address-derived key; (e) the contract names each row&#x27;s target mutant AND the equivalent mutant it deliberately does not chase. The one non-dying mutant is disposed per the non-dying-mutant rule -- measured EQUIVALENT in five contexts, carrying no row, with an absence record naming both its warrant and its EXPIRY CONDITION. It checked clause (f) even though that rule postdates the round, and found nothing owed: the detector&#x27;s oracles are anchored by two channels outside the value path. It also ran its own adversarial two-edit composite (fallthrough plus shifted index) and found the pass-2 decline assertions already close it. *** ON THE RULE QUESTION HELD IN BOTH CELLS -- whether two-site rounds should get a MECHANICAL check -- IT REJECTED THE MECHANISATION AGAINST ITS OWN FOUNDING INSTANCE: *** &#x27;a detector whose rows never mention the second site&#x27;s base address fails&#x27; **would have PASSED the defective pre-pass-2 detector**, because two rows already mentioned UTLB_DA1; the hole was per-site MUTANT duals, not string absence. The right landing spot already exists -- the pre-table check&#x27;s SIBLING PARITY clause -- sharpened by one word (sites of a multi-site fix ARE sibling arms) with the kill-table form stated: **every mutant built at one site owes a site-scoped dual at each other edited site, or a recorded reason.** Derivable from the diff, bounded because sites are finite, and it protects what the ask-again cap protects: the single query should buy novel adversarial shapes, not the author&#x27;s own asymmetry. A gate parsing kill tables would need machine-readable tables that do not exist -- #392-shaped harness growth for a class the checklist covers at zero mechanism. — evidence: Agent(model=fable) held-stage seat; both stages of this row were held for fable5 by a deliberate recorded omission. Liveness token FABLE_HOLDQ_20260826 plus a verbatim CHECKPOINT.md quote, both returned. Read-only, no builds.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — REVIEW: **DISCHARGED.** It re-checked the floor independently of fable5 and agreed on (a)-(e), added the clause-(f) residual above as COVERAGE DEBT, and confirmed clause (c) has no silent-skip path -- the probe&#x27;s identity row pins the exact count and reds to SH4TLBDA2_FAIL, so the gate&#x27;s `grep -c SH4TLBDA2_PASS` goes to 0 != 1. It noted the gate&#x27;s separate `check_min ... 10` is SLACK but that the exact pin lives inside the probe, so nothing is owed. It also disposed of the PR wrinkle fable5 raised but did not file, replacing &#x27;within the spec&#x27;s latitude&#x27; with a MEASURED reason: reserved bit 5 reaches `itlb_lo[e]` and NO CONSUMER -- memory_sh.c scans the ITLB only under FLAG_INSTR and the instruction path consults only `pr &amp; 2`, while `pr &amp; 1` is read only on the data path, which never sees an ITLB entry. Inert, pre-existing, outside the diff. **Row CLOSES once these entries land.** — evidence: Agent(model=opus) held-stage seat, batch D. BUILT AND MEASURED: two scratch trees (/tmp/gxm8b, /tmp/gxm8c), both cmp-verified byte-identical to SEC before removal, no .MUTANT sentinel left. Durable artifacts at _scratchpad/opus_holdq_20260826b/ -- m8sar_kill_table.log, 16 .raw run logs, and two composed-row prototypes. It disclosed contention unprompted (codex/agy/grok/ollama panel seats running throughout) and noted its own work is load-insensitive: gcc compiles of single-file offline harnesses, no wall-clock oracle, no gate, no rig, no emulator boot. -- REVIEW phase; the seat answered both stages in one pass.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-FIX, the only one this session, and TWO of its findings were measured true at 10/10. `relative_addr &amp; 0x800100` -- one character -- falsely declines array-1 entry 1 while every declined probe sits at exactly +0x800000 and every accept probe at 0 or +0x400000. And `== 0x800000` narrows the guard so the REST of array 2 silently aliases array 1. It prescribed both killing rows (+0x7ffffc accept, +0x800100 decline) and they work. It also caught the round&#x27;s third overclaim: R1/R2 were NAMED &#x27;array 1 answered ... in the same session&#x27; when that value lands in r2 and is overwritten by the da2 load before anything stores it -- a name, not a check.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SOUND, and the seat that got the least out of this pass -- its pass-1 prediction had already been built and refuted, and it raised no new mutant. Recorded as such rather than padded: a seat that agrees and adds nothing is a real datum about the diff, not a failure.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DETECTOR, and it found the STRUCTURAL half: R3, RPT and A3 were ALL built on the ITLB site, so the UTLB copies of three already-known mutants had no coverage. Its sharpest instance is a WRITE-ONLY fallthrough -- `return 1` moved inside the MEM_READ arm -- which is read-clean and therefore invisible to every read row, while a declined store lands in utlb_lo[e] for the entry array 1 just used. It said plainly it had not built these. All three were built and all three died on the rows added for them. *** THIS IS #451&#x27;S PASS-2 LESSON MIRRORED: there the FIRST site was under-exercised, here the second. A two-site round builds its rows on whichever site it wrote first, and that is now twice. ***</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DETECTOR, and it GENERALISED past every other seat&#x27;s instance. The guard is a bitwise-OR predicate, so single-bit growth into ANY of bits 8..22 is as realistic an edit as G1&#x27;s growth into bit 22 and just as invisible -- and bits 8..13 are the ENTRY SELECTOR, so such a growth silently refuses REAL translation entries. Its own instance (`&amp; 0x802000` at UTLB, declining entries 32-63) was built and died. It also gave the cheap general fix -- one accept row per device whose offset has every probeable low bit SET, tripping on growth into any of them at once -- which is what A4i/A4u are, and it caught that &#x27;G1 discharges the de-escalation clause&#x27; was itself an overclaim: G1 discharged one bit position at one site, and there are two predicates.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SOUND. Confirmed the fix shape against the family and raised no new mutant; its pass-1 asks were already built in by then.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SOUND. Its 238 KB answer was almost entirely reasoning-in-the-open rather than findings; recorded as answered-without-new-material.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SOUND. Confirmed the corrected MEM_SH analysis and the equivalence finding; raised no new mutant.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcrearmzero</span><span class="ttl">wdcrearmzero  The ATAPI re-arm reports ZERO bytes remaining mid-transfer, so every transfer above 64 KB stalls and never completes</span><span class="tip">wdcrearmzero  The ATAPI re-arm reports ZERO bytes remaining mid-transfer, so every transfer above 64 KB stalls and never completes — commits: 1f1a02b — witness: detector:regress/diff_wdc_identify.c -- OWNER-ADOPTED TERM, 2026-08-27.  The fix shipped with a MEASURED detector and a kill table, and no guest-path witness.  `detector:` IS NOT A RUNG: it ranks below every access witness and does NOT discharge the rung-3 ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires.  Before this term the row had to be written `none`, which asserts DOCUMENTED-ONLY and was false about a round that shipped a fix. — note: *** CLOSED 2026-08-27, and its detector was VACUOUS until the kill table said so. ***
