tools\pipeline\matrix.html:1966:the four handshake readers, and a PRETABLE.out. — evidence: Agent seat (fable5), adjudication 2026-08-29; every element re-derived from the tree read-only</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">--</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: opus5] The pass-1 design stage fired the seven scriptable seats only. Both flagship seats answered the same design questions at PASS 2 and both found something the design pass missed, so the substance is covered and the STAGE is not. NOT backfilled from those pass-2 answers: a pass-1 entry written from a pass-2 answer is a false record. THE QUESTION: three separate guards now stand in for one missing structural separation -- the dispatch phase guard, the read-arm phase guard, and the occupancy bound that turns out to be the only thing sizing a malloc(12). Is option F (give the CDB its own accumulator, per dev_mb89352.c) now the right call rather than a fourth guard? And was option C (clear the flag at completion) wrongly dismissed, given that #477&#x27;s read-arm repair is a phase-scoped version of exactly that? Measured migration cost of F: it relocates where a CDB lives, so every probe that fakes one stops working. — evidence: tools/pipeline/fable_queue.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Design pass on the five-member option set. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/codex.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Design pass. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Design pass. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/grok.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — [transport: kimi-k3:cloud (substitute)] NAMED THE POINT-OF-USE DECLINE IN ITS VERDICT LINE. Recorded late; the CLI seat is 403-walled and this substitute transport carried it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/ollama_kimi.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Design pass. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/ollama_glm.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Design pass. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/ollama_deepseek.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Design pass. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_071302/ollama_minimax.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — CONFIRMED — GO-WITH-CHANGES, and it MEASURED the regression the brief asked for: the phase guard alone wedges a legal CDB permanently after a stray data-port read, on a sequence HEAD services. L1, repaired in-round with its measured one-condition fix. Also measured the brief&#x27;s own &#x27;the command simply stalls&#x27; framing false. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_080240/(Agent seat -- no file in the panel dir)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — CONFIRMED — DO NOT CLOSE, and it was right on a measurable. KILLED the round&#x27;s own classification of M7_ge12: `&gt;= 12` is a heap-buffer-overflow WRITE at dev_wdc.c:1048 under ASAN, because inbuf_len == 12 is the ONLY bound on a copy into malloc(12) nine lines below -- and the detector reads 44/0 on BOTH sides. L4 corrected in place; the row `occupancy above 12 does not dispatch` now kills it alone. Also MEASURED wdcringnoflush (ATAPI_PKT_CMD and both reset paths never flush the ring; occ 32-&gt;44-&gt;56-&gt;68), which is the other entrance to this round&#x27;s wedge and upgrades wdcsrstnoabort to MEASURED. Plus three defects in my diff: a dropped tab from a whitespace anchor, the comment housed in the wrong arm, and a bare same-file citation. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_080240/ (Agent seat -- no file in the panel dir)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — CONFIRMED — *** DO NOT CLOSE, AND MEASURED CORRECT. *** Reasoned that deleting the atapi_cmd_in_progress conjunct would sweep the 45-row detector while reopening spurious CDB dispatch; it could not build the mutant in a read-only workspace. Built and measured here: M_phaseonly swept ALL 52 ROWS clean. Mechanism confirmed exactly as described -- wdc_command(ATAPI_PKT_CMD) sets PHASE_CMDOUT, a following wdc_command(WDCC_WRITE) clears the flag and leaves the phase STALE. So both conjuncts are load-bearing and #477&#x27;s headline was a WRONG RECORD in product code (L4, corrected in place). row_stale_phase_write added: the mutant now reds 2 rows, and the sector is DESTROYED (0 w, 0 b) rather than merely short -- worse than the seat predicted. Also confirmed independently that the atapi_st NULL class IS closed (looked for a seventh deref, found none) and that no legal sequence is rejected. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/codex.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — GO. No defect found in the shipped guards. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Answered; no verdict line (format miss). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/grok.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — [transport: kimi-k3:cloud substitute; CLI 403-walled] Answered 6.3 KB with a verdict line -- the FIRST panel since the PACKET_PREAMBLE fix, and the seat that stalled at 127 bytes one panel earlier now answers normally. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/ollama_kimi.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Answered; no verdict line (format miss). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/ollama_glm.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — REASONED. Packet-fed limits stated honestly. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/ollama_deepseek.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — MANUAL — Judged the two guards sound on the packet. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_093658/ollama_minimax.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-27 — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  *** RESTORED 2026-08-29.  My marker sweep DEFANGED this and should not have. ***  The sweep&#x27;s criterion was ROW-SCOPED -- &#x27;the named seat answered anywhere on this row&#x27; -- so fable5&#x27;s RESEARCH-phase answer of 2026-08-29 was taken to discharge this REGRESS-phase hold.  It does not: **regress is a FABLE-ONLY stage and fable5 has never answered it on this row**, so this hold is still genuinely owed.  Worse, because entry classification keys on note CONTENT, the defanged text no longer matched the held literal and fell through to the seat tally -- **making this entry the ONLY evidence that fable5 answered regress, which is a manufactured seat answer.**  That is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape, created by the sweep meant to remove it.  Found by an adjudicating seat that was explicitly asked whether anything had been wrongly defanged. QUESTION: this row closed 2026-08-27 while its own research stage stood at 7 of 9 seats, and check_stage_panels correctly refused it. The fix shipped in 0922465 (#477) WITH a full pass-2 panel and the row is detector-covered and green. SO WHICH PANEL DISCHARGES A ROW: the row&#x27;s own stage, or the stage of the round that fixed it? Today the gate demands the first -- under which a row can never close on work done under another row&#x27;s number, which is how most cross-filed fixes land here. If that is right, fire the two seats and audit the other cross-filed rows the same way. If it is wrong, the check needs a FIXED-ELSEWHERE disposition naming the discharging commit and panel. Do not answer &#x27;fire the seats&#x27; without saying which of those the rule is.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-held"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcnullxfer</span><span class="ttl">wdcnullxfer  #475&#x27;s clamp WOKE a NULL dereference that the broken counter had been hiding -- two guest register accesses SIGSEGV the host.  FIXED in-round as #476</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">wdcnullxfer  #475&#x27;s clamp WOKE a NULL dereference that the broken counter had been hiding -- two guest register accesses SIGSEGV the host.  FIXED in-round as #476 — witness: detector:regress/diff_wdc_identify.c -- a FORKING rung-1 fixture that reports the child&#x27;s SIGNAL.  It observes the real harm (SIGSEGV) rather than a proxy, and carries an attribution control: the pre-#475 blob survives the identical input.  *** RE-WITNESSED 2026-08-27 UNDER THE OWNER-ADOPTED `detector:` TERM. ***  It previously read `none`, whose schema meaning is DOCUMENTED-ONLY -- false about a row whose own witness text describes a detector.  That conflation is exactly what the term was adopted to end: before it, fixed-with-a-detector and documented-only had to be written the same way.  `detector:` is NOT a rung and does not discharge the rung-3 attempt this row still owes. — note: *** DID `#476` CLOSE THE CLASS OR ONE INSTANCE?  ENUMERATED 2026-08-27, and the answer is
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2089:* Q5 QUEUE RISK THE BATTERY CANNOT SEE, ranked: armbdt (the only MEASURED defect, battery structurally green over it); idesync (the #437 CLASS on the other disk stack, and the ATA rig boots a ramdisk kernel that issues no flush traffic); b120r; the four wdc residuals including a guest-set malloc count behind CHECK_ALLOCATION; the three selfmutant apparatus items; and m437multi carries INVERTED risk -- the battery now PINS the conservative behaviour that item wants changed, so its fix round must expect a SCHEDULED RED, not a regression.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">reprowitness</span><span class="ttl">reprowitness  The reproducibility rule asks &#x27;can this MACHINE boot&#x27; when it should ask &#x27;can this CODE PATH be executed&#x27;</span><span class="tip">reprowitness  The reproducibility rule asks &#x27;can this MACHINE boot&#x27; when it should ask &#x27;can this CODE PATH be executed&#x27; — note: *** BOTH GAPS CLOSED 2026-08-26; THIS ROW IS DISCHARGED PENDING ITS REMAINING SEATS. *** The codex seat answering this row&#x27;s hold_question found the shipped ladder INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT about rung 2 and missing a category its own checker already enforced. Both were verified by reading and then fixed in the carrier. (a) Clause (ii) said &#x27;rung-2-4&#x27; and demanded a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control -- &#x27;a known value returns through the same decode&#x27;. **Rung 2 is machine CONSTRUCTION, which performs no address decode and returns no device value, so the ladder&#x27;s own named exemplar (the macppc heap OOB, #23) could never satisfy the ladder&#x27;s own clause.** Clause (ii) is now scoped to ACCESS witnesses, rungs 3-4, and rung 2 owes construction-specific liveness and ATTRIBUTION controls instead -- same two-control discipline, different observable. (b) `check_witness.py:56` has accepted `harness:` since its first run and its header says outright that none of the four rungs fits a defect in the TEST APPARATUS itself; `precommit_check.sh:678` repeats it. The carrier still said &#x27;no witness at rung 2-4 -&gt; document instead&#x27;. **`harness:&lt;what proved it&gt;` is now stated, scoped to rounds whose SUBJECT is the apparatus, with the boundary that keeps it honest: it never licenses an #include/direct-call witness for emulator code.** *** THE META-POINT IS WORTH KEEPING: THIS IS THE STRUCTURE-VS-PROSE FAILURE INVERTED. *** The usual shape is a rule in prose that never fires. Here the STRUCTURE knew -- the checker had the category, enforced it, and documented why -- and the PROSE never absorbed it. Auto-loaded text can be stale in BOTH directions, and a tool&#x27;s own header is a primary source about the tool.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2105:* AGREES exitsweep stays at ONE SITE, but on a different and stronger ground: not &#x27;only one is reproducible&#x27; (false) but the THIRD DOCTRINE ARM, which bounds it on SEMANTICS and does not evaporate when someone writes a probe. Proposed reopening rule: add a second site only when it is witnessed, in the SAME DEVICE and SAME SEMANTIC CLASS so one fix design covers both, and covered by the SAME DETECTOR without a new oracle -- under which the eight footbridge sites are ONE candidate round, not eight.  [panel_20260819_072053]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: NOT DISCHARGED. The core finding was adopted, but the shipped ladder is INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT about rung 2 and omits a category its own checker already implements. *** Both gaps VERIFIED before recording. GAP 1 -- rung 2 versus clause (ii). The ladder admits CONSTRUCTION as a witness specifically for init-time defects, naming the macppc heap OOB. But the universal admissibility clauses require every rung-2-4 witness to carry a LIVENESS control and a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control -- &#x27;a known value returns through the same decode&#x27;. **A construction-time OOB performs no address decode and returns no device value, so the ladder&#x27;s own named exemplar cannot satisfy its own clause.** Clause (ii) should be scoped to ACCESS witnesses (rungs 3-4), with construction-specific controls for rung 2. GAP 2 -- the `harness:` category. `check_witness.py:56` accepts `^(boot:|probe:|construction:|harness:|none)` and its own header at :24-28 says **&#x27;`harness:` IS A GAP IN THE LADDER, FOUND BY THIS CHECK ON ITS FIRST RUN&#x27;** -- none of the four rungs fits a defect in the TEST APPARATUS itself. `precommit_check.sh:678` repeats the finding. CLAUDE.md still says &#x27;no witness at rung 2-4 -&gt; document instead&#x27; and never states the exception. **The tool wrote the gap down and the carrier never absorbed it**, which is the structure-vs-prose failure inverted: here the STRUCTURE knew and the PROSE did not. It also names the boundary that keeps the category honest -- `harness:` is proof only when the harness IS the subject, and must never license an `#include`/direct-call witness for emulator code. — evidence: codex 5.6-SOL xhigh, fired against the row&#x27;s own `hold_question` (_scratchpad/holdq_briefs/holdq_reprowitness.md; answer at scratchpad/holdq_out/reprowitness.txt). NOTE: codex streams reasoning to STDERR here -- the .err files run 1-3 MB while the answer file is 2-5 KB, so a size check on the wrong stream would score these as stalls.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-19 — ADOPT-WITH-CHANGES. Probe witnesses on COMMITTED BINARIES equal boots; offline #include harnesses EXCLUDED. Supplied the governing principle the panel converged on -- a valid witness must preserve the HARDWARE SELECTION AND ROUTING MECHANISMS -- and a four-part anti-laundering clause: a witness is invalid unless it demonstrates a defect symptom on the PRE-FIX committed HEAD, records pre-fix failure and post-fix success with the same script, carries a positive liveness control AND an isolation control, and otherwise counts as documentation rather than reproduction. It also rejected the brief&#x27;s own dfreq argument as insufficient -- correctly, though for a different reason than the one the measure seat then proved.  [panel_20260819_072053]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-19 — *** SUPPLIED THE DISCRIMINATOR THE OTHER SIX SEATS DID NOT: A TEST, NOT A DEFINITION. *** &#x27;If your reproduction still compiles and still fails after the in-tree machine description and the CPU/device dispatch are removed -- leaving only a direct call -- IT WAS NEVER A REPRODUCTION.&#x27; That is mechanically checkable, where &#x27;preserves routing&#x27; still needs judgement. Its one-sentence laundering test is the same idea sharpened: &#x27;I can write a probe that calls this function&#x27; is not a reproduction, it is A RESTATEMENT OF THE SOURCE.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2170:form: *a non-event admits no rung-3/4 witness (a ceiling four seat entries already record);
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2182:CAVEAT RECORDED ON EVERY RE-FIRED CODEX ENTRY: codex reviewed the brief AS IT WAS FIRED (byte-identical, from this panel&#x27;s own brief_sent.md, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live). THE TREE HAS MOVED SINCE. Its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a &#x27;+1&#x27; mutant that &#x27;admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green&#x27; -- CORRECT against the 9-row table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today&#x27;s 18-row table (2 failures). So a codex finding here is HISTORICAL unless re-checked, and three are already settled by measurement: &#x27;do tfreq first&#x27; (tfreq is fixed -- timer.c:188 &#x27;CLAMP FIRST, THEN compare, THEN store&#x27;), &#x27;mrwstore2 first by producing a real-guest witness&#x27; (measured: three guest boots, ZERO straddling stores from guest code, and only PowerPC can emit one at all), and &#x27;drop constblind&#x27; (a live instance was measured and fixed hours earlier). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/codex.txt.rerun</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — agy answered this cluster panel. ROUND C, alone. Different oracle (fsync ordering), different fix (a new diskimage_sync_id() export, a WDCC_FLUSHCACHE_EXT constant, new case arms), and it crosses into diskimage.c. Note the honest ceiling the filing already records: NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES 0xE7 OR 0xEA -- verified with two discriminating IDENTIFY variants where the guest demonstrably read the new IDENTIFY and still never flushed -- so this can only ever be construction-tested, never witnessed at rung 3 or 4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — grok answered this cluster panel. ROUND C, alone. Different oracle (fsync ordering), different fix (a new diskimage_sync_id() export, a WDCC_FLUSHCACHE_EXT constant, new case arms), and it crosses into diskimage.c. Note the honest ceiling the filing already records: NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES 0xE7 OR 0xEA -- verified with two discriminating IDENTIFY variants where the guest demonstrably read the new IDENTIFY and still never flushed -- so this can only ever be construction-tested, never witnessed at rung 3 or 4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — kimi answered this cluster panel. ROUND C, alone. Different oracle (fsync ordering), different fix (a new diskimage_sync_id() export, a WDCC_FLUSHCACHE_EXT constant, new case arms), and it crosses into diskimage.c. Note the honest ceiling the filing already records: NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES 0xE7 OR 0xEA -- verified with two discriminating IDENTIFY variants where the guest demonstrably read the new IDENTIFY and still never flushed -- so this can only ever be construction-tested, never witnessed at rung 3 or 4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — CLUSTER PANEL on wdc_command()&#x27;s `default:` arm -- five rows, one code path, none of them previously reviewed by anyone but the seat that filed it. *** THE SEATS WERE UNANIMOUS ON THE QUESTION THAT DECIDES THE WORK: SPLIT. *** Four independent verdict lines said so (agy: &#x27;four defects in one arm require three distinct oracles, cross subsystem boundaries&#x27;; deepseek: &#x27;one default: arm yields at least three distinct semantic classes/oracles&#x27;; minimax: &#x27;the four rows collapse to two distinct fix designs&#x27;; glm: &#x27;one arm, three distinct oracles -- host-log count, IRQ assertion, fsync ordering&#x27;). The reopening rule&#x27;s NO-NEW-ORACLE prong is what does the splitting, and the argument is mechanical rather than stylistic: a detector that counts host stderr lines cannot observe int_assert; one that observes int_assert cannot observe fsync ordering; one that observes fsync ordering cannot count log lines. THE AGREED SPLIT:
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2185:  Round C  idesync alone             -- oracle: fsync ordering; crosses a module boundary into diskimage.c and cannot be witnessed above rung 2, since no guest in this tree issues the opcode.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2189:FOR THIS ROW: ROUND C, alone. Different oracle (fsync ordering), different fix (a new diskimage_sync_id() export, a WDCC_FLUSHCACHE_EXT constant, new case arms), and it crosses into diskimage.c. Note the honest ceiling the filing already records: NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES 0xE7 OR 0xEA -- verified with two discriminating IDENTIFY variants where the guest demonstrably read the new IDENTIFY and still never flushed -- so this can only ever be construction-tested, never witnessed at rung 3 or 4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — deepseek answered this cluster panel. ROUND C, alone. Different oracle (fsync ordering), different fix (a new diskimage_sync_id() export, a WDCC_FLUSHCACHE_EXT constant, new case arms), and it crosses into diskimage.c. Note the honest ceiling the filing already records: NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES 0xE7 OR 0xEA -- verified with two discriminating IDENTIFY variants where the guest demonstrably read the new IDENTIFY and still never flushed -- so this can only ever be construction-tested, never witnessed at rung 3 or 4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — BELIEVED — minimax answered this cluster panel. ROUND C, alone. Different oracle (fsync ordering), different fix (a new diskimage_sync_id() export, a WDCC_FLUSHCACHE_EXT constant, new case arms), and it crosses into diskimage.c. Note the honest ceiling the filing already records: NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES 0xE7 OR 0xEA -- verified with two discriminating IDENTIFY variants where the guest demonstrably read the new IDENTIFY and still never flushed -- so this can only ever be construction-tested, never witnessed at rung 3 or 4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260819_235917/</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-19 — *** THE BRIEF&#x27;S FRAMING WAS HALF WRONG, AND THE HALF IT GOT WRONG CHANGES WHAT SHOULD BE FIXED. *** Verdict FIX-not-document, but not for the reason I gave it.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2202:Cost: a new public export, a new constant, two case arms and a detector that can only ever be construction-tested -- against a command NO GUEST IN THIS TREE ISSUES and which the model CORRECTLY DECLINES TO ADVERTISE. Worst ratio of the five rows. witness: none. — evidence: wdc_initialize_identify_struct() stops at word 68; diskimage.c walk census</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcflood</span><span class="ttl">wdcflood  wdc_command()&#x27;s default: fatal() is unlatched -- 100 commands, 100 host log lines</span><span class="tip">wdcflood  wdc_command()&#x27;s default: fatal() is unlatched -- 100 commands, 100 host log lines — commits: 92e917e — CLOSE (assess) — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/ (rung 3, MEASURED 2026-08-25 by opus5) -- real SH4 guest instructions through real decode and real memory_rw on the UNMODIFIED committed `-E landisk` description (machine_landisk.c:87-90, addr_mult=2, wd_command at physical 0x1400000e). Both admissibility controls present: LIVENESS (implemented command 0xec yields ZERO lines, so the rig can produce zero) and DEVICE-SIGNATURE (storing 0x5a echoes `command 0x5a` x20 and `command 0xaa` x0 -- the guest&#x27;s own byte, which RAM cannot produce). No device was added to reach it. — note: *** STATUS FIRST, BECAUSE THIS NOTE OPENED WITH PRE-FIX GUIDANCE THAT THE SHIPPED FIX HAS ALREADY CONSUMED.  Added 2026-08-29. ***  **FIXED at HEAD** -- #464 (six unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` sites, re-scoped from one arm to six), #485 (the completion interrupt, without which the abort was polling-only), #486 (repaired the round&#x27;s own non-discriminating clause-(b) gate row).  A rung-3 witness with both admissibility controls is on the row.  **It stays `in work` for exactly two reasons: a minimax pass-2 seat failure owed a re-fire with a repaired brief, and closure adjudication.**  The title is the FOUNDING claim, which is this ledger&#x27;s convention and not a wrong record -- but a reader meeting `in work` plus a &#x27;100 commands, 100 host log lines&#x27; title plus work-it-this-way guidance concludes the flood is live, which is the es438 / [[ledgerstatelies]] shape.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2204:  *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET APPLIES DIRECTLY TO THIS ROW (carrier rule, minted 2026-08-25 from #463). *** #463 de-fatalised a device by framing the choice as KEEP the unlatched `fatal()` versus DELETE it, chose delete, and its own research stage then found the third member had been shipping in-tree the whole time: **`memory_rw.c:495-523` bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`, because &#x27;probing at the end of a device&#x27;s mapped range is common and benign&#x27;.** The carrier already said &#x27;a guest-drivable fatal() needs a latch and not a verbosity flag&#x27; -- standing rule, never consulted by that round&#x27;s two-membered framing. **So before this row is worked: the option set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+BOUNDED-ANNOUNCE, and the first step is to grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first principles.** #463 also established that a bounded announce is not merely acceptable but RECOVERS the self-announcing property that de-fatalising destroys -- which for an unlatched flood is the whole point, since the flood and the announcement are the same channel.  *** MEASURED 2026-08-25, AND THE ROW IS AIMED AT THE WRONG SITE. *** The opus5 seat built a `cp -a` copy at /tmp/gxwdc, verified freshness independently of MAKE_RC, and left GXEMUL-SEC untouched (verified by `git status --porcelain`: no src file modified). *** THE FLOOD IS CONFIRMED AND IS WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS: *** 20 stores produce 20 `fatal()` lines WITH and WITHOUT `-q` (0 `debug()` lines either way), and the OVER-READ site at 500 iterations produced 2000 lines / 158,449 bytes of host log (`regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/n3.out`, preserved off the volatile session scratchpad this row used to cite). *** CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-26: this sentence said &quot;the read path&quot;, which points a reader at the non-8-bit READ guard. `n3.out` is 2000x &quot;reading too much&quot; -- the RING OVER-READ. Same numbers, wrong site named. The correction had already landed in the round block, the source comment and the gate comment while this field kept the false wording -- which is the corrections-land-in-CHANGELOG-only failure this ledger records having made twice before, now three times. It was found by a mechanical sweep for stale claims across all five records, not by reading. *** *** BUT `:583`, THE SITE THIS ROW NAMES, IS THE MORE GATED OF TWO FLOODS. *** It requires an attached IDE image (`:461-468` early-returns with a silenceable `debug()` otherwise) and yields ONE line per store. The unnamed sibling `wdc_get_inbuf` over-read at `:160` requires NOTHING -- landisk adds wdc unconditionally at machine_landisk.c:90 -- is on the READ path, and yields FOUR lines per single guest instruction (measured: mov.b/mov.w/mov.l x20 give 20/40/80). SIBLING CENSUS: ten `fatal()` in dev_wdc.c, FOUR latched by #337, SIX UNLATCHED -- :145, :160, :583, :607, :704, :1025. **Fixing :583 alone leaves the strictly worse :160 floodable at 4x with no precondition**, which is the project&#x27;s recorded &#x27;grep for its siblings&#x27; failure. SCOPE RULING SOUGHT: the round is the FILE, not the line -- six sites, one design, one detector. *** WHY #337 MISSED IT, AND IT IS EXPLICABLE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: *** that round&#x27;s scope was &quot;each replaced `exit(1)`&quot;, and `:583` was always a `fatal()`, never an `exit(1)`, so it fell outside the enumeration. *** THE OPTION-SET PREMISE WAS REFUTED, AND IT WAS MINE. *** The three-membered option set rule describes member 3 as &quot;announces the first N occurrences and then goes quiet&quot;. That describes `memory_rw.c:495-523` -- which is the tree&#x27;s ONLY counter-style bound, is DOUBLE-GATED so the counter never increments at default verbosity (`&amp;&amp;` short-circuits), is GLOBAL not per-instance, and prints no give-up notice. The house idiom is different and mature: **N=1 PER-INSTANCE LATCH plus an explicit &quot;(further occurrences suppressed)&quot; in the message text**, at roughly 34 sites across 11 device files. `dev_wdc.c` itself holds FOUR (`warned_badlen`, `warned_atapi_err`, `warned_atapi_dataout`, struct at :75-77) and is the tree&#x27;s REFERENCE implementation -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:147 says its own latch is &quot;Modelled on dev_wdc.c&#x27;s warned_badlen (#337)&quot;. *** AND `fatal()` SHOULD BE KEPT, ON A RECORDED HOUSE WARRANT: *** dev_asc.c:250-257 states that under `-q` verbosity settles at VERBOSITY_ERROR, so a WARNING-level debugmsg would be INVISIBLE to the boot-log hygiene grep and an ERROR-level one would trip #261&#x27;s break-on-ERROR. The chronology runs one way -- #245 chose verbosity-gating; #265/#269, #337, #460, #461 all chose latch + fatal(). TWO HAZARDS FOR THE DIFF, both from the seat: (1) SKETCH RULE -- DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc) has a common tail at :1019-1030 (`ret:` -&gt; memory_writemax64 -&gt; return 1), so any decline at the ACCESS level must `goto ret`; `wdc_command()` itself is void and called at :1003, so returning inside IT is safe. (2) CLAUSE (d) -- the guard would read the new `warned_*` field while the body reads `d-&gt;cur_command`, so the detector owes a state where a SECOND, DIFFERENT unimplemented command arrives after the latch has set; the 0x5a-vs-0xaa control is the seed for that row. OPEN QUESTION THE SEAT DECLINED TO SETTLE, recorded as open rather than guessed: #337 notes its two warned_badlen sites SHARE one flag, while #460&#x27;s gdrom comment records a MEASURED warrant that per-site bits beat a shared bit when two sites run in one session. For pure flood control a shared latch is strictly better; the tradeoff is information loss, and which matters here is unmeasured. PRIVILEGE, stated exactly: no device-layer check exists and memory_rw dispatches on physical address, so &quot;unprivileged&quot; overstates it -- the exact claim is &quot;any code that can reach the I/O window, with no emulator-side gate&quot;.  *** OWNER SCOPE DECISION 2026-08-25: THE ROUND IS THE WHOLE FILE -- ALL SIX UNLATCHED SITES, ONE DESIGN, ONE DETECTOR. *** Asked to choose between the filed single site, the two measured floods, and the file, the owner took the file. So the round covers :145, :160, :583, :607, :704 and :1025 using dev_wdc.c&#x27;s own #337 idiom (per-instance latch + &quot;(further occurrences suppressed)&quot;), keeping `fatal()` on the dev_asc.c:250-257 warrant and keeping the existing `d-&gt;error |= WDCE_ABRT`. The reopening rule permits it: same device, same semantic class, one fix design, one detector, no new oracle. **Only :160 and :583 carry a rung-3 witness today; the other four are joined on the SAME-CLASS clause and each should get a one-store witness in pass 1, per #337&#x27;s measured partial-file miss.**  *** PASS 1 (panel_20260825_195526): agy SETTLED Q1 WITH AN ARGUMENT NOBODY HAD MADE, AND IT DISSOLVES THE EVIDENCE CONFLICT RATHER THAN PICKING A SIDE. *** The brief posed shared-vs-per-site latching as #337 (shares `warned_badlen` across two sites) versus #460 (measured warrant for per-site bits). **Neither is wrong: #337&#x27;s two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH.** VERIFIED BY READING: dev_wdc.c:709 is `if (cpu-&gt;byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN) {` containing :731, and :739 is its `} else {` containing :761. A CPU does not flip endianness mid-session, so those two are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE AT RUNTIME and sharing a flag loses exactly zero information. #460&#x27;s warrant governs sites that can BOTH fire, which is all six here. **DESIGN SETTLED: SIX separate per-instance latches** (or one bitfield with six masks), never shared across read/write or overrun/over-read pairs. *** Q2 SETTLED: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** Census verified independently by agy against all of `src/devices/`: only dev_asc.c (3 sites) and dev_vga.c (1) carry it, and dev_wdc.c&#x27;s own four #337 sites do not. Adding it to six new sites would make the file internally inconsistent; editing the four existing ones is unjustified churn into working code. *** Q3 ANSWERED, AND IT CARRIES AN IMPLEMENTATION TRAP THE NAIVE FIX WALKS INTO. *** `:160` is a GUEST error and is safe to latch -- real hardware returns open bus on a read past the transfer, and the emulator returns `(uint64_t) -1`. **BUT the `return (uint64_t) -1;` MUST STAY OUTSIDE the latch guard.** Nesting it inside `if (!d-&gt;warned_...)` makes the SECOND over-read fall through to `d-&gt;inbuf_tail = (d-&gt;inbuf_tail + 1) % WDC_INBUF_SIZE;` and CORRUPT THE RING POINTER -- the fix would introduce a defect worse than the flood it removes. Same shape at `:1025`, inside the common tail: guard the `fatal()` only; `data[0] = odata;` and `return 1;` run regardless. *** Q5: agy CAUGHT AN INTERNAL CONTRADICTION IN MY OWN BRIEF. *** The proposed design specifies scalar `warned_*` fields while Q5 demanded a detector catch a per-device-versus-per-opcode mutant -- which would require a 256-bit bitmap (`uint32_t warned_unimpl_cmd[8]`). Resolution: the scalar per-site latch is correct (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines), and **the TRUE clause-(d) mutant is a different one: scope `d-&gt;error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch guard at :583.** Then the second unimplemented command silently SUCCEEDS. A detector that counts only host log lines passes it clean. That is a better (d) mutant than the one the brief proposed and it is the round&#x27;s headline detector obligation. TWO FURTHER EVASIONS from the same seat: nesting the `return -1` at :160 (above), and using `static int warned` instead of a per-instance field, which silences a secondary IDE controller at 0x170 because the primary at 0x1f0 already warned. *** Q7 WITNESS COSTS, one of which is a genuine obstacle: *** `:607` = a 16-bit write to wd_seccnt; `:1025` = a 16-bit read from wd_status; `:704` = a store to wd_data with no active command -- all cheap. **`:145` is NOT cheap: WDC_MAX_SECTORS is 512 and WDC_INBUF_SIZE is `512*(512+1)` = 262,656 bytes (verified by reading dev_wdc.c:47-48), so filling the ring by guest stores costs ~65,664 32-bit stores.** The disk-read fill path at :343 moves 512 bytes per sector and would be far cheaper, but it needs an attached image -- i.e. it needs `landiskdisk` first. ALL THREE of agy&#x27;s checkable claims were VERIFIED against source rather than credited: the endianness branch, the buffer arithmetic, and that wdc_get_inbuf() is called once per BYTE inside 16/32-bit transfers (:616, :620, :622, :623 little-endian; :627, :629, :630 big-endian), which is exactly the measured 4 lines per 32-bit guest load.  *** FULL PASS-1 PANEL COMPLETE (panel_20260825_195526): 8 SEATS ANSWERED. *** codex 501 KB, grok 19.5 KB, minimax 13.6 KB, agy 13.2 KB, kimi-k3:cloud 10.8 KB (the SUBSTITUTE transport, fired by hand -- the CLI is 403-walled), glm 4.4 KB, deepseek 4.1 KB. The kimi CLI is the only seat failure and it is a transport wall, not a seat outage. *** Q2 IS UNANIMOUS ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT ANSWERED IT: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** *** Q1 SPLIT 3-1-2, AND THE SPLIT IS DECIDED BY EVIDENCE RATHER THAN COUNT. *** SIX separate latches: agy, grok, kimi. FIVE plus a per-opcode set for :583: deepseek. FOUR (pairing): glm, minimax. **The two pairing seats both cite #337&#x27;s shared `warned_badlen` as the licence -- and that precedent is measurably not a licence.** agy and grok independently derived that #337&#x27;s two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH (:709 `if (cpu-&gt;byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN)`, :739 its `} else {`), so they are mutually exclusive at runtime and sharing loses nothing. grok put it exactly: *&quot;two endian copies of one check, not a licence to pair different events.&quot;* kimi added an independent refutation from THIS ROUND&#x27;S OWN WITNESS: the :160 over-read fired with NO DISK ATTACHED, so no overrun could have occurred -- **the two ring halves are causally independent, which is precisely what the pairing assumes they are not.** DESIGN SETTLED: six per-instance flags, wrap ONLY the `fatal()`, leave every guest-visible side effect outside the guard. *** Q3: FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY LOCATE THE REAL DEFECT AT :145, NOT :160 -- see `wdcringambig`, filed. *** glm: *&quot;:145 is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun.&quot;* minimax: *&quot;:145 IS a real defect that the latch does not fix -- it corrupts the ring and continues, so a TODO is owed there.&quot;* kimi located it first and most precisely. grok: *&quot;:145 is the unfixed pckbc/asc wrap, not a twin of the measured :160 flood.&quot;* Only agy said :160 was the site to worry about, and it was answering the question as the brief framed it. *** Q5 WAS CONFUSED IN THE BRIEF AND THREE SEATS SAID SO. *** grok: *&quot;Q5&#x27;s per-opcode (d) mutant contradicts the per-instance design the brief actually proposes -- that mutant is a spec change, not a floor test.&quot;* minimax and agy said the same. deepseek dissents usefully in the other direction: :583&#x27;s diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so a single flag hides distinct guest actions and a 256-bit set would preserve it. **Disposition: per-instance scalar for the fix (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines); deepseek&#x27;s per-opcode proposal is a SPEC IMPROVEMENT and is filed, not folded in.** The real (d) mutants, from three seats: scope `d-&gt;error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch at :583 (agy -- the second unknown command then silently SUCCEEDS); nest the `return (uint64_t) -1;` inside the guard at :160 (agy -- corrupts inbuf_tail); and **reuse a GUEST-RESETTABLE field such as `d-&gt;seccnt` as the latch (glm)** -- the sharpest of the three, because the guard then reads a field the guest controls. *** THE :145 WITNESS IS NOT A ONE-STORE WITNESS, AND codex SUPPLIED THE TWO HONEST ROUTES: *** prime `head = WDC_INBUF_SIZE - 1, tail = 0` in the detector and call the producer once; or, for a real guest-path measurement, attach an image and issue **513 undrained IDENTIFY commands** -- each adds 512 bytes through `wdc_addtoinbuf`, and the final byte of the 513th reaches the 262,656-byte wrap. The second route needs `landiskdisk`. *** AND A DETECTOR RULE FROM codex THAT APPLIES TO EVERY ROW: each site row must FIRE THE SITE TWICE. &quot;A one-off first warning proves announcement, not suppression.&quot; *** A row that triggers a site once cannot distinguish a working latch from no latch at all -- which is this project&#x27;s vacuity floor, stated for the latch class specifically.  *** A WRONG RECORD IN THIS ROW, WRITTEN BY ME AND CAUGHT BY THE ADJUDICATOR. CORRECTED 2026-08-25. *** Earlier in this note minimax is attributed the position that :145 is a real defect the latch does not fix, and is COUNTED AMONG SEATS SAYING DO-NOT-LATCH. **That count is FALSE.** minimax&#x27;s Q3 says verbatim: **&quot;So: latch both.&quot;** Its position is latch-and-mark, which is the disposition this round adopted -- it was recorded as dissenting from a ruling it agreed with. *** THE DON&#x27;T-LATCH CAMP IS glm ALONE *** (&quot;`:145` is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun&quot;), which turns a 2-of-8 reading into 1-of-8. **AND glm&#x27;s OWN FALLBACK SILENCES HARDER THAN THE LATCH IT OBJECTS TO:** it proposes &quot;downgrade it to `debug()`&quot;, and `debug()` has the early-out at `core/debugmsg.c:375` -- free-running at `verbose == 0` gives `v &lt; 0`, so it vanishes ENTIRELY in production, while a latched `fatal()` still announces the first occurrence and cannot be silenced by `-q`. The dissent&#x27;s remedy is strictly worse for the property the dissent defends. **Weigh deepseek&#x27;s Q5 dissent knowing its ring analysis was the panel&#x27;s weakest:** its &quot;:145 is a guest error -- the guest wrote more data than the ring can hold&quot; is factually off. The EMULATOR writes that ring on the guest&#x27;s behalf; the defect is state representation. *Second time in two days that a seat-count in this project&#x27;s records was wrong in the direction flattering the writer&#x27;s own ruling. The tally was never the argument -- but a misquoted seat is a wrong record whichever way it points.*  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25 (fable5, main-loop hat, on 8 of 9 mandated seats -- PROVISIONAL on its face w.r.t. the absent ninth, per that seat&#x27;s own rule). *** **(1) Q5 UPHELD, two sharpenings.** Scalar per-device flag at :583 this round; deepseek&#x27;s per-opcode set FILED. Per-opcode-versus-per-device is a SPEC choice, and deepseek&#x27;s framing (&quot;the scalar IS the (d) mutant&quot;) is circular -- it assumes per-opcode is the baseline and then calls the alternative a weakening. Clause (d) tests the CHOSEN spec&#x27;s repair; it does not adjudicate between two acceptable specs. Put per-device in the CONTRACT (clause e) -- &quot;does not claim per-opcode uniqueness at :583&quot; -- so a later alternating-opcode observation is OUTSIDE the contract rather than a floor breach. **SHARPENING 1, the best single point of the panel: the two-different-opcodes row SHIPS ANYWAY, with the expectation INVERTED -- ONE LINE TOTAL.** Same stimulus deepseek wanted, opposite oracle. It kills a last-warned-opcode implementation (under which an alternating 0x5a/0xaa guest floods forever) AND pins the per-device spec in one row. **SHARPENING 2: the filed item inherits a TWO-membered framing and must not.** Honest weakening of my own precedent: #337&#x27;s latched messages at :816 and :855 carry NO VALUE PAYLOAD -- :583 is the only site whose message names an opcode -- so &quot;three latches in this file&quot; is weaker precedent for discarding value-resolution than it looks. The filed option set is THREE-membered: scalar (shipped), per-opcode set (deepseek), and the in-tree COUNT-BOUND idiom (`n_trunc_msgs`, `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523`) -- which this row already banners and which nobody in the dispute named. **(2) `wdcringambig` DOES NOT BLOCK.** The latch bounds the announcement to first occurrence; it does not silence it. **The corrupting store is ALREADY SILENT TODAY** -- :141-146 stores, advances, then tests, so the wrap line fires once per 262,656-byte lap and the N+1th store that destroys data prints nothing. What the latch removes is repetition of a late, miscorrelated warning. THREE OBLIGATIONS make it honest, all in-round and cheap: (a) /* #NNN */ comments at :145 and :160 naming `wdcringambig` -- &quot;latch bounds the announcement; the ring invariant is untouched&quot;; (b) the detector CONTRACT disclaims ring health, and the :145 primed-state row is marked for re-pin under the oracle-correction rule when `wdcringambig` lands; (c) file glm&#x27;s dissent VERBATIM into `wdcringambig` as provenance. **THE TEMPTING THIRD PATH WAS CHECKED AND REJECTED FOR A MEASURED REASON.** grok and kimi both prefer restructuring :145 to the pckbc #288 shape (check-before-store, drop, latch -- verified in-tree at `dev_pckbc.c:158-167`). It must NOT fold in, and not merely by the reopening rule: **the restructure makes head==tail &lt;=&gt; empty sound ONLY on the byte path.** The bulk producer at :343-349 compares only against WDC_INBUF_SIZE and can land head exactly on tail with NO WARNING AT ALL (:876-879 mirrors it on tail). *pckbc had ONE producer; wdc has THREE.* A byte-path-only repair ships a half-invariant under a record that reads as soundness. **(3) SEQUENCING: `landiskdisk` SPLITS IN TWO.** The PROBE-level -d attach is part of `wdcflood`&#x27;s own witness battery and blocks nothing -- verified: :583 is unreachable without a disk (:461-468 early-returns), so the opus flood measurement necessarily ran disk-attached and the route is already proven. The BATTERY-RIG integration -- the half carrying the 28-34% NINSTRS poison -- is an independent deliverable and NOT a blocker. **The primed-head/tail row is a RUNG-1 DETECTOR row and cannot discharge :145&#x27;s witness**; the 513-IDENTIFY route can, and was verified sound: no ring reset exists at command start (the only head/tail writes are :143, :165, :347-349, :876-879), IDENTIFY feeds wdc_addtoinbuf 512 bytes per command (:525-533), and 513 x 512 = 262,656 = WDC_INBUF_SIZE exactly. **(4) THE REPETITION RULE: DO NOT MINT, and my dichotomy was wrong on BOTH horns.** Clause (a) does NOT cover it -- (a) is DETECTOR-level and the defect is ROW-level: a one-off row at a latched site prints one line pre-fix and one line post-fix, colourless in both worlds, so it contributes nothing to (a) and hides behind rows that do. **But no new clause is needed, because clause (d) already forces it mechanically:** the fix adds six latch predicates, (d)-DEPENDS-ON mandates a null-repair mutant per predicate (flag never set), and the only row shape that reddens a nulled latch is one firing the same site twice with an EXACT count. Codex&#x27;s sentence is the row-construction corollary of (d) for the latch class -- the same relation the accept-row failability rule bears to the de-escalation clause. Two overlapping statements of one rule is what the carrier refuses by name. **Disposition: one derivation sentence in the round block, not a carrier clause.** If a future latch round ships a SURVIVING one-off row, that measured escape is the (f)-style warrant to mint. **REFINEMENT ON THE ROWS (minimax): repetition with a &quot;&lt;= 1 line&quot; oracle passes a mutant that prints ZERO. The oracle must be EXACTLY ONE, THEN ZERO** -- announcement AND suppression, which is the pair a latch actually promises.  *** PASS 2 (panel_20260825_204943) FOUND THREE ESCAPES AGAINST A DETECTOR MEASURED 14/14, AND ONE OF THEM IS A STRUCTURAL BLINDNESS OF THE ROWS RATHER THAN A GAP IN THEM. *** All three are L1 -- pre-ship construction, fixed in-round, not filed. **(1) glm -- THE RESET-ON-NORMAL-OPERATION ESCAPE, and it is the sharpest.** ONE line, reusing the existing flag, guard and branch, added inside an existing case for a VALID command: `d-&gt;warned_unimpl_cmd = 0;`. **Every site row fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION with no normal device operation between**, so the flag is never reset between fires and the row stays green -- while a real guest that probes an unimplemented opcode, issues IDENTIFY, probes again, issues IDENTIFY, floods forever. The same one-line trick applies at every one of the six sites. *This is the `#463` distinctness-invariance finding wearing new clothes: rows that test a property back-to-back are invariant under any edit that only changes what happens BETWEEN the two fires.* **(2) agy -- THE COVERAGE CENSUS, CONFIRMED AGAINST MY OWN PROBE.** &quot;The detector&#x27;s six site rows do not test six distinct C sites&quot;: `:145` has **ZERO** rows while its sibling `:160` has two, and `:583` has three. Also **the non-byte rows probe 16-bit ONLY** -- `mov.w` at both :607 and :1025, never `mov.l`, so a `len != 1` guard narrowed to `len == 2` would escape. **(3) deepseek -- `d-&gt;warned_X = !d-&gt;warned_X;`**, claimed to toggle so that fires 1 and 3 print while the detector only fires twice. **READING SAYS THIS DOES NOT WORK** -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard (`if (!d-&gt;warned_unimpl_cmd) { d-&gt;warned_unimpl_cmd = 1; ...}`), so fire 2 never enters and the toggle never runs. **RECORDED AS A PREDICTION TO BE MEASURED, NOT AS REFUTED** -- this project settles disagreements by measurement, and the seat may be modelling a placement I have not considered. It goes in the second table either way, because the row that would kill it kills a whole CLASS. *** THE ROWS OWED, AND THE THIRD IS THE GENERAL ONE: *** `interleave` -- fire the site, issue a VALID command (0xEC IDENTIFY), fire again, demand ONE line; this is what kills glm&#x27;s escape. `w32`/`r32` -- 32-bit non-byte access at :607 and :1025, closing agy&#x27;s width gap. **`fire3` -- fire ONE site THREE times and demand exactly one line**, which kills the toggle class and any period-N latch at once, and is the cheapest row of the three. `:145` remains DISCLOSED-NOT-COVERED in the contract; agy&#x27;s point stands but the contract already says so. *** AND AN HONEST RECORD ABOUT PROCESS: THE CARRIER&#x27;S PRE-TABLE CHECK WOULD HAVE CAUGHT agy&#x27;s FINDING AND I DID NOT RUN IT. *** Its FIRST item is SIBLING PARITY -- *for each arm the diff touches, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm; an asymmetry is a row you owe*. `:145` with zero rows beside `:160` with two IS that asymmetry, visible by counting, before any mutant was built. The check exists precisely because a table re-run is expensive, and this round is now paying that cost: the first table was measured against a 14-row detector and adding rows changes other mutants&#x27; scores, so a SECOND table is owed. **The check costs three greps and about thirty minutes; skipping it cost a table.**  *** THE FULL PASS-2 PANEL (8 of 8 answering; the kimi CLI walled and COVERED BY ITS SUBSTITUTE -- panel.sh reported that correctly for the first time) FOUND AN L2 FLOOR BREACH, TWO WRONG RECORDS IN SHIPPED TEXT, AND A DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW BEHIND IT. *** **(L2 -- FLOOR BREACH, clause (b), grok) `:145` HAS NO DETECTOR ROW, SO THE FIX HAS SIX FIXED SITES AND THE DETECTOR SEES FIVE.** Clause (b) requires failure on a verbatim reinstatement at EVERY fixed site; reinstating `:145`&#x27;s unlatched form escapes clean. grok&#x27;s verdict was DO-NOT-SHIP-AS-A-SIX-SITE-FLOOR and it is right. **Confirmed by counting my own probe: the six site-x2 rows cover FIVE sites -- `P_OVERRUN` appears only in `disjoint`, which fires an unimplemented command and never touches the ring.** The 8/14 -&gt; 14/14 colour change is five tested sites plus `unimpl 2op`. Fix in-round, however late: either add a `:145` row or narrow the fix to five sites. **(L4 -- WRONG RECORD IN SHIPPED PROBE TEXT, codex and grok independently) THE HEADER CONTRADICTS ITS OWN CONTRACT.** It claims to catch *removal of any of the six per-instance latch guards* while the contract eight lines below excludes `:145`. It catches FIVE unique sites. **(L4 -- `disjoint` PROVES LESS THAN ITS NAME, codex.** It exercises only the unimplemented-command transcript, so it proves THAT stimulus matches one regex -- not that all six patterns map uniquely. The row&#x27;s note overstates it. *** (DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW, codex -- and this is the one I would have missed for good.) 14/14 DOES NOT ENFORCE SIX INDEPENDENT FLAGS. *** Every site test starts a NEW emulator (15 `session()` calls), so **pairing the read/write width flags, or the two ring flags, would pass every isolated row -- as would a PROCESS-GLOBAL latch**, unless one session exercises two controller instances. The round&#x27;s headline design decision -- six flags, not four -- is therefore UNMEASURED by its own detector. codex adds the mitigating half, checked: the implementation does use six separately initialised per-instance fields, `DEVINIT(wdc)` zeroes the whole allocation, and none is reset or aliased later. **So the CODE is right and the DETECTOR cannot prove it** -- which under clause (e) must be disclosed, not claimed. **(ESCAPE, codex) An inserted `return 1;` immediately after the non-byte-read `fatal()`** leaves the FIRST 16-bit read&#x27;s buffer untouched while the second runs normally -- and `r16 x2` checks only the line count. Needs a VALUE oracle, not a count. **(ESCAPE, ollama_kimi) `idata = data[0]` at the write-width site is a FOURTH guest-visible side effect** and has no pin row; my Q2 list named three. *** THE EPOCH-LATCH QUESTION (ollama_kimi and minimax, independently) IS ANSWERED AND THE ANSWER IS NO. *** They asked whether a guest-reachable device reset re-zeroes the `warned_*` flags, which would make the shipped code an epoch latch no row can see. Checked by reading: the only `memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct wdc_data))` is at :1126, immediately after the malloc at :1125 in DEVINIT. `COMMAND_RESET` (:484) and `ATAPI_SOFT_RESET` (:601) exist and neither re-zeroes the struct. **A real question, cheaply settled, and worth recording as a negative result so nobody re-asks it.** **(RECORDS, minimax) MY OWN BRIEF&#x27;S ROW ARITHMETIC DID NOT ADD UP:** it enumerated 2 controls + 6 site + 3 accept + 1 disjoint = 12 against a stated 14. The two unstated rows are the side-effect rows `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` -- which are the most interesting rows in the file and the brief left them out of its own census. **(CHALLENGE, minimax) `fatal()` RETURNING IS ASSUMED IN THE :1025 COMMENT, AND &quot;no early-out&quot; DOES NOT ESTABLISH IT** -- no-early-out is about verbosity, not program lifetime. The claim is true and this project settled it on 2026-08-21 by reading `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, but the comment should CITE that rather than lean on the adjacent no-early-out fact. A fair catch. **(USEFUL FOR `wdcringambig`, ollama_kimi) A WASTE-ONE-SLOT DISAMBIGUATOR IS NOT FORBIDDEN BY CAPACITY:** 512 x 512 = 262,144 usable is still &lt;= 262,655, so a max-length sector transfer still fits. Only the 513-undrained-IDENTIFY fill exceeds it. **Any future claim that &quot;waste-one breaks the exact-fit sizing&quot; is wrong**, and that is worth carrying to the ring round before it invents the objection.  *** KILL TABLE 1 -- 8 MUTANTS AGAINST THE 14-ROW DETECTOR, AND ONE ESCAPED. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table1_14rows/` (12 files + a README stating what the numbers mean and why the directory is stamped). Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. SCORES, every reddened row re-extracted with an ANCHORED trailing-`FAIL` match: `base` 14/14 (identity -- the harness scores the unmutated copy exactly as SEC does); `Mb1` 12/14 delete the :583 guard -&gt; `unimpl x2`, `unimpl 2op`; `M1` 12/14 null the repair, flag never set -&gt; same two; `M2` 11/14 SET BEFORE ANNOUNCE, zero lines -&gt; those two plus `disjoint`; `M3` 13/14 last-warned-opcode -&gt; **`unimpl 2op` ALONE**; `M4` 13/14 ABRT moved inside the guard -&gt; **`abrt 2nd` ALONE**; `M5` 13/14 `return -1` moved inside the guard -&gt; **`overread -1` ALONE**; `M6` **14/14 -- ESCAPED, ZERO ROWS REDDENED**. *** THE THREE SINGLE-ROW KILLS ARE THE USEFUL RESULT, NOT THE SCORES. *** `M3`, `M4` and `M5` each redden exactly ONE row, and those three rows exist for exactly those escapes -- nothing else in the file covers them. `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` are the two rows that read a guest-visible SIDE EFFECT rather than a line count, and they are the only rows that can see a repair swallowed by a guard. *** M2 IS THE MEASURED VINDICATION OF AN ORACLE CHOICE. *** It sets the flag before the test so the site emits ZERO lines. **A `&lt;= 1 line` oracle would have passed it**; `exactly 1` catches it. That choice was argued from a seat&#x27;s reasoning and is now measured. *** AND M6 IS THE ESCAPE, PREDICTED BY A SEAT ON PASS 2 AND CONFIRMED HERE. *** It swaps the latch operand for the GUEST-RESETTABLE `d-&gt;seccnt`: `if (len != 1 &amp;&amp; !d-&gt;seccnt) { d-&gt;seccnt = 1; fatal(...); }`. Every row in table 1 fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION, so nothing between the fires ever puts the operand back and the mutant looks perfectly latched. A guest that writes `seccnt = 0` between wide writes floods without bound. **This is a STRUCTURAL blindness of the row design, not a missing mutant** -- the same shape as `#463`&#x27;s distinctness invariance, where rows that assert a property back-to-back cannot see an edit that changes only what happens BETWEEN the assertions. *** DETECTOR NOW 22 ROWS, MEASURED 22/22 ON THE FIXED BUILD (`table2_22rows/base_22rows.raw`). *** Seven rows added, each closing something table 1 or pass 2 exposed: `overrun x2` (the :145 row -- **this is what closes the L2 floor breach**, and it needed a hardware loop, `dt r3` / `bf` with a computed displacement, because 513 undrained IDENTIFYs unrolled would be 513 debugger commands); `interleave` (fire, VALID command, fire); `w16 reset` (fire, guest CLEARS the operand, fire -- the M6 killer); `fire3` (kills period-N latches, not just period-2); `w32 x2` and `r32 x2` (the width rows probed 16-bit only, so a guard narrowed to `len == 2` escaped); `r16 value` and `w16 value` (value oracles at the two width sites, for a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- which a count-only row cannot see). Row hygiene checked: no name over 13 chars, no duplicates, no name that is a prefix of another followed by a space. TABLE 2 is running against the 22-row detector with three mutants table 1 could not have had: `Mb2` removes the :145 guard verbatim (the clause-(b) reinstatement at the site that had no row), `M7` is the reset-on-normal-operation escape, `M8` is the period-2 latch. All three anchors were verified to occur EXACTLY ONCE in the source first -- **a mutant that fails to apply scores a false clean**, which is the worst possible failure for a kill table. *** A CAPTION DEFECT IN THE APPARATUS, FOUND AND FIXED AT SOURCE. *** `killwdc.sh` wrote its header at start with the row count and the pre-fix score as LITERALS (`14 rows`, `8/14`). The moment pass 2 forced seven more rows, table 2&#x27;s own header became wrong about its own size. The script now DERIVES the count (`grep -c &#x27;^row(&#x27; ...`) and stamps a UTC start time instead of quoting scores it cannot know. Table 2&#x27;s already-written header is stale and is corrected in place; the script fix stops it recurring. *A table that misreports its own size cannot be cited safely at all.*  *** KILL TABLE 2 IS CLEAN: 22-ROW DETECTOR, TEN MUTANTS, **ZERO ESCAPES**, EVERY LOG INTERNALLY CONSISTENT. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table2_22rows/` with a header that DERIVES its row count rather than quoting one. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. base 22/22; Mb1 18/22; M1 18/22; M2 17/22; M3 21/22; M4 21/22; M5 21/22; M6 21/22; M7 21/22; M8 21/22; Mb2 21/22. **Consistency check on all eleven logs: score + anchored-red count == 22 in every one**, which is the check that would catch a contaminated extractor. *** SIX ESCAPES, SIX SINGLE-ROW KILLS, EACH BY THE ROW BUILT FOR IT -- and that one-to-one correspondence is the result, not the scores: *** M3 last-warned-opcode -&gt; `unimpl 2op` ALONE; M4 ABRT inside the guard -&gt; `abrt 2nd` ALONE; M5 `return -1` inside the guard -&gt; `overread -1` ALONE; M6 guest-resettable operand -&gt; `w16 reset` ALONE; M7 reset-on-normal-operation -&gt; `interleave` ALONE; Mb2 the :145 verbatim reinstatement -&gt; `overrun x2` ALONE. **Nothing else in the file sees any of them.** *** THE L2 FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT. *** `Mb2` is the exact edit that swept CLEAN against the 14-row detector -- removing :145&#x27;s guard verbatim -- and it now reddens `overrun x2`, the row that did not exist when the breach was found. Clause (b) holds at all six fixed sites. *** M8 VINDICATES A CAUTION, AND THE CAUTION IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. *** deepseek proposed `d-&gt;warned_X = !d-&gt;warned_X;` as a period-2 escape. I read its SPECIFIC edit as unworkable -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard, so fire 2 never reaches it -- and recorded it as **&quot;a prediction to be MEASURED, not refuted&quot;**. M8 constructs the same class differently (toggle BEFORE the guard) and escapes a two-fire oracle exactly as deepseek said the class would. **Wrong about the placement, RIGHT ABOUT THE CLASS.** Declaring it refuted on the strength of a correct reading of one construction would have lost a real escape. *A seat can be wrong about the mechanism and right about the family; the family is the part worth building a row for.* *** CLAUSE (c), THE NULL MEDIUM, IS NOW MEASURED AND IN ITS STRONGEST FORM: 0/22 ON BOTH. *** `nullmedium_absent.raw` (a binary that does not exist) and `nullmedium_notanemu.raw` (a real executable that is not an emulator) each score **0/22 -- every row fails, not merely some**. It had NOT been verified before; the question was raised against the round&#x27;s own readiness and answered in seconds. **AND THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED A DESIGN PROPERTY THAT WAS ACCIDENTAL RATHER THAN INTENDED:** the three accept rows expect ZERO diagnostics, so a naive implementation would PASS against silence -- zero is exactly what silence produces. They fail correctly only because `n()` returns `&quot;died&quot;` rather than `&quot;0&quot;` when the run produced nothing (probe :227-228). **That single line is what stops every accept row in this file from being vacuous under clause (c)**, and it was not written for that reason. Worth carrying to any future detector with absence-shaped oracles: an &quot;expect 0&quot; row MUST distinguish &#x27;ran and saw nothing&#x27; from &#x27;did not run&#x27;.  *** THE READINESS ADJUDICATION RETURNED **HOLD-FOR** AND FOUND TWO SHIPPED ROWS VACUOUS. ASKING BEFORE THE TABLE FINISHED IS WHAT MADE THAT POSSIBLE -- a clean sweep invites ratification. *** **`r16 value` WAS VACUOUS AS BUILT.** Its oracle was `printed(t,&quot;r2&quot;) != &quot;absent&quot;` against a wanted `&quot;read&quot;`, so it passed whenever the debugger echoed ANY hex value -- structurally incapable of failing while the emulator lived. **Confirmed: green under every one of table 2&#x27;s ten mutants -- it did not exist for table 1, which measured a 14-row detector, and an earlier note said &#x27;tables 1 and 2&#x27;, which is false.** The `live` control already covered session death, so the row measured nothing at all. **AND BOTH WIDTH VALUE ROWS CHECKED THE WRONG FIRE.** The escape they cite -- a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- corrupts the FIRST access (flag unset, guard entered, return skips the repair); the SECOND finds the flag set, skips the guard and repairs normally. Both rows read the value ONCE, after both fires, and so saw a perfectly good value. The contract line claiming they carried value oracles &quot;for exactly that edit&quot; was FALSE AS BUILT. Repaired to check BOTH fires, with a low-byte mask because the repair sets only `data[0]` and asserting the upper byte would make the row about the wrong thing. *** TABLE 3 EARNS THE REPAIRED ROWS THEIR MEASURED KILL, WHICH IS WHAT A REPAIRED ROW OWES: *** `table3_repaired/`, base 22/22. `Mv1` (delete `idata = data[0]`, the write-side repair) 18/22 -&gt; r16 value, w16 value, overrun x2, accept ec. `Mv2` (delete `data[0] = odata`, the read-side repair inside the `ret:` tail) 18/22 -&gt; devsig, abrt 2nd, r16 value, w16 value. **`Mv3` (codex&#x27;s EXACT edit -- insert `return 1;` after the latched READ `fatal()`) 21/22 -&gt; `r16 value` ALONE.** *** Mv3 IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE REPAIR RATHER THAN THE ROW. *** It corrupts only the first access, so **the ORIGINAL one-value form would have scored 22/22 against it** -- the escape would have shipped invisibly behind a row whose whole stated purpose was to catch it. One row in twenty-two sees it, and it is the repaired one. COLLATERAL WORTH READING RATHER THAN DISMISSING: `Mv2` reddens `devsig`, because deleting `data[0] = odata` breaks the status read the device-signature control depends on. That is the control doing its job -- a device that cannot answer through its own decode is exactly what `devsig` exists to notice -- not a spurious red. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. All three logs internally consistent (score + anchored reds == 22). *** THE STANDING LESSON, and it is the second time in one round: A REPAIRED ROW IS NOT A ROW UNTIL A MUTANT KILLS IT. *** Both rows looked correct after repair and one of them still could not have caught the edit it was written for. Reading a row and believing it is exactly the state the vacuity floor exists to refuse.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2232:  Round C  idesync alone             -- oracle: fsync ordering; crosses a module boundary into diskimage.c and cannot be witnessed above rung 2, since no guest in this tree issues the opcode.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2272:  Round C  idesync alone             -- oracle: fsync ordering; crosses a module boundary into diskimage.c and cannot be witnessed above rung 2, since no guest in this tree issues the opcode.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2429:*** WHAT ALL SIX MISSED, AND CODEX DID NOT: the latch is never CONSUMED. *** latched[] is cleared only by a non-latch mode write, never by the read, so after one latch the counter returns 0 indefinitely. Handed to the in-flight pitflip round because the consume semantics interact with the LSB/MSB flip-flop that round is fixing. It was my own Q4 in the brief and I did not check it before shipping. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260820_174300/</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — CLOSE (regress and review). The named defect is fixed and witnessed; the substantive hold -- the latch never consumed -- is discharged by af4884c with its kill table and the write-rewind mutant measured. R3 existing specifically to FAIL the design the panel majority recommended is the strongest detector-as-record form in this tree, and the C1 measurement is the settle-empirically rule and the seat-class doctrine working in one motion.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4sqwrite</span><span class="ttl">sh4sqwrite  The SQ WRITE half is never judged -- so an SQ page can raise no initial-page-write and no write-side protection fault, while the PREF still raises the read-side set</span><span class="tip">sh4sqwrite  The SQ WRITE half is never judged -- so an SQ page can raise no initial-page-write and no write-side protection fault, while the PREF still raises the read-side set — witness: none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable at rung 3 (a real guest store through real decode, observable in EXPEVT), but a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids. — note: *** SCOPE CORRECTION 2026-08-27: THE DEFECT IS THE **WRITE HALF ONLY**, and saying the window &#x27;bypasses the MMU ENTIRELY&#x27; reintroduces wording this row&#x27;s title was already corrected away from. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2504:*Filed as the strongest single finding of that pass; it needs a `sev` and a round of its own.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — The interim state sqreadtype deliberately ships, stated out loud so the next round is motivated by a named gap rather than rediscovered. Codex&#x27;s dissent that C1 is an &#x27;unshippable intermediate state&#x27; is recorded rather than resolved -- the round-scope rule decided it, not a majority. Smallest implementation and the licensing witness are both recorded in the bug file so the next round does not re-derive them.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** THIS ROW&#x27;S TITLE WAS A WRONG RECORD AND THE ROUND&#x27;S OWN DETECTOR DISPROVED IT. Corrected in place. *** It read &#x27;...after sqreadtype an SQ page raises no MMU fault of any kind&#x27;, which `sq miss evt` refutes by observing a read-type TLB miss (0x040) on the FIXED build -- and which is false under the charitable reading too, since user-mode PR=00/01 still takes a read-type protection violation. The claim was corrected in memory_sh.c:296-303 DURING #454 and survived in two more places, including this title, which flows into matrix.html twice. Mechanical rule 2&#x27;s corrected-in-one-place-survived-in-two, inside the round that celebrated a seven-site sibling sweep -- the sweep covered the OLD claim&#x27;s sites and nobody grepped the round&#x27;s own NEW claim. RANKING NOTE from the same seat, and it raises this row&#x27;s priority: #454 did not merely inherit a pre-existing conformance gap. Before it, the misplaced write-judgment at PREF was a FUNCTIONING, wrongly-placed tripwire -- a guest flushing onto a clean page got IPW (0x080), onto a PR=00 page got 0x0c0. #454 correctly deletes that, and the manual-correct enforcement point (the store half) does not exist, so a D-bit-tracking or write-protection-relying guest is observably WORSE OFF. That is the de-escalation clause&#x27;s exact hazard shape, and it means this row carries de-escalation weight rather than footnote weight.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4itlbpr</span><span class="ttl">sh4itlbpr  ITLB data array 1 is one bit too wide, and SIX shipped rows now REQUIRE the extra bit</span><span class="tip">sh4itlbpr  ITLB data array 1 is one bit too wide, and SIX shipped rows now REQUIRE the extra bit — witness: none -- ASSESSED 2026-08-27 and NOT CHANGED. A rung-3 probe is designable (privileged store/load at 0xf3000000, key on r2 &amp; 0x20) but is deliberately not built: the oracle-correction rule requires re-pinning six SHIPPED rows and measuring the committed build RED *before* any edit, which needs a build the machine embargo forbids. — note: *** FIRST EVIDENCE 2026-08-27, from a farmed codex job. The mask arithmetic is CONFIRMED exactly; the SEVERITY is lower than the seat implied, and the reason is a phrase in the manual the seat did not weigh. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2559:</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** THE ROW SAYS &#x27;NOT WITNESSED&#x27;, AND A WITNESS IS BOTH CONSTRUCTIBLE AND ADMISSIBLE. *** The carrier&#x27;s own rule is that &#x27;no witness of either kind&#x27; is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted -- dfreq&#x27;s was measured false by building one in a single session -- so this is that check, done by READING (flagged as a reading, not a measurement; the tree was busy with #461&#x27;s pre-fix run and a probe would have contended with it). THE OBSERVABLE ALREADY EXISTS: cpu_sh.c:168-173 registers `iprio_%x` as a readable setting for EVERY 0x20 slot in 0..0xfff, which includes `iprio_3e0` (IRQ15). The hardwired priority loop at cpu_sh.c:390 is `for (i = SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0; i &lt;= SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14; i += 0x20)`, so slot 0x3e0 is never written and keeps 0 while 0x3c0 (IRQ14) gets 1. Two `print` commands on an unmodified machine separate the two. AND RUNG 2 IS THE RIGHT RUNG FOR IT, not a concession: the ladder licenses machine construction as a reproduction for &#x27;defects in code construction itself executes (init-time)&#x27;, naming the macppc heap OOB #23 as the exemplar. This IS init-time -- the loop populates the priority table at CPU init and the defect is a slot it never reaches. The ladder&#x27;s warning about rung 2 is that registration proves presence not reachability, for ACCESS-PATH defects; this is not one. WHAT A RUNG-2 WITNESS WOULD NOT SETTLE, said plainly: it observes the priority TABLE, not delivery. It cannot show that an asserted line 15 is dropped -- for that the carrier&#x27;s free-running shape is required (sr=0x40000000, vbr into RAM, `bra .` after the trigger, breakpoint at VBR+0x600), and driving a PCIC line needs a PCI device landing on line 15, since dev_sh4.c:2951-2967 registers the 16 lines as `&lt;path&gt;.pcic.&lt;i&gt;` for bus_pci_init&#x27;s pciirq rather than as anything a guest store reaches directly. So the honest split is: the priority-0 STATE is witnessable at rung 2 today; the DROPPED-DELIVERY symptom is not, and the round should not claim it is. STALE CITATION CORRECTED: the row cites dev_sh4.c:2822-2836 for the wiring; that range is now the default arm&#x27;s fatal()/exit block. The wiring is at :2951-2967.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [filed by the MAIN LOOP wearing the fable5 model -- seat and adjudicator are distinct hats; the seats that found it are named in the text] dev_sh4.c:73 sets N_PCIC_IRQS 16 and :2822-2836 wires every one as SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0 + 0x20*i, so line 15 lands on 0x3e0 (SH4_INTEVT_IRQ15, sh4_exception.h:157). The priority loop at cpu_sh.c:352 stops at SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14 (0x3c0), so that slot keeps priority 0 for the machine&#x27;s life. *** EXTENDING THE BOUND FIXES NOTHING: the loop body is `15 - ((i - SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0) / 0x20)`, which yields priority 0 for IRQ15 -- exactly the state it is already in. The bound is not an off-by-one; it is where this formula runs out of nonzero priorities, so a round must decide what IRQ15&#x27;s priority OUGHT to be. *** #455 also changed the symptom class: before it, an assert on a priority-0 line ran fatal() then exit(1), so this was a guest-reachable host kill; after it the interrupt is SILENTLY DROPPED. That is the de-escalation clause&#x27;s own hazard arriving on schedule. HONEST LIMIT: header-and-loop-bound arithmetic across three files, NOT witnessed -- no probe has driven a PCIC line 15 assert.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4iprunpinned</span><span class="ttl">sh4iprunpinned  #458&#x27;s detector reads only four of the ~20 priority slots it maps, so deleting any single mapping line for an unread slot scores full marks</span><span class="tip">sh4iprunpinned  #458&#x27;s detector reads only four of the ~20 priority slots it maps, so deleting any single mapping line for an unread slot scores full marks — witness: none — note: 
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2613:**SEVERITY SET LOW, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: ASSESSED and not changed -- a bare latch with no reset value; the readback is 0x0000 and no consumer has been shown to care.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, and the MANUAL confirms the value is wrong. *** dev_sh4.c:2715-2720 is a bare latch -- `odata = d-&gt;cpg_frqcr` with no reset value written at init, so a guest reads back whatever DEVINIT&#x27;s memset left, i.e. 0x0000. THE MANUAL, READ LOCALLY rather than taken from a search summary: _scratchpad/sh7750_hardware.txt:18607-18608 reads &#x27;FRQCR is initialized only by a power-on reset via the RESET pin. The initial value of each bit is determined by the clock operating mode&#x27;, and the bit table immediately below at :18610-18614 gives initial values CKOEN=1 (bit 11), PLL1EN=1 (bit 10), PLL2EN=1 (bit 9) with bits 15-12 = 0. So the reset value carries at least 0x0E00 and is NEVER 0x0000. The register table at :18476 marks the reset column &#x27;Undefined*&#x27;; :18607 is the footnote that explains the asterisk, so &#x27;undefined&#x27; means MODE-DEPENDENT, not zero -- a distinction that would have been easy to read the wrong way from the table alone. A guest reading FRQCR therefore sees both PLLs disabled and clock output disabled, the opposite of the reset state. WITNESS: one 16-bit guest load from 0xffc00000 on an unmodified SH-4 machine. HONEST LIMIT, the same shape as sh4pcic15&#x27;s: the model has no clock-operating-mode concept, so a round must DECIDE what to present rather than read one value off the manual. The three bits above are mode-INDEPENDENT and settle the floor; IFC2 and the divider bits are not.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — dev_sh4.c&#x27;s `case SH4_CPG_FRQCR` models FRQCR as a plain store/load with no initialisation, so a guest reads 0x0000. SH7750 Hardware Manual Rev.7.00 Table 10.3 gives the legal initial values as H&#x27;0E1A / H&#x27;0E23 / H&#x27;0E13 / H&#x27;0E0A depending on clock mode. THIS IS THE HONEST LONG-RUN ANSWER TO sh4pclock: real hardware derives the peripheral clock from FRQCR and the mode pins, and a seat measured that the CPU:peripheral ratio in that table is only ever 4 or 6 -- calibrated against dreamcast, whose 200 MHz / 50 MHz = 4 is a legal mode this tree independently knows. A round here could make pclock DERIVED rather than assigned. Note the bound is not enough on its own: machine_hpcsh.c sets no emulated_hz at all, so the tree does not even claim a Jornada CPU clock to divide.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">hpcshcpu</span><span class="ttl">hpcshcpu  the repo contradicts itself about whether hpcsh is an SH-3 or an SH-4 machine, and neither claim is settleable in this tree</span><span class="tip">hpcshcpu  the repo contradicts itself about whether hpcsh is an SH-3 or an SH-4 machine, and neither claim is settleable in this tree — witness: none — note:   *** DISPOSITION 2026-08-24 (fable5 queue ranking): DROP FROM THE ACTIONABLE QUEUE, KEEP AS A RECORD. *** Two seats independently refuse to settle it and the row&#x27;s own entry says neither claim is settleable in this tree. **A defect that cannot be reproduced cannot be test-first&#x27;d, and a round that cannot be test-first&#x27;d is not a round** -- it is a document, which the witness ladder already says plainly (&#x27;no witness at rung 2-4 -&gt; document instead, stop&#x27;). Converted to: *assessed, cannot settle without an external SH3/SH4 identity source*. RE-OPEN ONLY if such documentation is obtained -- and that is not hypothetical, because obtaining the SH7750 manual on 2026-08-22 settled an SH question in the same session it was raised and refuted a finding this project was about to file.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — doc/TODO.html:322-323 lists &#x27;SH3-specific devices: Pretty much everything!&#x27; immediately followed by &#x27;NetBSD/evbsh3, hpcsh!&#x27;, grouping hpcsh with an SH3 target; machine_hpcsh.c:89 names SH7750, which is arch 4. TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REFUSED TO SETTLE THIS FROM MEMORY, which is the #357/#362/#365 citation failure NOT repeating -- there is no SH-3 manual in _scratchpad, hpcsh&#x27;s platid/bootinfo block is entirely commented out, HISTORY never mentions hpcsh, and vripreg.h (included by machine_hpcsh.c) is NEC VR41xx MIPS, a copy-paste leftover from hpcmips. IF IT IS SETTLED as SH-3, note what changes: arch-3 removes the WHOLE dev_sh4 block (CCN/MMU, INTC, TMU, RTC, BSC, DMAC, SCIF, TLB arrays, PCIC), both SH-3 interrupt handlers are /* TODO */ stubs (cpu_sh.c:527-533) that silently drop asserts, and cpu_sh.c:224 uses a leftover UTLB loop counter as templ.line -- latent today only because that handler is a stub. Obtain a source first; the SH7750 precedent shows that costs one download.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4pclockquiet</span><span class="ttl">sh4pclockquiet  five measured mutants of #459&#x27;s pclock guard all score 16/16, because the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001}</span><span class="tip">sh4pclockquiet  five measured mutants of #459&#x27;s pclock guard all score 16/16, because the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001} — witness: none — note: 
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2627:mutant outcome below is a static conclusion, not a fresh measurement.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — COVERAGE DEBT filed after #459&#x27;s capped ask-again. A measuring seat built six mutants and FIVE scored 16/16: m_unie (`&amp;&amp; !(idata &amp; TCR_UNIE)`) which lands a TCR with timer_hz == 0 for EXACTLY the value a guest must write to use a TMU at all, while going QUIETER than the fix; m_below_switch (guard moved below the debug line) giving 4 unlatched `clock set to 0.000000 Hz` announcements for 4 stores; m_valkey (key `idata ? idata : 1u`) giving 3 lines for 4 values; m_tmr2 (`&amp;&amp; timer_nr != 2`); and m_wrongnr (every line says &#x27;timer 0&#x27;). Only m_valnarrow died, 15/16, killed by `unlatch`. *** THE ROOT CAUSE IS ONE SENTENCE: the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001} -- the three kill rows and says/once/pertmr all write 0x0000 and unlatch writes TCR_TPSC_P16, so ANY narrowing predicate that admits both escapes. *** m_valnarrow&#x27;s death shows the remedy: a second distinct value. FOUR of the five closing rows cost ZERO extra sessions; only unlatch2 at TCR_UNIE|TCR_TPSC_P4 needs a new one, and it kills m_unie and the whole narrowing family.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdmawidth</span><span class="ttl">gdromdmawidth  the DMA tail store ignores `len`: an ALIGNED byte or 16-bit write to an implemented offset clobbers all 32 bits of the shadow register</span><span class="tip">gdromdmawidth  the DMA tail store ignores `len`: an ALIGNED byte or 16-bit write to an implemented offset clobbers all 32 bits of the shadow register — commits: 029a137 — witness: none committed -- MEASURED 2026-08-23 at rung 3 via a scratchpad probe (reported, not pinned): the row is DELIBERATELY not shipped, because pinning 0x000000ff would enshrine this model&#x27;s own behaviour as an oracle — note: *** A FARMED agy JOB RE-DERIVED THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-26, AND ITS RECOMMENDATION WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DEFECT. Recorded because the failure mode is instructive, not because the seat was careless. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:2647:as either a fix or a false finding.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window&#x27;s +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect&#x27;s actual content -- clobbering a value register&#x27;s upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief&#x27;s &#x27;SH-4 can run either endianness&#x27; is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega&#x27;s Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing&#x27;s own &#x27;real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits&#x27; was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match, but *** IT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE *** -- its warrant is &#x27;a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so the latch costs a correct guest nothing&#x27;, and that warrant FAILS here. Decided design recorded as B&#x27;: decline at the four VALUE registers only, keep +0x18 byte-capable. It also flagged that the row pinning the REAL contract is currently UNBUILDABLE -- any nonzero store at +0x18 hits one of sh4gdromexit&#x27;s filed exit(1) kills -- so this item is gated behind that round by probe-buildability rather than by ranking.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** WITNESS CONSTRUCTIBLE AT RUNG 3, contrary to `witness: none`. Verified by READING the arm; flagged as a reading, since a probe run would have contended with a live gate. *** The carrier&#x27;s rule is that &#x27;no witness of either kind&#x27; is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted. Checked: `len` appears exactly THREE times in DEVICE_ACCESS(dreamcast_gdrom_dma), and all three are byte marshalling -- memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len) on the way in and two memory_writemax64(..., len, ...) on the way out. The store itself, at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:889 (re-derived 2026-08-23), is `d-&gt;dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` -- a full 32-bit assignment with no width discrimination anywhere. THE WITNESS IS `dma seed` PLUS ONE STORE, exactly as the filing predicted: 32-bit store 0x11223344 at DMA+0x04, then a BYTE store of 0xff at DMA+0x04 (ALIGNED, so it matches `case 0x04` and is NOT the #461 alias), then read +0x04 back. Real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits; this model yields 0x000000ff. Ordinary guest instructions through real address decode on a committed machine -- rung 3, no new device and no machine edit, so no laundering. NO BUILD NEEDED EITHER: the defect is pre-existing upstream and untouched by #461, so it is in the current binary.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] Supplied the discriminating row and the comment amendment, both adopted. The row -- W32 +0x04 = 0x11223344, then W8 +0x04 = 0xff, then R32 +0x04 -- separates all three options: A gives 0x112233ff little-endian (0xff223344 big-endian), B gives 0x11223344 preserved, C gives 0x000000ff. Its amendment text is the one to use: it SEPARATES ADDRESS DECODING FROM WIDTH VALIDATION, so #461&#x27;s alignment rule cannot be misread as proof that implemented cases are width-safe.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] Found the OTHER in-tree precedent, independently: sh4_bsc16_report() at dev_sh4.c:1646, the tree&#x27;s existing handling for a narrow access to a wide register. Its conclusion: &#x27;in-tree precedent unequivocally supports Option B (decline partial/narrow writes with a latched warn-once diagnostic)&#x27;. Two seats finding two different precedents that agree is the strongest form this panel produces -- the answer was already in the tree twice and the brief invented a three-way choice that the tree had effectively made.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] *** FOUND THE DECISIVE PRECEDENT, AND IT IS IN THIS VERY FILE FROM THE PREVIOUS ROUND. *** dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515-522, written by #460: &#x27;DO NOT &quot;EMULATE&quot; NARROW ACCESS INSTEAD. A pass-1 panel split on that, and it was MEASURED ACTIVELY HARMFUL: letting cmd_count step by 1 makes eleven byte writes plus one word write go 11 -&gt; 13, SKIPPING the == 12 equality, so busy&amp;0x08 never clears and the next write reaches the &quot;Too much GDROM_DATA&quot; exit -- otherwise unreachable. It also writes cmd[12], one past the array.&#x27; VERIFIED BY READING. Option A is exactly that shape, ruled out by the file&#x27;s own measured history one round ago. Its reachability line is the right distinction too: &#x27;Reachable as a guest instruction; unobserved as a driver path. That is not Option C.&#x27; -- rung-3 reachability is established, driver reachability is not, and the ladder says a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE while severity orders it.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] Went furthest on the endianness question the brief called the real technical risk, working out what memory_readmax64 does to a narrow value and where a byte write at +0x04 lands in dma_reg[1]. Its answer is the reason Option A is unsafe to implement from the brief alone: the fact is derivable but was NOT established in the tree, and SH-4 runs either endianness.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] Option B with the clearest cost/benefit statement: one branch on `len != 4` before the tail store; consistent with #461&#x27;s decline-rather-than-corrupt philosophy; establishes the missing in-tree precedent for the WIDTH axis without committing to Option A&#x27;s endianness-dependent merge; and it &#x27;turns an unreachable silent-clobber into an unreachable LOUD no-op&#x27;. Explicitly ranked B over C: &#x27;B is C plus a guard for the same engineering cost.&#x27;</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] Reached codex&#x27;s discriminating row independently, with the same three expected values including the endianness split on Option A. Its amendment wording is the crisper of the two: &#x27;An aligned offset reaching the default arm is genuinely an unimplemented word. This does not imply that an aligned narrow access to an IMPLEMENTED offset is safe; narrow accesses to implemented registers are a separate width question and are currently not width-discriminated.&#x27;</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_215652] *** THE ONLY SEAT THAT REFUSED TO PRETEND THE REACHABILITY QUESTION WAS SETTLED, and it is right. *** &#x27;Probably not in well-behaved guest code, but the brief does not let us prove a negative.&#x27; It also caught a trap in the brief&#x27;s own framing: adding the witness row makes the defect reachable IN CI, which &#x27;is not the same as reachable&#x27; by a guest driver -- a distinction this project&#x27;s witness ladder makes and the brief blurred. Its answer is correctly CONDITIONAL rather than confident.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Pre-existing upstream, untouched by #461, found by the pass-2 measuring seat while building mutants against the alias fix. NOT joined: #461&#x27;s semantic class is &#x27;unimplemented offset&#x27;; this is &#x27;implemented offset, wrong width&#x27; -- a different fix design (read-modify-write honouring len, which raises an endianness question the default arm never has to answer) and a different oracle, so the round-scope rule makes it a separate round. *** IT IS THE SAME FAMILY AS THE ALIAS #461 DID FIX, AND #461&#x27;s NEW RULE DOES NOT COVER IT: *** &#x27;alignment alone separates unimplemented word from misaligned access to an implemented one&#x27; is true and says nothing about an ALIGNED NARROW write to an implemented offset. A future reader applying that rule here would conclude it is safe. Related to [hlen], but the OPPOSITE direction: hlen is a wide access spanning registers, this is a narrow access clobbering one. `dma seed` is one store away from being a witness.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">probetimebased</span><span class="ttl">probetimebased  pty probes with NO prompt detection: 15 live (11 grandfathered + 4 that arrived unnoticed), and gate_hygiene&#x27;s ratchet was pinned at 11</span><span class="tip">probetimebased  pty probes with NO prompt detection: 15 live (11 grandfathered + 4 that arrived unnoticed), and gate_hygiene&#x27;s ratchet was pinned at 11 — commits: abf665c — HOLD: full panel OWED. This row was REFRAMED 2026-08-24 from a speed defect to a SOUNDNESS defect by one seat (five rows score `survived` as their PASS value, and `alive` is true for a HUNG emulator). Recording that single finding is what made the stage gate expect a full panel -- the row had none before, because it had never been worked. HELD rather than dropping the finding or firing a sixth panel today: the finding is worth more than the row&#x27;s tidiness, and the sibling row probeslow just had its own nine-seat panel whose seats unanimously said to fold probeslow INTO this row. THE MERGE AND THE PANEL ARE ONE JOB; do them together rather than panelling a row that is about to absorb another. — witness: harness:RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 by replicating `gate_hygiene.sh`&#x27;s OWN computation rather than approximating it -- `_ptylist` (find, minus artifacts/, grep -l pty.fork, basenames), then `cd $HERE` and `xargs grep -L &#x27;endswith(&#x27;`.  Result: denominator 41, promptless **15**, grandfathered **11**.  `check &quot;readiness: promptless probes, grandfathered set only&quot; 15 11` is RED. — note:   *** THE FILED COUNT OF `FIVE ROWS` IS WRONG: IT IS NINE OF 57, AND SIX OF THE NINE PREDATE #462. Measured 2026-08-24 from the archived log `_scratchpad/kill462/probe_M2.raw`. *** The nine whose got-column is the pure-liveness form (`&#x27;survived&#x27; if alive else &#x27;died&#x27;`, want `survived`) are: rd lo, wr lo, rd hi, rd w16, scan, dma rd, arm survive, nodata live, nodata stale. **So the reframing is NOT confined to the three #462 rows the vacuity-floor carve-out blessed** -- six ordinary read rows in the pre-existing detector assert nothing except that the process did not exit, and under this row&#x27;s finding that predicate is TRUE OF A HUNG EMULATOR. *** THE DERIVATION IS RECORDED BECAUSE I GOT IT WRONG TWICE FIRST, in both of the directions the carrier warns about. *** (1) Counting `row()` CALL SITES through `ast` gave `6 of 52` -- but CALL SITES ARE NOT ROWS: the probe emits 58 from 52 literal-label sites because three sites sit inside loops, and the site at :263 has a COMPUTED label, which is exactly why four of the nine (rd lo, wr lo, rd hi, rd w16) cannot be found by grepping for `row(&quot;name&quot;`. (2) Anchoring the got-column at offset 14 instead of 15 -- the `%-14s` field is followed by a separator space -- returned `0 of 57`, a clean, confident PHANTOM ABSENCE that would have been read as `no row uses this form`. Both were caught only by carrying the DENOMINATOR: `0 of 57` and `0 of 0` are different claims and only one is evidence.  *** AND THE SECOND HALF OF THIS ROW&#x27;S CLAIM MEASURES AS SEVEN, NOT SIX, of 33. *** The filing says `six pty probes have NO prompt detection at all`. Counted 2026-08-24 with one pass -- `grep -l pty.fork *.py` then `xargs grep -L &#x27;endswith(&#x27;` -- **33 pty-forking probes, 7 with zero occurrences**: drive_guest.py, gdrom_data_probe.py, gdrom_default_probe.py, sh_intprio_probe.py, sh_iprbcd_probe.py, sh_pclock_probe.py, sh_tlbmulti_probe.py. The CLOSED-WORLD mechanism is confirmed by reading `gate_hygiene.sh:97-99`: `py_code()` pipes every file through `xargs -0 grep -h &quot;endswith(&quot;`, so a file containing ZERO occurrences contributes to no counter and the ratchet passes. **A probe with no prompt detection is invisible to the ratchet built to catch probes with BAD prompt detection.**  *** AND THE `absent` PATH WENT FROM READING TO MEASUREMENT WITHIN THE HOUR, IN THIS ROUND&#x27;S OWN THIRD TABLE. *** The adjudicating seat wrote that printed() returning `absent` would redden the idle rows under a hang, but offered it as `a reading, not a measurement -- no absent-red appears anywhere in the preserved kill map`. Verified: 0 of the 14 table-2 logs contain one. **Table 3&#x27;s M11 run contains one.** The comparison, same mutant, two runs: TABLE 2 -- `nodata stale` reads `died`, host TERMINATED, M11 scores 54/57 with three reds. TABLE 3 -- **all NINE liveness rows read `survived`**, so the host never terminated, and M11 scores 56/58 with two reds: `stale says` at `0` against a wanted `1`, and `stale idle` at **`absent`** against `0x0`. *** SO M11&#x27;S OBSERVABLE FAILURE IS NOT STABLE ACROSS RUNS -- a NULL-pointer read of 408 bytes faults on one run and does not on another -- AND ON THE RUN WHERE IT DID NOT FAULT, NOT ONE LIVENESS ROW REDDENED. *** Had `stale says` and `stale idle` not existed, M11 would have scored 58/58 and ESCAPED. That is a direct, measured demonstration of why the not-EOF predicate&#x27;s blind spot matters, and it arrived inside the very round that was arguing about it. **HONEST LIMIT: `absent` with the process alive is CONSISTENT WITH a hang, and that reading is not yet established** -- it is equally consistent with the session simply outrunning its spin budget. Distinguishing them is exactly what the proposed three-state predicate (terminated / responsive / unresponsive) would do, and it is now the strongest argument for building it. *This also strengthens the case that the DETECTOR is sound while the PREDICATE is not: the value rows caught what the liveness rows could not see.*  *** THE `absent` CENSUS, WITH DENOMINATORS, AND A CONTROL THAT RULES OUT A FLAKY RIG. *** Across every preserved log: table 1 **0 of 10**, table 2 **0 of 14**, table 3 **1 of 4 completed runs** -- so one firing in 28 mutant runs, and it is M11. *** THE CONTROL IS M10, MEASURED IN THE SAME TWO TABLES: its reds are BYTE-IDENTICAL across them -- `arm count2` and `arm enable2` both times, 55/57 then 56/58. *** So the instability is specific to M11&#x27;s NULL-source `memory_rw` fault path and is NOT a general property of the rig, the probe, or the host under load. **That distinction is the whole value of the measurement**: without it, `M11&#x27;s mode changed` is indistinguishable from `this rig is flaky`, and the second reading would discredit every score in all three tables rather than one cell in one of them.  *** THE STALL DIAGNOSIS WAS CONFIRMED BY INTERVENTION, NOT INFERRED FROM A CONTRAST -- 125 BYTES -&gt; 24,211 BYTES, SAME SEAT, SAME PANEL, SAME EVENING, ONE VARIABLE. *** minimax stalled at 125 bytes on a brief with ZERO inlined code that said `Read gdrom_default_probe.py&#x27;s session()/rd()`. The brief was corrected -- `rd()` and the command loop inlined verbatim -- and the same seat was re-fired into the same panel: **24,211 bytes, a 194x difference.** The carrier&#x27;s inline-every-fact rule has been stated for months from anecdote; this is the controlled version, and the counterfactual is measured rather than argued. *** AND THE SEAT THAT `STALLED` IMMEDIATELY CORRECTED THIS ROW&#x27;S CENTRAL CLAIM. *** This row rests on `rd() returns False ONLY on EOF`. minimax, as soon as it could SEE the code: `this is NOT just EOF -- it could be EIO, EBADF, etc.` The `except OSError` arm catches considerably more than end-of-file, so `alive == False` means `the read raised OSError or returned empty`, not `the process exited`. **That sharpens the row rather than refuting it** -- the hang-blindness stands, since none of those conditions fires for a wedged-but-open pty -- but the premise as written is imprecise and a detector built on the literal wording would mis-describe what it observes. *The seat was never unable to contribute; it was unable to SEE.*  *** THE PANEL CONVERGED ON THE PREMISE, NOT THE CONCLUSION: THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY ISOLATED `rd() RETURNS FALSE ONLY ON EOF` AS THE SINGLE LOAD-BEARING AND UNVERIFIED CLAIM -- and one of them showed it is WRONG AS WRITTEN. *** minimax: `rd() claim is wrong` (`except OSError` catches EIO, EBADF). kimi: real `only if` that claim holds. glm: `rests on a single unverified load-bearing premise`. **That is a panel doing the job a panel is for -- not voting on the conclusion, but finding the one sentence everything else hangs from.** deepseek then gave the corrected form its sharpest statement: **`alive` is a NON-EXIT predicate, not a liveness predicate** -- which says what it DOES observe, where the row&#x27;s own wording said only what it fails to observe. The conclusion survives (a wedged-but-open pty raises no OSError and returns no EOF, so the hang-blindness stands) but the row must be restated in deepseek&#x27;s terms before it is acted on.  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S SHAPE IS NOW ADJUDICATED: the cheap fix is a SESSION-TAIL SENTINEL, not a per-command change, and the reason is the corrected premise. *** Because `alive` is a NON-EXIT predicate, the cheapest way to add a liveness reading is ONE EXTRA NON-EXIT CHECK AT THE END -- not re-timing the reads that already work. Appending it leaves every scored row&#x27;s window untouched, so it re-measures nothing; codex&#x27;s positive-completion detection is the eventual full conversion and carries a fresh matrix. **D1 explains the SHAPE of D4, and D2/D3 keep the expensive forms out of this round.**  *** THE CENSUS NUMERATOR WAS RIGHT AND MY DERIVATION OF IT WAS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES -- corrected 2026-08-25 after a measuring seat re-derived it and I re-checked with `ast`. *** I wrote that the count is `9 of 57` because `three sites sit inside loops, and one site at :263 has a COMPUTED label`. Measured: **EXACTLY TWO** `row()` sites are inside a loop, and **THOSE SAME TWO** carry the computed labels; `:263` is a `put h` format string inside `run()` and emits NO ROW AT ALL. The arithmetic that actually reaches the total is `52 call sites - 2 in-loop + 2 x 4 iterations = 58`. *** AND THE DENOMINATOR WAS STALE: it is 9 of **58**, not 57 -- the shipped probe emits 58 (`GDROM_DEF_RESULT=58/58`). *** So the row said `9 of 57` with a derivation that reaches neither 57 nor 58. **This is the right-conclusion-wrong-reason class the carrier warns gets REUSED**, and it is the second instance in this round: `safe 4`&#x27;s warrant claimed `&amp; 1` kills +0x04 when under `&amp; 1` nothing dies. *A reader re-deriving from my sentence does not reach 58, which is the whole test of whether a stated reason is load-bearing or decorative.* **AND THE MORE USEFUL CENSUS IS A DIFFERENT ONE: 13 of 58**, not 9 -- `want == &#x27;survived&#x27;` is the wrong predicate for hang-blindness. The right one is *which rows PASS on an EMPTY transcript with alive True*, which adds four zero-count rows (`accept8`, `dma accept`, `nodata notx`, `accept quiet`) whose PASS value is a count of 0, and a wedged emulator produces 0.  *** `hang-blind` IS THE WRONG WORD FOR THE DETECTOR AND THE RIGHT ONE FOR THE ROWS -- corrected by the adjudicating seat against its own earlier phrasing. *** 45 of 58 rows carry a positive oracle a silent session breaks (`printed()` returns `absent` -&gt; RED), so a hang IS caught loudly by the detector -- it is **MISATTRIBUTED**, reading as value failures rather than as a hung session. Strictly better than blind, and the cost is DIAGNOSIS, not detection: a hang regression reddens the battery but the signature points at values. *** AND THE TWO SUPPOSEDLY-BLIND ROWS ARE IN DIFFERENT STATES, which the verify-across-all-tables caveat caught before the record could call both `genuinely blind`: *** `accept8` has NO recorded kill anywhere -- genuinely unproven coverage. `dma accept` **HAS a measured kill on record** -- `m_deldma18`, 31/32, reddening that row alone -- but it is a **#461** mutant, so it is absent from all three #462 tables (verified: 0 red across 40 mutant runs) **and its per-row log is in the `killartifacts` gap**, making it verifiable from PROSE ONLY. *That is this session&#x27;s own archive defect blocking the verification of a claim, in the round that filed it and wrote the durability rule against it.* FILED as coverage debt, not owed: a row with no kill is UNPROVEN coverage, not a demonstrated false pass, so the stopping rule files it.  *** PARTIAL SHIP at `abf665c`, and the row STAYS OPEN: that commit corrected the probe&#x27;s own CONTRACT (a wrong record about what `alive` observes) but did NOT fix the predicate. *** The distinction matters for anyone reading the row later: what shipped is the honest description -- `alive` means no EOF and no OSError were seen inside the sampled windows, it is True for a hung, a frozen AND an already-killed process, and a red `alive == died` cannot tell a clean exit from a pty error. What is still OWED is the fix: the session-tail sentinel giving three states (terminated / responsive / unresponsive), which re-measures nothing because it is APPENDED. *A row that ships a corrected record and keeps its defect open is the normal case, not an anomaly -- and naming the commit is what stops the correction from later being mistaken for the fix.*
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3253:**DISPOSITION IT RECOMMENDS: documented-only for existing probes; require CASE SELECTION in new
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3540:PASS-1 PANEL: `_scratchpad/panel_20260825_030653_matrix`. SIX SEATS FIRED, FIVE ANSWERED, ONE DOWN. grok, codex, minimax-m3, kimi-k3:cloud and glm-5.2 answered; **agy FAILED on a SIXTH seat-failure class -- its own streaming transport dropped mid-answer** (`connection to the agent was interrupted ... subscriber fell behind updates, stalled for 6s`) -- and was re-fired, since a dropped connection is worth retrying where a wall is not. *** grok&#x27;s verdict is the headline: DO NOT TRUST STATES. *** THE FINDINGS, by class. (1) STATE CONTRADICTS SUBSTANCE: `pitlatch` and `pitclobber` sit `in work` while every closure note says CLOSE and the residual their hold names is already discharged by a CLOSED carrier; `hpcshcpu` is `open` while its own note says DROP FROM THE ACTIONABLE QUEUE; `m8invpred` is `held` behind a codex wall discharged 2026-08-20; `reprowitness` is `in work` although the witness ladder it produced has been in the carrier since 2026-08-19; `gdromdmawidth` is `open` while admitting shipped code carries a comment under its name. (2) *** THE BURIAL SHAPE, TEXTBOOK FORM: `b115` and `b126` EACH CARRY TWO INDEPENDENT DEFECTS UNDER ONE ID. *** Closing either on one defect buries the other -- the exact hazard the carrier records. This is the highest-severity finding here. (3) STALE BLOCKERS: `gdromdmawidth` waits on `sh4gdromexit`, a carrier that will never do the work attributed to it. (4) UNEVIDENCED CLOSES: `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault`, `witnessinvisible` close asserting shipped substance with no commits; `b75` is a contested close with no recorded adjudication; `fbwitness` closes with an absent witness while its title claims a probe. (5) A CORRECTION THAT DID NOT PROPAGATE: a measured 4x inflation was corrected in `arcbiosubsan` and never in `sgiarcbiosoob`, which still asserts it -- mechanical rule 2 (grep for a claim&#x27;s SIBLINGS before calling it fixed) failing in the ledger itself. *** AND A WARNING FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS ROUND, because the round that filed it got this wrong THREE TIMES: THE LEDGER HAS MULTIPLE SCHEMA ERAS AND A CHECKER THAT KNOWS ONE ERA INVENTS ANOMALIES. *** (a) Narrative lives in `entries` on 198 older rows and `state_note` on 19 newer ones; counting only the latter reported 191 rows with no narrative when the true number is ZERO. (b) `witness` is FORWARD-ONLY from 2026-08-19 (`CLAUDE.md:1191`), so of 16 closed-without-witness rows FOURTEEN are compliant and only `rtcdet` and `fbwitness` are owed. (c) `closure.by` postdates part of the ledger. (d) &#x27;open/held WITH commits&#x27; looked like 16 anomalies and is ZERO -- all sixteen carry a HOLD marker, which is the normal batching state by design. **Establish the validity rule for each class BEFORE touching a cell, and print a denominator beside every count.**  *** THE SCOPE WAS MEASURED AND IT IS 12 ROWS, NOT ~50. The ~50 figure this row was FILED with was my own, and it was wrong for the FIFTH time in one session, by the same mechanism every previous time. *** Establishing each class&#x27;s validity rule BEFORE counting -- which is what the adjudication demanded -- collapses the raw counts hard: A closed-without-witness 16 raw -&gt; **2** (14 predate the 2026-08-19 forward-only cutoff and are COMPLIANT); B closed-without-commits 6 raw -&gt; **3** (three are withdrawn / not-a-bug / documented-only); C open-or-held-with-commits 17 raw -&gt; **1** (fifteen carry a HOLD, the normal batching state, and one was `asicbadaddr` itself, closed when #463 committed); D closed-without-closure.by 2 raw -&gt; **2**; E state-contradicts-closure 30 raw -&gt; **5**. *** RULE 5, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT COST THE MOST: `closure.note` MAY DISPOSE OF A STAGE, NOT THE ROW. *** The ledger defines four `phase_labels` -- assess, research, review, regress -- so `CLOSE (assess)` closes the ASSESS STAGE and the row legitimately stays `held`. TWENTY-FIVE of class E&#x27;s thirty are that shape; `exitsweep`&#x27;s own hold says so out loud (*&quot;CLOSURE REVERTED ... the fable5 batch answered this row&#x27;s held stage&quot;*). Reading a stage-scoped disposition as a row-scoped one manufactured 25 false defects. THE TWELVE THAT SURVIVE: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmadefault, witnessinvisible, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m437ord, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, cmtattr, hpcshcpu -- plus this row. Note the convergence worth trusting: `fbwitness`, `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` were named INDEPENDENTLY by the reading seats AND fall out of the mechanical census, which is the only kind of agreement that counts here.  RECONCILIATION, demanded by the adjudicator before either number may be quoted: the class column sums to **13 instances** and the repair set is **11 rows**. 13 - 1 dedup (`fbwitness` is in BOTH closed-no-witness and closed-no-commits) - 1 self (`ledgerstatelies` is this tracking row) = 11. Both numbers are correct; the arithmetic was simply never shown, which made the correction READ as contradicting its own column -- **a sixth counting defect sitting inside the correction that reports the fifth.**  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S CENTRAL FINDING, MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT WHAT EITHER SEAT CLAIMED: `state` IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE RECORD. *** Two file-reading seats disagreed on `fbwitness` -- one said `CLOSE (assess)` disposes of a STAGE so the row must be `held`, the other said it retires the assessment so `closed` is fine. Settled empirically rather than by vote, and BOTH inferences are unsupported: **23 rows carry the IDENTICAL `closure.note` of `CLOSE (assess)` and they are spread across THREE states -- 12 `held`, 7 `in work`, 4 `closed`** (the closed four being ns16550, fablequeue, carrier, fbwitness). The obvious second hypothesis was tested and also fails: the number of STAGES with recorded entries is statistically identical across the three groups (mean 1.8 / 1.8 / 1.9, same min and max), so stage coverage does not explain the split either. **`state` and `closure.note` are independent fields with no enforced relationship**, so a reader cannot derive one from the other and neither can a checker. That is why &#x27;DO NOT TRUST STATES&#x27; is the correct verdict, and it reframes this round: **the repair cannot be a mechanical rewrite of 11 cells, because there is no rule to rewrite them TO.** The round must first establish what `state` MEANS -- most plausibly &#x27;is there work outstanding on this row&#x27;, which is a different question from &#x27;has any stage been adjudicated&#x27; -- and only then repair. ALSO REFUTED HERE, and worth keeping because it was checkable in two minutes: the claim that `fbwitness` is ALONE among its 2026-08-20 cohort in being closed. That cohort is 84 rows -- 17 closed, 43 held, 11 dropped, 13 in work -- and four of the closed share `fbwitness`&#x27;s exact closure string. A seat&#x27;s argument from &#x27;this row is the only one&#x27; is worth checking every time; this one was wrong.  *** FIVE SEATS, ELEVEN ROWS, AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS THE EVIDENCE RATHER THAN THE NOISE. *** agy, kimi-k3, glm-5.2, minimax-m3 and grok each assigned a correct state to all eleven rows, reading the same ledger under the same four validity rules stated in the brief. **They agree on only 4 of 11.** `hpcshcpu` drew THREE different answers (closed / dropped / held); six more drew two. This is what a field with no derivable meaning looks like from the outside: competent independent readers do not converge, because there is nothing to converge ON. *** IT ALSO PARTITIONS THE ROUND CLEANLY, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. *** **UNANIMOUS (4) -- and all four are ALREADY IN THE RIGHT STATE**: `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` (closed, missing `commits`), `m437ord` (closed, missing `closure.by`), `cmtattr` (held, correct as-is). What these need is FIELDS, not state changes, and a field that is absent is absent under any definition of `state` -- so they are repairable NOW, independently of the rule question. **SPLIT (7)**: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, hpcshcpu. Every one of these turns on what `state` MEANS, and none can be repaired until that is settled -- editing them now would be manufacturing the appearance of correctness by picking whichever seat I found most persuasive. **THE SPLIT IS ALSO A CHECK ON THE 23-ROW FINDING**, arrived at from the opposite direction: that one measured one closure string against three states; this one measures five readers against one row set. Both say the same thing and neither depends on the other&#x27;s method.  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25, AND IT REFRAMED THE ROUND WHILE CONVICTING ME OF ONE OVERREACH. *** The ruling: `state` and `closure.note` are different TENSES. The note is a PAST-TENSE event log of what happened to a STAGE; `state` is a PRESENT-TENSE assertion of what the ROW still OWES. **Under that reading the multi-state spread of one closure string is the ledger WORKING AS DESIGNED, not the defect** -- rows that experienced the same stage event legitimately differ in what else they owe -- and the stage-coverage test failing is CORROBORATION rather than a second mystery, because state depends on what REMAINS, not on how much happened. **SO THE THING TO FILE IS THE MISSING SCHEMA SEMANTICS, NOT THE SPREAD.** That single absence is the cause behind six mis-counts in one session, two seats inferring opposite conventions from one string, and five seats agreeing on only 4 of 11 rows. The fix is written: a `state_semantics` block now sits in the ledger&#x27;s own schema, PENDING OWNER RATIFICATION, forward-only in the same way `witness` was. *** MY OVERREACH, RETRACTED IN PLACE: I wrote that &#x27;for most of the 11 there is no fact of the matter to restore -- only a decision&#x27;. THAT IS FALSE AND IT WAS CONVENIENT FOR ME*** -- it converts eleven tedious substance reads into one interesting rule question. What died was a repair METHOD (derive state from the note), not the fact of the matter, which is read from commits, gates, holds and filed questions -- exactly how `asicbadaddr` was closed, with no note convention needed. **The seminar is over in one paragraph; the eleven reads remain owed.** *** AND NO NUMBER IN THIS ROW MAY BE QUOTED AS A PREMISE UNTIL A SECOND PARTY CHECKS IT. *** I have mis-counted this ledger six times in one session. Every ruling above depends on the EXISTENCE of the spread, not its size -- ONE held row and ONE closed row sharing a closure string already prove non-derivability -- so the whole second-party check is two greps. The figures 23, 84 and the 1.8/1.8/1.9 stage means are UNVERIFIED SUPPORTING COLOUR, labelled as such, and are leads rather than premises.  *** A SEVENTH COUNTING HAZARD, FOUND BY MAKING IT: SEAT NAMES ARE NOT SEAT ANSWERS. *** While closing #463&#x27;s stages I reported research at 9/9; it was 8/9. The `codex` entry on that stage is a **`[HELD AWAITING SEAT:` MARKER** -- a record that the seat is OUTSTANDING -- and I counted it because I counted seat NAMES. `check_stage_panels.py` excludes marker entries deliberately, and its own comment says exactly why: *&#x27;otherwise marking a gap would close it.&#x27;* **Writing down that a seat is missing would, under a name-count, make the stage look complete** -- the seat-failure-as-agreement shape in its purest mechanical form. **THE LEDGER CARRIES 44 SUCH MARKER ENTRIES**, each one a seat that has NOT answered. Any future census of panel coverage that counts names rather than answers over-reports by up to that much. Three marker tokens exist and all three must be excluded: `[HELD AWAITING SEAT:`, `[NOT A PANEL STAGE: SINGLE-SEAT RE-READ]`, and `[RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY]`. *This belongs with the schema-semantics finding rather than beside it: `entries` is not a list of answers, it is a list of EVENTS, and some events record an absence. That is the same present-tense/past-tense confusion that `state` and `closure.note` embody, one level down.*  *** A NEW INSTANCE OF THIS ROW&#x27;S OWN DEFECT, FOUND 2026-08-25 BY THE ADJUDICATOR TRIPPING OVER IT WHILE ADJUDICATING THIS VERY BLOCK. *** Asked to rule on the queue, the fable5 seat reported that `idesync` &quot;is already closed as document-do-not-fix&quot;. The ledger says `state = &quot;in work&quot;` with `commits` EMPTY. Both readings are half right, and the shape is new: *** THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BUT THE DOCUMENT HAS NOT SHIPPED. *** The row carries an opus5 REVIEW verdict (2026-08-20) of DOCUMENT-DO-NOT-FIX on a deciding fact nobody else raised -- the drive never advertises FLUSH CACHE, so the highest IDENTIFY word written never sets it -- plus a closed assess stage (`closure.by = fable5`, `CLOSE (assess)`). A SUBSTANCE reader therefore sees a finished decision and says &quot;closed&quot;; a STATE reader sees no commits and says &quot;unfinished&quot;. Under the semantics now written, `closed` means discharged BY WORK, and a documenting commit IS work that has not happened -- so the state field is RIGHT and the adjudicator was WRONG, while the adjudicator was right about the only thing anyone would act on. *** THE LESSON IS THAT `document-do-not-fix` NEEDS ITS OWN DISCHARGE, AND NOTHING TRACKS IT. *** A row in this state is invisible to both readings: it looks decided to anyone reading the verdict and claimed to anyone reading the state, so nobody ships the document. That is an UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION with no carrier -- the class section I of precommit exists for, one field over. Candidate fix, NOT applied pending ratification: `closed` requires either a commit or an explicit `documented-only` closure badge, so an assessed-not-changed record cannot close without the record existing. The project&#x27;s ethos already demands honest &#x27;assessed, not changed&#x27; records; what is missing is anything that NOTICES when one was promised and never written.
