tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3620:The carrier already states the rule (*&#x27;a line number in a comment is invalidated by your own edits to that file; re-grep every same-file citation AFTER the last edit&#x27;*). **#463 broke it SIX TIMES while knowing it**, which is the argument for mechanising rather than restating: (1) `:200,:203` for the debug() calls, stale when the #463 comment was first inserted; (2) the SAME cite stale AGAIN after the comment was trimmed three lines shorter -- **a correction that went stale a second time**; (3) `:230` for `default:` in the round block, found by a records-audit seat; (4) `:144-152` for the mask_13 handler in the probe docstring; (5) four BARE artifact names with seven candidates each -- citation decay by NAMESPACE rather than by movement, caught by section X; (6) `:227` for `default:` going stale inside the very edit that corrected a different overstatement. *** THE SHAPE IS ALWAYS THE SAME AND ALWAYS INVISIBLE: the text does not change and stops being true. *** Section D checks that ADDED citations EXIST; it cannot see a citation that still resolves to a line of the wrong content, and existence is not correctness -- its own output says so. PROPOSED CHECK, cheap and mechanical: for each `:NNN` in a source comment, require an adjacent ANCHOR TOKEN (a short quoted fragment of the cited line) and verify the token is actually ON line NNN. That converts every citation into a self-verifying pair and catches movement, trimming and namespace decay alike. Cost: one token per citation, and the round that wrote six stale ones would have caught all six at commit time.  *** A SEVENTH INSTANCE, AND IT PRODUCED A BETTER FIX THAN THE CHECKER THIS ROW PROPOSED. *** Adding seven lines about transaction closure moved `default:` again, and `:229` came to point at `d-&gt;mask_9[r] = idata;`. Caught only because the citation was re-derived immediately after the edit -- the manual discipline works exactly as long as someone remembers it, which is the definition of a check that eventually does not happen. **THE REAL FIX IS CHEAPER THAN AN ANCHOR-TOKEN CHECKER: a SAME-FILE citation does not need a line number.** `default:` is in the same function, below the comment; `the default: arm below` and `the struct above` are unambiguous, need no maintenance, and CANNOT DECAY -- there is nothing there to go stale. Applied: all same-file `:NNN` citations in this comment are now positional, and the only numbers left are CROSS-FILE -- and the ENUMERATION here named four when there are SIX (`core/debugmsg.c:375`, `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:790-798`, `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:72`, `dreamcast_sysasicvar.h:74`, `configure:312-314`); the CLAIM that all remaining are cross-file is true and zero bare `:NNN` remain, but the list was not exhaustive -- which edits to THIS file cannot move. **THE RULE THAT FALLS OUT: cite a line number only ACROSS files; within a file, cite by POSITION or by the name of the thing.** That removes the entire class from same-file comments at zero cost, and leaves the checker worth building for the cross-file remainder, which is a much smaller surface -- seven instances this round were ALL same-file.  *** AN EIGHTH INSTANCE, AND THIS ONE REFUTES A JUDGEMENT RATHER THAN AN EDIT. *** After converting every same-file citation to a positional reference, two bare cross-file numbers remained, each following a QUALIFIED cite to the same file. I inspected them and judged the idiom clear enough -- `core/debugmsg.c:384-390, against debug()&#x27;s at :375`. **A pass-2 seat then read `:375` as a SAME-FILE citation and reported claim 3 as failing**, with a second seat independently flagging one `:NNN` it &#x27;cannot prove cross-file&#x27;. **The judgement was refuted by demonstration, which is stronger than any argument I had for it**: the question was never whether the idiom CAN be read correctly but whether it IS, and two of six readers did not. Fixed by repeating the filename rather than relying on proximity. THE RULE SHARPENS: **a citation&#x27;s qualifier must be ON the citation, not merely NEAR it.** Proximity is not qualification -- it depends on the reader carrying context across a line break, which is exactly what a reader skimming a 60-line comment does not do.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">asicannounce</span><span class="ttl">asicannounce  #463 deleted the ASIC&#x27;s only diagnostic on a warrant that was measured too broad -- a bounded latched announce IS constructible, on a predicate that is not empty</span><span class="tip">asicannounce  #463 deleted the ASIC&#x27;s only diagnostic on a warrant that was measured too broad -- a bounded latched announce IS constructible, on a predicate that is not empty — witness: none needed to file -- the measurable consequence is READ from source and stated below. A rung-3 witness is cheap and is the same access `asic_badaddr_probe.py`&#x27;s `kill byte` row already drives (a byte probe at +0x0d). — note: *** FILED 2026-08-25 UNDER THE LATE-DISSENT RULE, DISPOSITION 3, AND IT IS FILED SEPARATELY BECAUSE AN EARLIER DRAFT FOLDED IT INTO `asicindexcase` -- WHICH IS THE ONE-ID-TWO-DEFECTS FAILURE THIS PROJECT RECORDED ON 2026-08-24. *** `asicindexcase` is about an INDEXING `case` added at an r == 3 offset. Someone could close that with a bounds guard and **the announce question would vanish with it**, unexamined. Different question, different id. *** THE DISSENT, CARRIED VERBATIM AS PROVENANCE (three seats, independently -- codex and grok file-reading, deepseek packet-fed): *** codex -- *&quot;byte/word probes are distinguishable using `len`, and unaligned calls using `relative_addr`; a predicate such as &#x27;not an aligned 4-byte subaccess&#x27; is demonstrably nonempty. Therefore a bounded diagnostic remains viable. Silent fall-through may still be chosen as policy, but this commit has not proved that a latch would announce legal traffic or that every useful handler-local predicate is empty.&quot;* deepseek -- *&quot;A byte access to +0x0d is not a lane of a legal multi-word access... the predicate would not be empty; it would select byte accesses to reserved word 3.&quot;* grok -- *&quot;The latch FILTER is empty for len=4 in the modelled groups; that does NOT refute the in-tree bounded-announce option.&quot;* *** WHAT #463 ACTUALLY ESTABLISHED, restated correctly: *** the predicate is empty ONLY for an ALIGNED 32-BIT access at +0x0c, where a legal `fmov.d` half and a direct probe are byte-identical calls at the handler. Every other shape -- byte, word, unaligned -- is distinguishable. The round&#x27;s record claimed the option was REFUTED; it was not, and that claim is corrected in place in the CHANGELOG and the ledger. *** IT CLEARS THE &#x27;EARNS A ROUND&#x27; DISCRIMINATOR, WHICH A DESIGN DISSENT USUALLY DOES NOT: IT STATES A MEASURABLE CONSEQUENCE. *** MEASURED BY READING: `core/debugmsg.c:375` is `if ((quiet_mode &amp;&amp; !ss) || v &lt; 0) return;`, and free-running at `verbose == 0` gives v = -1. So a byte probe at +0x0d produces **ZERO host output today**; under a bounded latch it produces N. **That delta IS the self-announcing property the round&#x27;s own de-escalation clause says the fix destroyed** -- so this is not documented-only, it is a measurable behaviour difference. SUPPORTING MEASUREMENT from the same seat, worth keeping because it is what makes the predicate safe: every multi-word path in this tree decomposes into ALIGNED 4-BYTE lanes only -- `fmov.d` via `cpu-&gt;memory_rw(..., sizeof(data), ...)` in all six encodings, and the store queue at `cpu_sh_instr.c:3767-3775` as eight ascending 4-byte calls. So `not an aligned 4-byte subaccess` excludes EVERY lane of EVERY legal multi-word access. *** AND THE THIRD OPTION IS ALREADY IN THE TREE, which is what the THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET rule was minted from on this very round: *** the fork&#x27;s house idiom is an N=1 per-instance latch plus `fatal()`, ~34 sites across 11 device files, with the `dev_asc.c:248-257` warrant for keeping `fatal()` over `debugmsg()`. The round debated delete-versus-keep and never costed the third member. DO NOT reopen `#463` for this. The code shipped, is gated and is witnessed; this is a NEW round if it runs at all, ranked by severity against live defects like any other filed item -- and on severity it is insurance, not a live defect.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdrompiooddlen</span><span class="ttl">gdrompiooddlen  the GDROM PIO read guards ONE byte and then reads TWO -- LATENT, because no path can produce an odd length today</span><span class="tip">gdrompiooddlen  the GDROM PIO read guards ONE byte and then reads TWO -- LATENT, because no path can produce an odd length today — witness: none, and none is possible today -- the OWED reachability measurement was done BY READING and came back NEGATIVE. The code asymmetry is real; the route to it is not. See the correction below. — note: *** FOUND 2026-08-25 WHILE VERIFYING A PREMISE IN THE `gdrompionull` PASS-1 BRIEF, NOT WHILE LOOKING FOR IT. *** The brief&#x27;s Q4 asked, almost as an afterthought, whether `d-&gt;cur_data_offset` is reset anywhere -- because the round had reasoned about `data_len` throughout and barely looked at the guard&#x27;s OTHER operand. It is reset (`:327`, beside the NULL). But reading the guard to check that showed something else. *** THE GUARD CHECKS ONE BYTE AND THE BODY READS TWO. *** Verified by reading, three lines:   (1) `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:611` -- `if (d-&gt;cur_data_offset &lt; d-&gt;data_len) {`   (2) `:612-613` -- `odata = d-&gt;data[d-&gt;cur_data_offset ++];` then `odata |= (d-&gt;data[d-&gt;cur_data_offset ++] &lt;&lt; 8);` -- **two increments, one bounds test**   (3) `alloc_data` `:309` -- `malloc(d-&gt;data_len)`, **exactly `data_len` bytes, no padding, no rounding**. So at `cur_data_offset == data_len - 1` the guard PASSES, the first read is the last valid byte, and **the second read is `d-&gt;data[data_len]` -- one byte past the allocation**. *** REACHABILITY, STATED AS WHAT IS READ AND WHAT IS OWED. *** READ: `data_len = d-&gt;cnt` (`:307`), and `d-&gt;cnt` is GUEST-WRITABLE A BYTE AT A TIME -- `:704` sets the low byte from a CNTLO store and `:712` the high byte from CNTHI, so an ODD count is expressible. The two subsequent adjustments do not remove oddness: `:409-410` only replaces `cnt` when it is ZERO, and `:486-487` only rewrites the single value 65536. **OWED: a measurement that an odd `cnt` actually survives to `alloc_data` on a path a guest can drive.** The existing `gdrom_pio_null_probe.py` already stages commands through these registers and could answer it with two extra stores; I have NOT run it, and this row does not claim the defect is reachable until that runs. *** WHY THIS IS A DIFFERENT DEFECT FROM `gdrompionull` AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT. *** That row is `data` NULL while `data_len` lies -- a state-invariant violation whose fix is one assignment in the tail. This is an OFF-BY-ONE in a bounds test, on a path where `data` is a perfectly valid pointer and `data_len` is honest. Different fix design (`&lt; data_len` becomes `+ 1 &lt; data_len`, or the read becomes width-aware), different oracle (a heap over-read, not a SIGSEGV), so under the reopening rule they are separate rounds. **Note the two interact in the safe direction: `gdrompionull`&#x27;s fix sets `data_len = 0`, under which this guard is false and the over-read cannot occur on THAT path.** It does nothing for the odd-count path. *** AND UNLIKE THIS DEVICE&#x27;S OTHER OUT-OF-BOUNDS ISSUES, ASan SHOULD SEE THIS ONE. *** The file&#x27;s own comment at `:272` records that ASan cannot detect its member-to-member overrun because that is intra-object inside one malloc. **This is a read PAST a malloc&#x27;d buffer&#x27;s end**, which is the case ASan is built for -- so `build-asan/` is a real second oracle here, where for the aliasing family it was not. That is worth testing early because it is cheap and because a positive ASan result would settle reachability and severity in one run. SEVERITY, stated honestly and NOT inflated: a ONE-BYTE heap over-READ. It does not write, and the byte it returns goes to the guest as the high half of a 16-bit read. That is an information leak of one adjacent heap byte per odd-length transfer and a potential crash only if the allocation ends exactly on a page boundary. It is materially less severe than the SIGSEGV in `gdrompionull` and should rank below it.  *** REACHABILITY SETTLED 2026-08-25, THE SAME DAY THIS ROW WAS FILED, AND IT SETTLED AGAINST THE ROW. *** The filing said the odd-count route was OWED and explicitly did NOT claim the defect was reachable. That was the right caution, and the answer is NO. *** `data_len` HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER, AND BOTH ROUTES TO IT FORCE AN EVEN VALUE. *** Verified by grepping every assignment: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:307` in `alloc_data` (`d-&gt;data_len = d-&gt;cnt;`) is the only one -- the apparent second hit at `:413` is a printf FORMAT STRING, not an assignment, which is the kind of match that would have made this look open if counted rather than read. `alloc_data` has exactly two call sites and each is guarded:   * `:342`, the Read-TOC path, is preceded by `:338-341` -- `if (d-&gt;cnt != 408) { fatal(...); exit(1); }`. **408 is even**, and nothing else reaches this call.   * `:418`, the read-sectors path, is preceded by `:412-415` -- `if (sector_count * 2048 != d-&gt;cnt) { fatal(...); break; }`. **Any multiple of 2048 is even.** Note `:409-410` only substitutes a count when `cnt == 0`, so a guest-set odd count survives THAT line -- and is then rejected by the equality check two lines later. **So the guest CAN express an odd `cnt` at the CNTLO/CNTHI registers (:704, :712), and that half of the filing was right; what it cannot do is get that value past either guard into `alloc_data`.** *** DISPOSITION: LATENT, NOT LIVE. SEVERITY DROPS FROM A ONE-BYTE HEAP OVER-READ TO A STRUCTURAL HAZARD WITH NO ROUTE. *** The asymmetry itself is untouched and still worth recording: `:611` guards `cur_data_offset &lt; data_len` and `:612-613` reads TWO bytes, while `alloc_data` mallocs exactly `data_len` with no padding. **Any future edit that admits an odd length -- a new command arm, a relaxed guard, a different transfer size -- makes this live immediately, and the code carries nothing that would stop it.** That is a real thing to know and a cheap thing to fix (`+ 1 &lt; data_len`, or make the read width-aware), but it is NOT a defect a guest can reach today and this row may not be ranked as one. *** AND THE ASan NOTE IN THE FILING IS NOW MOOT AND IS WITHDRAWN. *** It said `build-asan/` would be a real second oracle here, unlike the intra-object aliasing family. True in principle and irrelevant in fact: ASan cannot observe an access no execution reaches. *Process note worth keeping: this row was filed with reachability marked OWED rather than asserted, and the owed check refuted it within the hour -- at the cost of two greps. Had it been filed as a live heap over-read it would have been a WRONG RECORD ranked above real work, and the correction would have arrived later and cost more.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcperopcode</span><span class="ttl">wdcperopcode  wdc_command()&#x27;s unimplemented-command latch is per-DEVICE, so a guest cycling opcodes gets ONE line and 255 silent aborts -- a SPEC choice, filed not fixed</span><span class="tip">wdcperopcode  wdc_command()&#x27;s unimplemented-command latch is per-DEVICE, so a guest cycling opcodes gets ONE line and 255 silent aborts -- a SPEC choice, filed not fixed — witness: n/a -- this is a SPEC improvement, not a defect. The shipped behaviour is deliberate and is pinned by the `unimpl 2op` row, which fires TWO different opcodes and demands ONE line. — note: *** FILED 2026-08-26 BECAUSE THE SHIPPED DETECTOR&#x27;S CONTRACT CLAIMED A FILING THAT DID NOT EXIST. *** `wdc_flood_probe.py`&#x27;s header says *&quot;deepseek argued for a per-opcode set; FILED, not shipped&quot;* -- and no row carried it; the only trace was prose inside `wdcflood`&#x27;s state_note, which is not something a reader can find. **A contract line asserting a disposition is a WRONG RECORD until the disposition exists as a row.** Caught by the readiness adjudication before the round shipped. THE PROPOSAL, from the deepseek seat on the `wdcflood` pass-1 panel: `:583`&#x27;s diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so a single per-device flag suppresses every later unimplemented command and hides distinct guest actions. A 256-bit set (`uint32_t warned_unimpl_cmd[8]`) is cheap and preserves them. WHY IT WAS NOT SHIPPED, and the reason is not that it is wrong: the scalar is exactly what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines, which is the flood the round exists to bound. Per-opcode-versus-per-device is a SPEC choice between two acceptable behaviours, and the adjudication ruled that clause (d) tests the CHOSEN spec&#x27;s repair rather than adjudicating between specs -- deepseek&#x27;s framing (&quot;the scalar IS the (d) mutant&quot;) is circular, since it assumes per-opcode is the baseline and then calls the alternative a weakening. *** THE SHIPPED SPEC IS PINNED, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE TO DEFER: *** the `unimpl 2op` row fires TWO DIFFERENT opcodes and demands ONE line, so a later change to per-opcode is a VISIBLE spec change that reddens a row, not a silent drift. The same row kills a &quot;last-warned-opcode&quot; latch (measured: `M3`, 21/22, that row alone), under which an alternating guest floods forever. IF IT IS EVER WORKED, the option set is THREE-membered and the third member is the one the dispute never named: scalar (shipped), per-opcode set (deepseek), and the in-tree COUNT-BOUND idiom (`n_trunc_msgs`, `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523`). Note also that #337&#x27;s other latched messages at `:816`/`:855` carry NO value payload, so &quot;three latches in this file&quot; is weaker precedent for discarding value-resolution than it looks -- `:583` is the only site whose message names an opcode.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcexpecteddata</span><span class="ttl">wdcexpecteddata  an EXPECTED DATA write has no quiet pin, so dropping !write_in_progress would warn-and-latch on legal I/O with every detector row green</span><span class="tip">wdcexpecteddata  an EXPECTED DATA write has no quiet pin, so dropping !write_in_progress would warn-and-latch on legal I/O with every detector row green — witness: none -- building it needs a WRITE-command staging sequence `wdc_flood_probe.py` does not have, which is why it is filed rather than fixed. — note: *** RAISED BY THE grok SEAT ON THE `wdcflood` PASS-2 PANEL AND RECORDED NOWHERE UNTIL THE READINESS ADJUDICATION FOUND IT DROPPED. *** The unexpected-DATA-write site is guarded by `!d-&gt;write_in_progress &amp;&amp; !d-&gt;atapi_cmd_in_progress`. **Drop the first conjunct and the site warns-and-latches on a perfectly legal write during an active transfer** -- and every row in the 22-row detector stays GREEN, because none of them stages a WRITE command. *** THIS IS THE ACCEPT-SIDE HOLE THE OTHER THREE QUIET PINS CANNOT COVER. *** `accept ec`, `accept w8` and `accept r8` pin legal COMMAND, legal narrow WRITE and legal narrow READ. None of them puts the device into `write_in_progress`, so the one predicate whose accept side is guest-reachable during real I/O has no pin at all. COST OF CLOSING IT: a WRITE-command staging sequence (set up a 0x30 WRITE, reach DRQ, then store to DATA), which is materially more than the one-or-two-instruction stimuli every other row uses. That is the honest reason it is filed -- not that it does not matter. NOTE THE ASYMMETRY IT REVEALS, which generalises past this row: a latch&#x27;s accept side is only as good as the STATES the detector can reach, and a detector built from cheap stimuli systematically under-covers predicates that depend on expensive state.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcflagindep</span><span class="ttl">wdcflagindep  three of six wdc latch classes are pinned at one site each, and per-INSTANCE storage is unpinned entirely -- the fork has a two-controller rig</span><span class="tip">wdcflagindep  three of six wdc latch classes are pinned at one site each, and per-INSTANCE storage is unpinned entirely -- the fork has a two-controller rig — witness: n/a -- coverage debt against a shipped detector, not a defect. — note: *** FILED 2026-08-26 FROM THE opus5 FINAL-DIFF READ, AS THE RESIDUE OF A CONTRACT BULLET THAT WAS UNSCOPED. *** `wdc_flood_probe.py` listed reset-on-normal-operation and guest-resettable-operand under DESIGNED TO CATCH with no scope, while each was measured at exactly ONE site of six. Now scoped in the contract; the missing rows are here. **UNPINNED, each a known-reachable escape:** reset-on-normal at :160, :704 and :145; operand-swap at :583 -- and that one is CONCRETE: the guard reads `warned_unimpl_cmd` while the body reads `d-&gt;seccnt` at :660, a field the guest writes at :1003, so `if (!d-&gt;seccnt)` scores a clean sweep and no row builds the disagreeing state. **AND THE RING-PAIR HALF OF FLAG INDEPENDENCE:** `wr indep` now pins :607/:1025 by firing both in ONE session; the same shape pins :145/:160 at the cost of one more runloop. *** PER-INSTANCE STORAGE IS THE ONE THAT NEEDS A DIFFERENT RIG, AND THE FORK HAS ONE. *** A process-global `static` latch passes every row because every row starts a fresh emulator. `dev_pcic.c:234-242` adds TWO wdc instances unconditionally at 0x14000180 and 0x140001f0 (its comment: &#x27;Yuck. Now there are two.&#x27;), reached from every hpcmips subtype via `dev_vr41xx.c`. Use a WIDTH site, not :583 -- the width check precedes command dispatch, so no disk image is needed at all. New MIPS encodings, bounded work, and honestly lower value than the three-line rows above.  *** THE ASK-AGAIN ADDED THREE MORE MEMBERS TO THIS ROW, AND ONE OF THEM REFUTES A CONTRACT LINE. *** (1) **LAST-VALUE LATCH AT :704 IS UNPINNED.** Keying `warned_data_unexpected` on `len` -- or on `idata` -- sweeps clean, because `datawr x2` writes an IDENTICAL len and an IDENTICAL 0x1234 twice. The two width sites are now pinned by `w mixed`/`r mixed`; this one is not. (2) **RESET-ON-NORMAL AT :583 IS PINNED FOR ONE VALID OPCODE OF NINETEEN.** `interleave`&#x27;s only valid command is 0xEC, so a reset hidden in `WDCC_RECAL`, `WDCC_IDLE_IMMED`, `SET_FEATURES`, `ATAPI_SOFT_RESET` or `WDCC_DIAGNOSE` sweeps clean. The recorded kill is opcode-specific and the record did not say so. (3) *** AND THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST: A RESET ARRIVING THROUGH THE SIBLING HANDLER IS PINNED NOWHERE. *** `dev_wdc_altstatus_access` shares the same `d` as `dev_wdc_access`, and **zero rows in the probe touch its address** -- verified by grep. Clearing the six flags alongside `d-&gt;cur_command = COMMAND_RESET` on a `WDCTL_4BIT` write sweeps clean. **That is where a real driver&#x27;s reset goes**, which makes it the most plausible member of the family and the least covered. It refutes the contract&#x27;s earlier &#x27;reset-on-normal -- pinned at :607/:1025&#x27; as a general claim: those rows pin resets arriving through `dev_wdc_access` only. Contract corrected in place. *The pattern across all three: a row set built from ONE handler cannot see state changes arriving through a SIBLING handler that shares the device struct. That is a probe-shape gap, not a missing mutant, and it generalises past this device.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcsectorreset</span><span class="ttl">wdcsectorreset  a one-line latch reset on a NORMAL register write restores the #464 width flood and leaves all 26 detector rows green</span><span class="tip">wdcsectorreset  a one-line latch reset on a NORMAL register write restores the #464 width flood and leaves all 26 detector rows green — witness: none yet -- SOURCE-PROVEN by the codex seat on the #464 pass-2 panel, NOT measured. The seat said so itself: &#x27;source-proven to leave the current 26 rows unchanged, but still needs the named private-mutant measurement before being called a measured 26/26 false pass.&#x27; Building that mutant is this row&#x27;s first job, and it is cheap -- the kill-table harness from #464 takes it directly. — note: *** FILED 2026-08-26 FROM THE #464 PASS-2 PANEL (_scratchpad/panel_20260826_090604), codex seat, [P2]. *** THE MUTANT, verbatim as given: add `d-&gt;warned_nonbyte_write = 0;` to the `wd_sector` write arm, after the existing `d-&gt;sector = ...` assignment. One line, no guard touched, no diagnostic removed. **A guest that alternates a wide write to wd_seccnt with an ordinary 8-bit write to wd_sector then floods at one host line per pair, forever.** *** WHY ALL 26 ROWS STAY GREEN, AND IT IS THE SAME BLIND SPOT `interleave` WAS BUILT FOR, ONE REGISTER OVER: *** `interleave` pins reset-on-normal-operation at :583 only, using a valid COMMAND opcode. No row in the file writes wd_sector between two wide accesses, so no row can observe a reset keyed there. The detector&#x27;s own contract already discloses this class as scoped to one site of six -- it is recorded as `wdcflagindep` coverage debt -- but that row names the SITES, not this CONSTRUCTION, and closing `wdcflagindep` on a different site would bury this one. *** IT IS SMALLER THAN THE ESCAPE THE CAPPED ASK-AGAIN FOUND. *** #464&#x27;s ask-again found width-keying (two lines, at the guard). This is ONE line at a different register and produces an unbounded flood rather than one-line-per-store. **So the ask-again&#x27;s cap was not the binding constraint here -- the ask-again asked about the GUARD, and this edit never touches a guard.** Worth recording as a limit of that technique rather than a failure of it. DISPOSITION, and it follows the rules rather than a preference: not a wrong record; not a MEASURED false pass, because the seat labelled it source-proven and explicitly un-measured. #464&#x27;s detector satisfies vacuity-floor clauses (a) through (f), so an escape above the floor is COVERAGE DEBT -- the #447 precedent exactly. FILED. FIRST JOB: build the mutant and score it. If it sweeps 26/26 as predicted, this becomes a measured escape and the row carries the escape list; the closing row is a reset-on-normal-operation probe at wd_sector, which the #464 harness can express directly. **Do NOT fold this into `wdcflagindep`** -- that row is about which SITES share a flag; this is about WHICH REGISTER can clear one.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">panelrefire</span><span class="ttl">panelrefire  panel.sh printed &#x27;re-firing once&#x27; and re-fired nothing -- message corrected; the automatic retry itself is not implemented</span><span class="tip">panelrefire  panel.sh printed &#x27;re-firing once&#x27; and re-fired nothing -- message corrected; the automatic retry itself is not implemented — witness: harness:read -- panel.sh:395-396 printed the claim then `return 2`, and every caller is a bare `check &quot;name&quot; &quot;$file&quot;` whose exit status is discarded, with no seat command re-invoked anywhere after the seat check. Verified by reading the script; no build or run needed. — note: *** FILED 2026-08-26 BY THE CARRIER EXEMPLAR AUDIT. The FALSE MESSAGE is already fixed; what is filed is the missing BEHAVIOUR. *** `panel.sh` printed **&#x27;re-firing once (a wall can be minutes long) ...&#x27;** and then `return 2`. Nothing consumed that status and nothing was ever relaunched. *** WHY THIS IS WORSE THAN A STALE CITATION, and it is the reason it was fixed the same hour it was found: *** a stale citation misleads a reader who chooses to follow it. This printed a claim of ACTION, to the operator, at the exact moment a seat was lost -- so the one person who could have re-fired it by hand was told it had already happened. **It is the failure panel.sh itself warns about sixty lines earlier: &#x27;a documented check that cannot fail, which is worse than no check because it reassures.&#x27;** DONE: the message is now an INSTRUCTION (&#x27;RE-FIRE THIS SEAT ONCE BY HAND ... NOT done automatically&#x27;) rather than a false report, and the carrier&#x27;s &#x27;both applied&#x27; claim is corrected in place. OWED, and deliberately not done in the same edit: **the automatic retry.** It is a behaviour change to the script EVERY panel runs, so it owes two negative controls before it ships -- (i) prove the retry actually fires on a walled seat, and (ii) prove a seat that ANSWERED is not retried. Without (ii) the retry could double every panel&#x27;s cost silently, which is the mirror failure. Ranking: harness coverage, below live defects. But the value is concrete -- the carrier records a real instance where a walled seat was recovered only because the owner happened to notice it available in their own UI, which is precisely the human-noticing this harness exists to stop depending on.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3624:**SEVERITY SET LOW, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: the standing `rm` rests on a false reason but is retained as a conservative clean-build precaution; the cost is build time, not correctness.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromallocpair</span><span class="ttl">gdromallocpair  alloc_data() does not release before it allocates -- #465&#x27;s free_data() pairing is CONVENTIONAL, not STRUCTURAL</span><span class="tip">gdromallocpair  alloc_data() does not release before it allocates -- #465&#x27;s free_data() pairing is CONVENTIONAL, not STRUCTURAL — witness: none -- documented-only. READ and verified 2026-08-26: no third call site exists today, so there is nothing to reproduce. The hazard is what a future caller would do, which is why it is FILED rather than fixed. — note: *** FOUND BY THE GROK SEAT ON #465&#x27;s PASS 2, VERIFIED HERE BY READING. *** `free_data(d)` runs ONCE at the function entry (`:343`); both `alloc_data(d)` sites (`:359`, `:435`) are downstream, so today the buffer is always released before it is replaced. **But `alloc_data()` overwrites `d-&gt;data` unconditionally with no release of its own** -- a future THIRD call site not preceded by that entry release leaks the previous buffer, silently. *** THE SHAPE IS THE POINT, and it is #465&#x27;s own lesson one level up: *** that round added `free_data()` as `alloc_data()`&#x27;s counterpart precisely because two fields set together were being released apart. The pairing it established is CONVENTIONAL -- every current path happens to do it -- rather than STRUCTURAL, where the unpaired form is impossible. That is exactly the distinction the round&#x27;s own corrected comment now draws, after a pass-2 seat refused the word &#x27;unrepresentable&#x27;. NOT FIXED, deliberately: a release inside `alloc_data()` is an ADDITION to product code, and the addition licence requires a measured delta or a transferred warrant. **Neither exists yet -- with no third caller, no mutant can measure the difference.** The honest disposition is to record the hazard with its warrant so a round that ADDS a third call site inherits the obligation. Related, same seat: `d-&gt;cnt` is guest-writable at `GDROM_CNTLO`/`CNTHI` and reaches `data_len` only through `alloc_data()`. Assessed as a related pair, not this defect.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">probewiringscope</span><span class="ttl">probewiringscope  check_probe_wiring.py cannot see any detector below regress/&#x27;s top level, while its own docstring claims it covers them</span><span class="tip">probewiringscope  check_probe_wiring.py cannot see any detector below regress/&#x27;s top level, while its own docstring claims it covers them — witness: harness:READ and verified 2026-08-26 -- `check_probe_wiring.py:2` says &quot;Every committed DETECTOR under regress/&quot;; `:475` filters `f.endswith(&#x27;.py&#x27;) and &quot;/&quot; not in f` and `:481` uses `os.listdir(regress)`, so the walk never descends. Found by the opus5 seat on #465&#x27;s pass 2. — note: *** FIXED AS #471, COMPLETE RATHER THAN PARTIAL -- which is what the farmed design demanded: &#x27;land a complete path-aware fix WITH an explicit non-shipping classification and the controls above. Do not land only re-key + walk.&#x27; ***
