tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3878:title now names its deriver inline, so that one row cannot be re-derived with the wrong tool.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4valm33</span><span class="ttl">sh4valm33  COVERAGE DEBT: mutant M33 (reinstating a terminating site reachable by a guest mov.l to TCR2 at 0xffd80028) escapes the detector -- the arm itself was FIXED in #459 and is not live</span><span class="tip">sh4valm33  COVERAGE DEBT: mutant M33 (reinstating a terminating site reachable by a guest mov.l to TCR2 at 0xffd80028) escapes the detector -- the arm itself was FIXED in #459 and is not live — witness: none yet -- rung-3 shaped (a real guest store through real dispatch) but not run; a witness needs a build, which the machine embargo forbids. — note: *** FILED 2026-08-27, carried out of [[sh4valrows]] as that row was dropped. It is the ONE escape of that row&#x27;s claimed set that survived checking. ***
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3966:*** THE HONEST LIMIT, AND IT IS NOT A TECHNICALITY: RUNG 1 IS A DETECTOR, NEVER A WITNESS. *** The ladder is explicit -- *if it still compiles and still fails after the machine description and CPU/device dispatch are removed, leaving only a direct call, it was never a reproduction*. So this unblocks **measuring and detecting**, not witnessing. A rung-3 witness for any of these still needs a real emulator run.
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:3994:**SEVERITY SET MED, 2026-09-07, by the MAIN LOOP** (not a flagship adjudication -- opus5/fable5 are reserved for the 09-09 adjudication, and a rating is exactly the kind of judgment call that seat exists for; overturn it freely).  REASON: MEASUREMENT INTEGRITY: an ASAN sweep over pre-#427 copies measures code that is not shipped, so a green sweep is not evidence about HEAD.  Ranked with the other apparatus-lies-to-you rows.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcaddtoinbufbound</span><span class="ttl">wdcaddtoinbufbound  the ATAPI data-port WRITE arm has no phase guard, so a guest can inject bytes into a live DATA-IN transfer and desynchronise the next 32768-byte chunk</span><span class="tip">wdcaddtoinbufbound  the ATAPI data-port WRITE arm has no phase guard, so a guest can inject bytes into a live DATA-IN transfer and desynchronise the next 32768-byte chunk — commits: ae68e29 — HOLD: regress — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/wdcaddtoinbufbound/rung3_20260830/ -- RUNG 3, BUILT AND GREEN on one ask.  Unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, wdc via the committed bus_pci/piix4_ide path, free-running, 1.52 s, 20 of 20 predictions across both legs.  Sentinel ADDTOINBUF_RUNG3_DONE.  Controls: RAM liveness; a DEVICE SIGNATURE (two reads of one address giving push order, which a cell cannot); encodings 46/46 words AND mnemonics against the emulator&#x27;s own disassembler; disk content at known offsets.  On the committed build the guest is handed ITS OWN INJECTED BYTES back as disk data. — note: *** RIDER FROM THE FABLE SEAT 2026-08-27: THE WRITE-ARM BOUND COVERS MORE THAN DATA
tools\pipeline\matrix.html:4049:adjudication.  Evidence: codex closure pass 2026-09-07, regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_closeable_20260907.txt.*</span></span><span class="state st-in work" title="regress">in work</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S LOAD-BEARING PREMISE BY OPENING THE FIXTURE, AND THE REFUTATION CHANGES WHAT THE ROUND OWES. ***  The brief said any fix keeping foreign bytes out of the DATA-IN stream turns the shipped `re-arm: refills a NON-EMPTY ring too` row RED, so the oracle-correction rule applies unconditionally.  **It does not.**  That row plants its foreign bytes by DIRECT PRIMITIVE CALLS -- five `wdc_addtoinbuf(&amp;e, 0x77)` -- and fires the re-arm with a `MEM_READ`.  **A write-arm fix never touches it.**  Only the re-arm option reds it.  So the protocol is OPTION-CONDITIONAL, and it warned against running it anyway: re-pinning an oracle no code change will redden is expectation churn with no red to show, **which the oracle-correction rule itself calls the laundering signature.**  It then swept every OTHER shipped row for collateral under a write-arm fix and found none: the three `midxfer` rows and both mid-DATAIN write rows assert `stub_frees == 0` with NO occupancy oracle, and their fixtures were already re-pinned so a phase-keying fix does not false-fail.  **A3 confirmed AND UNDERSOLD.**  The severity downgrade is larger than claimed: the source&#x27;s `shared ring feeds the CDB-dispatch and drain paths` hedge is ALSO dead, because `wdc_new_command_reset()` flushes at the top of every `wdc_command()`.  Blast radius at HEAD is the guest&#x27;s own current transfer plus self-inflicted DRQ ghosting -- **no host-safety, no cross-command reach** -- which ranks it below every row with a host-safety or flood channel, including the two check-after-the-store siblings the brief keeps out of scope.  It required the downgrade be WRITTEN with its carrying rows named, since the row&#x27;s `sev` is empty and a reader would otherwise re-derive the old rank from the prose trail.  **A4: ONE STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT OPTION MISSING** -- allowlist-by-DIRECTION (decline unless the device expects host-&gt;device bytes) rather than blocklisting DATAIN.  That is what real hardware does, and it covers the idle-state and COMPLETED-phase pushes a DATAIN blocklist leaves alive -- today even the `not expecting any` arm announces and then PUSHES ANYWAY.  It also measured that the re-arm option is **a partial fix by construction**: an injection during the LAST chunk has no following re-arm.  **Q2 CONFIRMED, and pinned by a SHIPPED ROW rather than by argument** (`no-push write does not dispatch`: occupancy 12, push-free write, DATAIN, frees 0) -- which it noted is unlike the transferred warrant that bit this round earlier.  **A5: `wdcrearmwalk` does NOT join** -- it is coverage debt, not a fix site, so *one fix design covers both* fails definitionally.  But it ruled the follow-on precisely: if this round&#x27;s clause-(d) enumeration forces a multi-re-arm row, that row is THIS round&#x27;s under L1 and `wdcrearmwalk` then closes as OVERTAKEN under the three conditions, rather than joining.  Named FOUR stale records with their line anchors; three were corrected this session after verifying each against source. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THE WITNESS IS BUILT AND IT WORKS -- 20 of 20, both legs, every control green -- AND IT REFUTED TWO OF THE BRIEF&#x27;S PREMISES, ONE OF WHICH CHANGES THE ROUND&#x27;S DISPOSITION. ***  Badge A disclosed unprompted: it was the opus5 seat on `#494`, so its baseline contains its own fix, same device and file.  **(1) THE SKETCH AS BRIEFED COULD NOT HAVE EXHIBITED THE DEFECT AT ALL.**  I wrote *32 blocks on a CD-ROM* assuming 2048-byte blocks.  In this tree CD-ROM `logical_block_size` is **512** -- the 2048 branch is inside `#if 0` -- so 32 blocks is 16384 bytes, under the 32768 chunk clamp: no second chunk, no re-arm, nothing to measure.  **It needs 128 blocks.**  A seat that had merely followed the brief would have produced a clean run and called the defect absent.  **(2) `ANY FIX THAT KEEPS FOREIGN BYTES OUT TURNS THE SHIPPED ROW RED` IS MEASURED FALSE**, independently confirming what the fable5 adjudication derived by reading the fixture: fixA (write-arm guard) leaves the detector at **128 rows, 0 failures**; only fixB (flush at the re-arm) reds it, at 1.  **So the round is NOT under the oracle-correction rule unless it picks the re-arm option**, and the row is a rare DISCRIMINATOR between the two -- the opposite of the carrier&#x27;s *green under every candidate fix, settles nothing* corollary.  *** (3) THE FLOOR FINDING, AND IT GOVERNS THE ROUND: THE SHIPPED 128-ROW DETECTOR DOES NOT OBSERVE THIS DEFECT AT ALL. ***  `base` scores 0 failures WITH THE DEFECT LIVE, so vacuity-floor clause (a) -- fails on the pre-fix build -- is **not satisfiable from existing rows**.  If the round ships the write-arm guard, **the witness must ship AS the detector or it ships below the floor.**  And two one-expression clause-(d) mutants escape the detector entirely (`!= PHASE_CMDOUT` -&gt; `== PHASE_COMPLETED`; `&amp;&amp; len == 1`), caught only by the witness at 17/20.  **fixA&#x27;s zero is a MEANINGFUL zero**, and it proved that rather than asserting it: `mutA1` moves 14 rows on the same arm, so the rows ARE sensitive to a guard there.  That is the negative control for *fixA disturbs nothing*.  **THE WITNESS AND THE DETECTOR ARE COMPLEMENTARY AND NEITHER SUBSUMES THE OTHER** -- mutA1 is caught by the detector and by the witness&#x27;s signature control; mutA2/mutA3 only by the witness.  Ship both.  **Two option members missing, and the first is orthogonal to my whole axis:** (e) DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE -- my (a)-(d) are LOCATIONS, refuse-vs-announce is a DISPOSITION and they compose; the silence is a separate defect from the push, and a guest can inject into a live transfer with nothing anywhere saying so.  (f) COUNT the foreign bytes into `atapi_len` -- **named so nobody takes it**, since it turns guest bytes into transfer data, but an unnamed option is one a later round re-invents.  It also noted (c) SUBSUMES (a) and removes a coupling: **fixA&#x27;s correctness is not self-contained** -- it is safe only because `#477`&#x27;s read-arm phase test stops a stray read moving the phase off CMDOUT mid-CDB.  **A LIVE L4 IN PRODUCT CODE:** `#477`&#x27;s objection cites the `default:` len-3/8 arm as its example, and `#479` retired that example with `!badlen_this` **in a comment block fifteen lines below it in the same file**.  The warrant was already dead of a second cause and its text was never updated -- the second time this campaign has been bitten by a warrant a later round retired.  **AND IT CAUGHT A LIVE HAZARD ON THIS MACHINE**: `/tmp/gxsec-build`, the WSL arc tree, held a PRE-`#494` `dev_wdc.c` while `build/`, `build-asan/` and `est/` all carried the fix.  A scratch clone taken from it would have measured `#494`-less code in the file under review and `cmp`-ed clean against its own donor.  Propagated and rebuilt with the two-part clearance run; filed as `novpadfourth`.  It also proved SEC moving under its run was behaviourally null rather than asserting it -- compiled both sources with identical flags and found the objects differ in exactly three `push $&lt;line&gt;` constants, each +9, with `.rodata` identical and a negative control showing the comparison CAN fail. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, MEASURING seat -- 32 artifacts at regress/artifacts/wdcaddtoinbufbound/rung3_20260830/, each sentinel-verified before copying</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered at length against the sources.  Its output streams reasoning, so the answer sits at the end. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Confirmed BOTH halves of the reframing against the tree: the primitive is bounded, and the earlier claim that it was unbounded was retired by `#484`.  That is the correction the brief leads with, checked by a file-reading seat rather than accepted. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Began from the repo and the inlined premises rather than the brief&#x27;s framing, explicitly checking the write-arm path and the four option-set candidates against code.  **Sat at 552 bytes across three samples -- the stall signature exactly -- while its process was alive and reading files**, the same false alarm it produced on the previous panel.  Sample the PROCESS, not the size. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Opened by verifying the brief&#x27;s premises against `dev_wdc.c` and the ledger before answering, and worked the whole write-arm path in the tree.  Its reasoning is visible in the transcript as it happens. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** FOUR DEFECTS FROM THE PACKET&#x27;S OWN INTERNAL CONSISTENCY, AND ONE WOULD HAVE LET A WRONG FIX SHIP. ***  Its Q2 finding is the sharpest in the panel: **the defusal holds IFF the fix keys on `atapi_phase`.**  `#477`&#x27;s hazard lives at occupancy-12, which is CMDOUT -- where the `PHASE_CMDOUT` conjunct is SATISFIED and therefore blocks nothing.  A phase-keyed guard never suppresses in CMDOUT, so it is doubly safe.  **But a FLAG-keyed guard -- `atapi_cmd_in_progress &amp;&amp; !write_in_progress`, the diagnostic&#x27;s own predicate -- matches CDB collection too, re-enters that state, and re-arms #477&#x27;s objection with the conjunct no help at all.**  So the fix must ADD a phase read: the very variable the brief says is read nowhere on that path.  It said plainly the panel must not let an implementer pick the flag-keyed form.  Three witness holes, each with its control: a ONCE-LATCHED decline passes a witness that injects once (inject twice); a decline keyed on ring-occupancy or `atapi_len &gt; 0` passes a witness that only ever injects into a non-empty ring (inject into an empty one); and whichever store WIDTH the run settles on, the other is a hole (cover 2-byte and two 1-byte).  Also: Q1&#x27;s option set omits accept-and-re-pin, and Q5&#x27;s join justification is contradicted by the brief&#x27;s own arithmetic. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Refused ratification on scope rather than on mechanism: the core defect is READ and the option set largely complete, but **Q2&#x27;s transferred warrant is not settled from the packet** -- converging with glm from a different direction -- Q3&#x27;s mutant is trivial, and **Q4&#x27;s weakest control is the unproven 2-byte store width**, which is the same hole glm named third.  Short and correctly scoped; it named what it could not settle instead of guessing. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Verified the chain SSC premises -&gt; SSD mechanism -&gt; SSC2 oracle-correction rule does not contradict itself, then named two gaps the brief itself flags, one option-set refinement, and a Q2 caveat that survives its reading -- the third seat to land on Q2 independently. — evidence: [panel_20260830_095837]  wdcaddtoinbufbound pass 1, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Went clause by clause and ruled the floor MET on all six, then named what was still owed.  **Its blocker R2 was a records defect I had committed inside this round: `shipped.diff` was rewritten IN PLACE after the table consumed it**, so the artifact dir cited a run whose input existed nowhere durable.  It named the staged blob and the two timestamps; the as-run bytes were recovered from that blob and both versions now sit here with `PROVENANCE.txt`.  **A3 is the finding that saved a row from being built: the `#477` dependency ALREADY has a defender** -- `pkt_cdb_case(&quot;a stray read does not wedge the CDB&quot;)`, whose own comment calls it THE SOLE CARRIER of the #477 read-arm guard -- and it stays the defender post-#495 by two independent conjuncts.  So no row is owed; one record sentence is.  A4: ruled the un-taken announce option must be recorded as a DECISION rather than an oversight, and supplied the affirmative warrant for silence that the round had not written: the guard&#x27;s firing domain includes DATAOUT, where an announce would print a FALSE sentence and **burn the N=1 latch** so a later genuine injection is announced to nobody.  A5: ratified `med` and required the old lifetime-hazard rider be reconciled rather than left unanswered. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** FOUND FOUR MORE ESCAPES AND A DEFECT IN THE SCORING ITSELF.  Badge A: it authored the pass-1 mutants AND the witness this round ships, and its headline finding is a defect in ITS OWN M3. ***  **E1 `&amp;&amp; len == 2` -- the MIRROR of its own M3.**  `len == 1` reds 3 rows; `len == 2`, one character away, sweeps clean, because the probe injects with a 2-byte store.  **The round measured the inert member of the family and concluded the family was covered.**  Reproduced independently here at 22/22; repaired by making the second injection a SINGLE byte, which closes the family with one instruction.  **E2 `atapi_cmd_in_progress` -&gt; `atapi_st != NULL` -- and it MEASURED the damage.**  Post-packet push/pop reads `5a,a5` shipped versus `ff,ff` swapped.  `atapi_st` is allocated at first dispatch and never nulled, so after one command the swap refuses every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device.  Correctly ruled NOT a clause-(d) breach by the DEPENDS-ON letter and filed as `wdcatapistswap`.  **E3** reproduced the M5 breach independently.  **E4** exposed that every probe here has only a little-endian leg, so the BE half of every guard in that switch is unpinned by construction -- filed as `wdcprobeendian`.  *** AND THE FINDING IT DID NOT GO LOOKING FOR: THE SCORER CANNOT TELL A KILL FROM A NON-MEASUREMENT. ***  Three of its seven runs were scored `KILLED (20 red)` while measuring nothing -- `words 0 of 46`, pc already at HALT, 0.00 s, **on both legs including the unmutated base that had scored clean minutes earlier**.  Re-run quietly, two ESCAPED.  **Every one carried the completion sentinel**, which proves the probe finished writing and not that it measured.  It named the discriminator correctly and corrected its own first attempt at it: **the BASE LEG, not a threshold** -- a threshold would have rejected M7, whose mutant leg legitimately kills the guest.  Cause: this loop ran a second table concurrently, and it noted the `#489` precedent covers TREE SAFETY, not MEASUREMENT VALIDITY.  It also measured the `#477` coupling to run PROTECTIVE rather than harmful -- removing that test breaks the CDB dispatch by itself, and `#495` makes the failure safer and louder -- so the shipped comment&#x27;s direction is backwards (L4).  And it caught that the `else if` skips the `default:` arm, losing `#337`/`#478`&#x27;s latched diagnostic for 3- and 8-byte stores, then checked both consumers and found them provably inert in the guard&#x27;s own state. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered at length against the sources. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered on the packet and the tree. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Started from the repo rather than the brief&#x27;s framing.  **Sat at ~500 bytes across three samples for the third panel running -- the stall signature -- while alive and reading files.**  Sample the PROCESS, not the size. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Verified the premises against the tree before answering, working the write-arm path and the phase constants directly. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **Predicted E1 from the packet alone** -- *a fifth mutant that escapes by the kill table&#x27;s own logic, `&amp;&amp; len == 2`, one character from a mutant already run* -- and it was measured escaping at 22/22.  Also: the `#477` dependency is memory-safety critical and enforced only by prose; the C2 deliverable is absent entirely; and the refusal set is wider than the diff&#x27;s hardware rationale defends, which is the DATAOUT over-refusal. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **Named the M5 breach and said plainly it was NOT CHECKABLE FROM THE PACKET.**  It was checkable here and it was right: `== PHASE_DATAIN` passes every row because the witness injects during DATAIN.  That honesty is the useful part -- it marked its own claim&#x27;s evidential status instead of asserting it.  Also proposed a De Morgan rewrite of the empty block (declined: the empty form is what measured 20/20) and ruled the C2 no-row sentence sufficient. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **Caught the DATAOUT over-refusal**: `!= PHASE_CMDOUT` also covers PHASE_DATAOUT, where a guest legitimately drives host-to-device bytes.  Verified inert today -- the constant is assigned at one site that latches a not-implemented warning and is read nowhere else -- but a forward hazard for whoever implements it, since the witness walks only DATA-IN and would stay green.  Recorded in the shipped comment. — evidence: [panel_20260830_105834]  #495 pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcrearmwalk</span><span class="ttl">wdcrearmwalk  the #473 detector pins the FIRST re-arm only; a transfer crossing several chunk boundaries is never walked end to end</span><span class="tip">wdcrearmwalk  the #473 detector pins the FIRST re-arm only; a transfer crossing several chunk boundaries is never walked end to end — witness: none — note: Filed by `#473` and named in that detector&#x27;s clause-(e) contract.
