"c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ghostround</span><span class="ttl">A round can ship without writing back, and no gate can see it</span><span class="tip">A round can ship without writing back, and no gate can see it — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** RULED THE ROUND ALREADY RAN, AND RULED AGAINST ITS OWN RECOMMENDATION IN THE SAME ANSWER. ***  Reconstructed the chronology from the repo: the 10:36 matrix, then `#479` at 12:18 shipping F-narrow, `#480`&#x27;s L2 call-site fix, `#481` closing the ALT-CTRL gap, `#482` splitting the joint row into four one-tenant rows.  A3: **the standing RED row it had itself recommended is DEAD** -- &#x27;`a permanently red row is the dual of this project&#x27;s check-that-cannot-fail -- a check that cannot PASS`&#x27;, and it violates the blanks rule since a deliberate red and a regression red render identically.  `#481` closed the gap instead, which is better.  It gave the disclosure ladder in order: close the gap; else an accept/tripwire row pinning today&#x27;s behaviour GREEN; else prose plus a FILED item.  A2: **corrected MY premise** -- `#492` did NOT touch `dev_wdc.c` (`git show d9efd33 -- src/devices/dev_wdc.c` is empty); it touched a checker.  It then checked the interaction precisely and found the two detectors cover DISJOINT mutant classes, so both are needed.  A5: the witness string is defective THREE ways -- a stale count, `probe:` where `detector:` was adopted for exactly this shape (the mis-scoring `check_witness.py`&#x27;s own header calls laundering), and the rung-3 accept probe owed its own clause.  It required PER-ROW CARRIER NAMING or three closures are mutually indistinguishable, which is the overtaken-row rule&#x27;s condition 2 unmet.  It named the structural remedy that was adopted: a BRIEF_TEMPLATE settled-premise line, because the carrier&#x27;s own finding is that the prose which fires is the prose in the artifact present at the moment of decision. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** CONFIRMED THE GHOST ROUND AND THEN FOUND A LIVE HIGH-SEVERITY DEFECT THE PANEL WAS NOT CONVENED FOR. ***  Settled the premise by `git log -S&#x27;wdc_new_command_reset&#x27;`, quoted the shipped helper verbatim, and named both preserved kill tables (13/13 and 10/10).  Then MEASURED nine mutants in a scratch tree at 128 rows baseline: SEVEN KILLED including the per-SITE partial resets (`MdAltRingOnly`, `MdAltWipOnly`, `MdAltNoRing` -- the ALT-CTRL site resetting a strict subset) and `MdNoFlag`, which drops `atapi_cmd_in_progress`, **a fourth tenant NEITHER shipped table enumerates**, and still dies 6 red.  TWO ESCAPED AND IT PROVED THEM EQUIVALENT RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT: a positional differential showed `MdRingDrain`/`MdRingRewind` leave head/tail at 777 vs 0 while occupancy, capacity and readback are byte-identical -- the inbuf is a correct circular buffer, so position is unobservable.  **It then drew the consequence against the anchor rule: clause (f)&#x27;s reviewer check answers NONE for the ring and that is CORRECT here, because nothing in the device can observe the shift; an anchored ring row would be the addition licence&#x27;s &#x27;it cannot hurt&#x27;.**  THE FINDING THAT BECAME A ROW: `#481` widened offset-1 aliasing from a harmless signature latch into a FULL SOFT RESET.  `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)` reads `relative_addr` ZERO times over a TWO-BYTE region, so a guest write to `0x3f7` -- the READ-ONLY Drive Address Register -- aborts a live ATA write and purges 40 queued bytes.  **#481&#x27;s OWN committed artifact already measured it at rung 3** and no row carried it.  Filed as `wdcaltoffset`, sev=high.  Also: the kill drivers score a CRASH as a result -- `build_run` checks the COMPILE status, then runs the mutant and greps FAIL lines without reading the run&#x27;s exit status or the `WDC_IDENTIFY_PASS` sentinel, so a mutant that dies partway is scored by how far it got.  Not hypothetical here: `write_in_progress` and `write_count` are set together and CLEARED AT DIFFERENT SITES, and the drain guard divides by `512 * d-&gt;write_count` -- an operand swap leaving the flag while zeroing the count is a SIGFPE.  **It disclosed its own violation**: wrote one file into the tree against instruction, removed it, and verified `git status` and the `dev_wdc.c` md5 afterwards. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Caught it independently while grepping the ledger, and surfaced `wdcaltwidth` -- an OPEN row with ZERO entries about the alt-ctrl register&#x27;s access width -- which is what made me check whether the &#x27;ALT-CTRL gap&#x27; was already filed under another name.  That check is what kept the round from filing one defect twice.  Its wall grep flagged it as walled and it was a FALSE POSITIVE: every match was codex QUOTING ledger rows whose notes record old seat-wall history. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Caught it, treating F-narrow as &#x27;the historical Pass 1 context (#479)&#x27; rather than as a live proposal. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Caught it, and corrected a SECOND premise of mine in the same breath: **&#x27;Two rounds have shipped since&#x27; is false as a bound** -- after the matrix came #479, #480, #481, #482 and more.  It opened by pinning session start to HEAD `d9efd33` before answering anything. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Caught it, and its reasoning is visible in the transcript as it happens: &#x27;the fix `wdc_new_command_reset()` is ALREADY IN THE TREE -- this is #479/#480/#481 ... So the tree ALREADY contains the fix?  That would mean this round&#x27;s work is already done.&#x27; — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** COULD NOT CATCH THE GHOST ROUND, AND THAT IS NOT A SEAT FAILURE. ***  A ghost round is a REPO FACT, and a packet-fed seat can only know what the brief inlines -- the brief asserted &#x27;PASS 1, before any edit&#x27; and it could not check that.  Recording this as a failure would be exactly the conflation the pipeline doctrine forbids. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — As glm: structurally unable to settle a repo fact from a packet.  Its answer is about the design as briefed, and is sound on that scope. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — As glm and deepseek.  **The three packet-fed seats failing to catch this, while all four file-reading seats caught it, is the FILE-READING vs PACKET-FED distinction confirmed by measurement rather than asserted** -- and it is the clearest instance this project has recorded. — evidence: [panel_20260829_213915]  ghost-round panel, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">