regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\grok_witness_20260908.txt:1:I'll read the Alpha PAL, dyntrans, machine, and existing probe sources first so the witness design is grounded in how this tree actually runs, not in the previous pass's claims.The defect site and the two Alpha probes are the next reads; I’ll also pull dyntrans invalidation and how alphabook1 maps memory so the witness can actually cache a VPN.PAL dispatch is confirmed; next I’ll read how VADDR vs ALL invalidation actually matches entries, and how Alpha load/store fills and consumes the dyntrans TLB.A rung-3 guest witness **is constructible** on unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`. The observable is a **stale dyntrans hit after a PTE remap**, not a dispatch spy. Section 1 is right on the defect; two extra constraints decide whether the row is real or vacuous.
regress\artifacts\alphatbinegsel\grok_witness_20260908.txt:29:`swpctx` is the only writer of `apcb_ptbr`, and it already ALL-flushes (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c`). After that, the first walked `ldq` of the test VPN misses (`host_load` NULL), goes through `memory_rw` → `alpha_translate_v2p` → `update_translation_table`, which writes `l3->host_load[x3]`. Alpha RAM is `DM_DYNTRANS_OK | DM_EMULATED_RAM`; `MEMORY_NOT_FULL_PAGE` is 256, not 2, so a translate result of 2 is cacheable. `alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py` already measured that the second access is a fast-path hit. 128 TLB slots (`ALPHA_MAX_VPH_TLB_ENTRIES`); sequential replacement will not evict the handful of pages this program touches.
