"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">otherkilldeath</span><span class="ttl">sgi_re_width_probe.py&#x27;s `other kill` row is a DEATH-ONLY oracle -- a one-character change to the text it claims to pin scores 35/35, MEASURED</span><span class="tip">sgi_re_width_probe.py&#x27;s `other kill` row is a DEATH-ONLY oracle -- a one-character change to the text it claims to pin scores 35/35, MEASURED — witness: harness:MEASURED by the #497 pass-2 seat in its own clone, with the prediction filed BEFORE building.  base 35/35 CONTROL=OK; x_othertext (catch-all message text altered, exit(1) untouched) 35/35 with ZERO red rows; x_readtext (one read arm&#x27;s text altered) 34/35, reddening `tlb_a ld kill got=exit1-txt want=exit1+txt`.  The matched pair is the point: it proves the six siblings ARE failable and this one is not.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">holdphase</span><span class="ttl">the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker</span><span class="tip">the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker — witness: harness:MEASURED by the codex and opus5 seats on the #497b pass-2 panel, independently, and confirmed here row by row before reverting.  SIX markers were wrongly cleared, ALL of them phase=regress / seat=fable5: ns16550, carrier, es438, sh4sci, wdcdataoutdrain, and wdccdbmidxfer entry [26].  *** THIS FIELD PREVIOUSLY NAMED THE SIXTH AS &#x27;research/opus5, answered at review&#x27;.  THAT IS THE MARKER THE COMMIT **KEPT** -- disposition (b) -- NOT the one it cleared.  Caught by the fable5 seat; I had copied the framing from another seat&#x27;s table without checking which entry it pointed at. ***  widthkillclass was the one CORRECT clearance: held at review, all seven named seats answered at review.  A strictly-later + same-stage test keeps all six and clears that one.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gxprobepccache</span><span class="ttl">gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail</span><span class="tip">gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail — witness: harness:MEASURED by the #497-family measuring seat and re-verified here.  `gxprobe.py` skips a redundant register assignment when `cache_regs` is on; `_exec_at()` pops the cached pc after stepping, but a driver calling `send(&quot;step&quot;)` DIRECTLY did not -- so its next `set_reg(&quot;pc&quot;, a)` was dropped as redundant.  The transcript is unambiguous: the first access carries a `pc=` line, the second and third do not, and they execute `nop`.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgideprimop</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);`</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);` — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/sgideprimop/witness_20260831.sh -- rung-3 on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  DE_PRIM_TRIANGLE (0x02000000) and DE_PRIM_POINT (0x00000000) each kill the host with the op echoed in the diagnostic; DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE (0x03000000) survives with zero kill messages.  The RECTANGLE control is what makes it discriminating rather than merely fatal.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION of nine dispositions, verified by opening files rather than by reasoning; it stated its line numbers matched the brief&#x27;s ranges, proving it was in the fork and not a pristine copy.  **Zero dispositions reversed on substance; two corrected.**  *** ITS CENTRAL FINDING PREVENTED THE ROUND FROM MINTING THE DEFECT IT HAD JUST FILED. ***  The natural oracle-correction for `tail_acc_x` is `exit1` -&gt; `exit0` (*the host now survives*).  Traced as a CHANNEL rather than an intent: under a future one-token death reinstatement -- `exit(0)` at the default arm -- the session announces once and dies, `how` is `exit0`, the re-pinned row reads GREEN, `tail_acc` still counts 1, no other session reaches `draw_primitive()`, and the detector scores a **TOTAL ESCAPE, 29/29**.  **Exit status cannot vary between *declined and quit* and *died with status 0*.**  Repair: re-pin onto a positive post-draw observable (a load through the device; `printed()` already reads `died`), keeping an exit-status row only as a second channel.  **L1 -- a row under construction -- so no stopping-rule tension**, and the round owes a FOURTH mutant: death-by-`exit(0)` at the default arm.  *Knowable at writing time from the CHANNEL check, which is exactly why that check runs before the table.*  **Corrected my `exit0oracle` census: 13 sites, not 9** -- my grep anchored on the variable name `how` and missed four rows using `how1`..`how4`.  **Upheld the RANKING with a measurement I did not have**: it built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found `lo_sent` KILLS it via the read-side sentinel even though all four `*_alive` rows pass -- so the blindness is per-ROW, not per-detector, and no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole.  **Re-verified F5 mechanically rather than accepting my ruling in my own favour**: `DRAW` is a PREFIX of the fatal and `(once)` appends after it, and the `tail_acc` session is a fresh process issuing exactly one draw-triggering access -- so first occurrence = only occurrence = 1, latched or not.  Keep-the-text confirmed, and it noted the current text stays TRUE post-fix (still unimplemented, now declined), so there is no wrong-record pressure to reword.  **One records correction against me on F1:** `#498`&#x27;s own probe header ALREADY carried the reset-value fact in its DOES-NOT-CATCH section.  What is new is the headline promotion and the `memset` verification a packet-fed seat&#x27;s refusal forced -- *the round should credit the header rather than book a fresh discovery*.  Also flagged two records nits, both correct: my &quot;one uncommitted edit&quot; undercounted, and `CHECKPOINT.md`&#x27;s HEAD had gone stale by its own rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — THE NINTH SEAT; this stage was held open for it.  **Q1: the precedent is not elsewhere in the tree -- it is INSIDE `draw_primitive()` itself, twice.**  The ROP `default:` arms latch-and-announce without exiting, and the XFER guard in the same function has its `exit(1)` COMMENTED OUT.  So decline + latched announce is the file&#x27;s own idiom and the fix is not an import.  It killed the other three members on facts: ACCEPT needs a computed-then-discarded value and nothing computes a POINT or TRIANGLE; FAULT is an ADDRESS-SPACE disposition and this is a command-register VALUE, with no space to leave unmapped; BACKED ACCEPT fabricates storage where there is no register.  *** Q2 -- A FIFTH MUTANT, AND IT IS A FLOOR OBLIGATION RATHER THAN COVERAGE DEBT. ***  **`M5 primdrop`: delete the `case DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE:` LABEL** so RECTANGLE falls into the de-fatalised `default:`.  One line, and plausible rather than contrived because the four documented primitives INTERLEAVE.  Not covered by M1-M4: guard intact, bit intact, `|=` intact, and NO DEATH at all -- strictly quieter than M4.  **Measured, not argued: `grep` for `PRIMITIVE|0x2060|0x01000000|0x03000000` across all three shipped probes returns ZERO hits -- not one probe in the tree programs the primitive register or issues a legal LINE or RECTANGLE**, so every row I prescribed tests only the DECLINE side and the mutant sweeps all three clean.  **Why it is FLOOR and not debt:** the de-escalation clause requires the detector shipping WITH the fix to pin the ACCEPT SIDE of each predicate the guard gated -- *part of the fix&#x27;s cost*.  **My PRETABLE section 2 enumerated five rows and not one is an accept row.**  Before the fix, deleting that label meant `exit(1)` on the guest&#x27;s first rectangle -- unmissable; after it, one `(once)` line and a console that never renders text, since the file records at `:760-761` that OpenBSD draws characters this way.  **That is the accidental tripwire the fix destroys.**  Kill channel, needing no framebuffer read-back: a legal RECTANGLE with `DE_DRAWMODE_ROP` set and an unimplemented `rop`, asserting `count(DRAW) == 0` AND `count(&quot;[ sgi_de: RECT: rop[&quot;) == 1` -- an ARM-UNIQUE anchor (verified: two hits in the file, LINE and RECT).  Caveat that decides the session shape: `rop_used` is function-static, so the anchor fires once per PROCESS.  *** AND A HAZARD IN THE VERY PRECEDENT THAT LICENSES THE FIX: COPY THE SHAPE, NOT THE SCOPE. ***  `static char rop_used[256]` and `static int first` are FUNCTION-STATIC -- process-global, shared by every device instance, the `n_trunc_msgs` anti-pattern.  Census: **4 lines, ALL in this file, ALL inside `draw_primitive()`** -- the tree&#x27;s only such sites.  `d-&gt;warned` + `(once)` is the house form.  It also recorded a tension rather than resolving it: the in-file precedent latches PER VALUE while this fix latches ONE BIT for 254 op classes, so POINT-then-TRIANGLE prints once.  Deliberate (it is M1&#x27;s killer) but **the round must say why it did not use an `(op &gt;&gt; 24)`-indexed table**, or a reader takes the difference for an oversight.  *Hygiene: it reported that the tree was committed under it twice mid-review, checked that none of the five files it cites changed, and confirmed it wrote nothing.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  **CORRECTED HALF OF MY OWN HEADLINE CLAIM.**  I recorded POINT as what *an uninitialised OR PARTIALLY-WRITTEN op register* produces.  The reset half is proven (`memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct sgi_re_data))` zeroes `de_reg[]`); **the partially-written half is FALSE** -- `#498`&#x27;s width guard declines byte and halfword accesses BEFORE `de_reg[]` is updated, so accepted 32/64-bit writes always store complete words.  *One of this round&#x27;s own fixes closed the door I was citing.*  **DID THE OPTION-SET PRECEDENT GREP the three-membered-set rule demands**, and the tree answers clearly: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` declines an unimplemented command with a per-instance `(once)` diagnostic and completes normally, `dev_m8820x.c` ignores one; `dev_asc.c` raises a guest interrupt ONLY because it has an explicitly modelled illegal-command channel, which this site does not.  That is why FAULT is wrong here -- an in-tree warrant, not an argument.  *** TWO MORE ONE-CHARACTER MUTANT CLASSES, ONE OF WHICH IS NEW TO THIS PROJECT. ***  (a) `1 &lt;&lt; 7` -&gt; `1 &lt;&lt; 6` aliases an existing bit -- the `#498` collision class, killed by pre-arming all seven existing latch bits in one session before triggering.  (b) **`d-&gt;warned |= SGIRE_W_PRIMOP` -&gt; `d-&gt;warned = SGIRE_W_PRIMOP`, one character, which CLEARS EVERY EARLIER LATCH** -- killed by an A -&gt; primitive -&gt; A session requiring A&#x27;s message exactly once.  It proposes combining them: fire all seven latch sites, trigger POINT, revisit the sites, and require every message exactly once.  *** AND A VACUITY FINDING AGAINST SHIPPED MATERIAL: `exit0` IS NOT A SAFE SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  *&quot;A wrong `exit(0)` still kills the emulator and satisfies that oracle.&quot;*  `#498`&#x27;s `hi_alive`/`lo_alive`/`lo_alive2` rows all pin `how == &quot;exit0&quot;`.  Assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally with a post-trigger command.  Filed as its own row -- it is coverage debt against a shipped detector above the floor, not an L2.  **GUEST-CONTROL TRACE, concrete:** RE base `0x15000000`, DE mapping `0x15002000`, `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` offset `0x60`, START alias `0x60 | 0x800 = 0x860`, so a guest `sw` to **`0xffffffffb5002860`** with `t0 = 0` draws a POINT.  `startFlag` captures bit `0x800` and is cleared; `regnr = 0x60/4`.  **And `if (startFlag)` is NOT conditioned on `MEM_WRITE`, so a START-aliased READ also invokes drawing** -- worth a row of its own.  Notes that `break`-inside-latch is a non-mutant here: `default` is last and the function has no tail, so moving or deleting that `break` changes nothing. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Checked the tree rather than the packet and said so: noticed HEAD was two commits past the brief&#x27;s `e898c84`, read the queue row, and confirmed **nothing has shipped for this row** -- the same premise error the measuring seat caught on the sibling round.  Independently confirmed the mask/constant table, the no-common-tail claim (*&quot;the switch&#x27;s closing brace is the last statement&quot;*), that `op` is guest-controlled through `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` and *is already DE_PRIM_POINT at RESET with no store*, and that bits [31:26] are unused by every `DE_PRIM_` constant so `DE_PRIM_LINE | 0x04000000` dies.  Chose **decline + latched announce**, not FAULT, and ruled the caller should NOT see the decline.  *** ITS BEST FINDING IS A THIRD BLAST-RADIUS TIER THAT BOTH I AND THE PACKET-FED SEATS MISSED. ***  I had corrected the radius to *one certain row (tail_acc_x) and one conditional on wording (tail_acc)*.  grok added: **a REWORD does not RED `hi_draw`/`hi_rd_draw` -- it BLINDS them.**  Both assert `count(DRAW) == 0`, so once the substring no longer exists they stay green *for the wrong reason* -- vacuous by construction, invisible to every gate.  It also caught that my `gate_arm.sh` comment said *both rows* red, *&quot;That comment overstates the same way the brief does&quot;* -- an L4 in shipped material, corrected in place.  **The design constraint that follows is sharper than mine: KEEP THE MESSAGE TEXT and add `(once)`.**  Keeping it costs ONE re-pin; rewording costs two re-pins AND silently blinds two further rows.  Disclosed honestly that it did not re-run the probe -- *&quot;this is a read of the live oracles against the code paths they exercise&quot;* -- which is the READING/MEASUREMENT distinction stated without being asked. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING; went to the tree for everything it could not settle and said which.  *** CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT IN MY BRIEF: I INLINED THE SUPERSEDED LATCH CENSUS. ***  My settled-premises block gave `(once)` 9, `(further occurrences suppressed)` 3, `(reported once)` 2.  **Those are the 2026-08-26 literals that the carrier itself DEMOTES in writing** -- *&quot;Superseded literals, kept only as the demoted record ... Never a current claim&quot;* -- re-derived on 08-30 as 24/4/5.  kimi measured 25/4/5 and I re-ran the carrier&#x27;s own deriver: **25 / 4 / 5**, confirming kimi and refuting my brief.  *The carrier names a deriver precisely so nobody transcribes the number, and I transcribed the one it had crossed out.*  The count grew 24 -&gt; 25 because THIS session&#x27;s `#498` added a `(once)` site -- the rounds that consult the number are the rounds that change it, which is the carrier&#x27;s stated reason for the deriver.  **Its conclusion is unaffected and strengthened: `(once)` is not merely the plurality but the overwhelming majority.**  *** ITS SECOND CORRECTION IS WRONG, AND CHECKING IT VINDICATED THE CARRIER. ***  kimi reports that the tree&#x27;s counter-style outlier *&quot;lives in `src/core/memory.c`&quot;* and that it saw **no counter** there, only a verbosity gate with a probe-tolerance early-out.  Measured: `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` DOES carry `static int n_trunc_msgs = 0;` with `n_trunc_msgs &lt; 8` and `n_trunc_msgs ++` -- a real counter, exactly as the carrier describes.  **kimi found a DIFFERENT diagnostic** -- `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr()` in `core/memory.c`, which `memory_rw.c` calls and which genuinely has no counter.  Two similar-looking diagnostics in one area; it searched for the described behaviour and landed on the wrong one.  *Recorded rather than quietly dropped: a seat&#x27;s correction carries authority, and this one would have sent a future round to rewrite an accurate carrier paragraph.*  *** AND A THIRD Q4 OBSERVER THAT grok AND codex BOTH MISSED: THE HARDCODED ROW DENOMINATOR. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` checks `sgi_de rows green` against a literal `29`.  When `tail_acc_x` reds, that count falls to 28 and **the denominator check reds too** -- so the oracle-correction re-pin must bump the literal in the same edit, or the round meets two reds and attributes one of them wrongly.  Neither other file-reading seat named it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST FINDING AND IT LANDS AGAINST THE AUTHOR: the 2-of-4 reframing is a PARTITION of the 254/256 claim, not a correction, and recording only the sharper half is the locally-true restatement that hides the defect. ***  Also asked the one checkable caveat nobody else did -- are op bits [31:26] live? -- which on checking WIDENED the defect: the primitive field is bits [25:24] and the mask is [31:24], so a legal LINE or RECTANGLE with a stray high bit also dies.  Named four premises as not settleable from the packet, each with the single fact that would settle it, and separated the all-zeroes CONSTANT from the register&#x27;s RESET VALUE. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Supplied the clause-(d) mutant BEFORE the fix exists -- drop the `!` from the guard, so the latch never sets and the announce never fires while the bit name and the absent exit(1) both still read correct -- AND the row that kills it: two distinct unimplemented primitives in one session, expecting exactly one message.  Precision correction: 254/256 counts MASKED TOP-BYTE CLASSES, not full 32-bit op values. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Refused the no-common-tail premise and answered CONDITIONALLY ON BOTH BRANCHES, naming #498&#x27;s `goto`-to-reach-the-tail as the governing precedent if a tail exists.  Marked the uninitialised-register inference as REASONED rather than READ -- the distinction the author had blurred. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pcc2widthkills</span><span class="ttl">dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer)</span><span class="tip">dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer) — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/pcc2widthkills/witness_20260831.sh -- a RUNG-3 witness. Real m88k guest instructions through real decode on unmodified `-E mvme88k -e mvme187`: the trace shows `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` followed by `TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_SCCERR`.  Built by the kimi seat, REPRODUCED independently by the coordinator with a `put b` negative control and a `put w` positive control.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Reached the 5-of-10 split independently of kimi, from the alignment rule plus the fact that `dev_pcc2_access` switches on the transaction&#x27;s STARTING offset.  **Refuted my settled premise 5**: a `memcpy` runs BEFORE the switch on the read path, so these are not value-declines at all -- in-range wide reads return the register span and end-crossing reads are zero-filled. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, and it settled the question that decides whether this row is a defect or documentation -- **against me, and in the direction that makes the row WORSE**.  *** Q1: WIDE ACCESS IS REACHABLE.  My premise was that these are byte-wide control registers and the CPU or bus glue might be unable to deliver a 2- or 4-byte access at all.  Refuted twice over, both verified here. ***  **(a) The tree&#x27;s OWN header documents the legality of what is being killed.**  `src/include/thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h:9-10`: *&quot;PCCchip2 control and status register can be accessed as bytes (8 bits), two-bytes (16 bits), or four-bytes (32 bits).&quot;*  So **byte-wide is a property of this MODEL&#x27;s `switch (relative_addr)`, not of the chip the header describes** -- the guards kill on accesses the hardware documentation in this very tree says are legal.  **(b) The CPU delivers them.**  `src/cpus/cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c` is compiled for `LS_SIZE` 1, 2, 4 and 8, declares `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE]` and passes that size to `cpu-&gt;memory_rw`, faulting on misalignment rather than narrowing the access.  So `len` in {1,2,4,8} reaches the device.  *This is the third time tonight a seat has refuted a premise of mine, and the second where the refutation RAISED severity rather than lowering it.*  **Q2: DO NOT convert `#119`&#x27;s two width latches in this round** -- that belongs to `staticlatchscope`.  A clean scoping answer to a question I had genuinely not decided.  **Q3: A RUNG-3 PROBE IS POSSIBLE, with an address**: the debugger&#x27;s `put h` / `put w` reaches `dev_pcc2_access` at `0xfff42000 + off`.  That discharges the attempt this row owes with a concrete invocation rather than a maybe.  **Q4: the smallest survivors of a *host-lived + message-appeared* detector are a SHARED LATCH BIT across the twelve arms, and WARN-THEN-FALL-THROUGH** -- i.e. the detector must pin per-arm identity and the refusal&#x27;s EFFECT, not merely that something was announced. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** STOPPED READING AND MEASURED.  Killed the host twice on the unmodified tree -- once by direct `put w`, once through REAL GUEST DECODE (hand-assembled m88k words, pc=0x1000, step 4), with the trace showing `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` then the SCCERR kill.  Reproduced independently by the coordinator. ***  Also found the probe-authorship trap that outlives this row -- **`put b` never reaches the device** (0 messages vs 1 for `put w`), because the debugger&#x27;s byte path passes NO_EXCEPTIONS and `memory_rw` skips the device call for DM_DEFAULT devices -- and measured that a wide read from an UNGUARDED base spanning guarded registers is ALREADY ACCEPTED today. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE PANEL&#x27;S BEST ANSWER AND ITS THREE CONCLUSIONS SPLIT THREE WAYS, which is the argument for auditing premises rather than only derivations. ***  (a) Refused my unshown claim that `fatal()` returns and named its body as the settling fact -- CHECKED, it returns, so my premise was right and unsupported.  (b) Challenged `grep -c &#x27;^ *exit(1);&#x27;` as blind to other spellings -- the count held at 13, **but the four extra `exit(` hits are `#119`&#x27;s own comments, which is how the fourfold in-file precedent was found at all**.  (c) REFUTED my Q3 premise outright: I claimed the read path assigns `odata`; it appears twice in the file and both are in a DIFFERENT access function, so a declined read returns the caller&#x27;s pre-set value and the ACCEPT option&#x27;s premise is false here. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Ended on the point that matters for the detector: a shared message format across twelve arms makes it easy to keep the message while changing the behaviour -- i.e. rows that check only *a message appeared* are the ones a mutant will walk through. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Landed on the same conclusion I reached from the source: the in-file precedent&#x27;s shape is right and its latching is not.  Flagged the unlatched announce as a defect of the precedent rather than a feature to copy. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lcabadval</span><span class="ttl">dev_lca.c: FOUR chipset registers kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set -- the #443 BADVAL class</span><span class="tip">dev_lca.c: FOUR chipset registers kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set -- the #443 BADVAL class — witness: harness:ENUMERATED from the shipped source, 2026-08-31.  Eleven live terminating calls.  The value guards, each ending in exit(1): `idata &amp; ~1` (:206), `idata &amp; ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` (:226), `idata != 0ULL &amp;&amp; idata != 0x300800000ULL` (:241), `idata != 0x700000ULL` (:256), `idata != 0x240000000ULL` (:280), `idata != 0x3ff00000ULL` (:295).  Each diagnostic says so in its own words -- *&quot;(0x%&quot; PRIx64&quot;) from the only implemented value&quot;*.  Plus two range guards that also kill: `dev &gt;= 21` (:129) and `ofs &gt; len` (:163).  NO rung-3 witness: READ, not booted.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: codex, grok, kimi, opus5, fable5]  Three packet-fed seats answered and settled what a packet can settle -- the accept-set census, and (once I checked it) which fields are consumed.  The rest needs the tree. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST ANSWER.  Refuted the row&#x27;s own title without needing the one fact it could not settle, and caught my brief contradicting itself between Q2 and Q3 -- do these fields feed decode or does the emulator not model them?  Both were asserted. ***  The source settles it against me: base/mask/tlb_enable are written and read back and nothing else; only `ioc_conf` is consumed (`bus = d-&gt;ioc_conf &amp; 1`).  **That splits the fix design** -- three arms are pure storage where a decline may keep the store, and the fourth is not.  Also the sharpest design point of the panel: for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the MAXIMALLY ORDINARY write, so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one.  Named `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` as the single fact that would close the census, which is exactly the right move for a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Derived the accept sets independently and reached the same correction.  Held the line on reachability: named an LCA IOC register specification or PROM source as the one thing that would settle whether these are hardware-legal values or merely the ones this emulator was developed against.  There is no such document in `_scratchpad/`. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Went arm by arm and attached a single named settling fact to each rather than answering around the gaps -- the discipline this project asks of a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row&#x27;s own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm&#x27;s text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth&#x27;s printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger&#x27;s earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four. — evidence: [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — RUNG-3 ATTEMPT: MADE 2026-08-31 per the owner ruling, by a reading seat.  *** RESULT: A WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE AND A FULL SEQUENCE IS SPECIFIED -- so this row is NOT documented-only, and `none&#x27; would have meant `did not think to ask&#x27;. ***  Target `-E alpha -e alphabook1 0x4000:/dev/null&#x27;; machine_alpha.c&#x27;s ST_ALPHABOOK1 is the ONLY subtype calling device_add(machine,&quot;lca&quot;).  Construction and debugger-prompt arrival are ALREADY MEASURED elsewhere in this repo -- CHANGELOG #279&#x27;s blast-radius gate lists alphabook1 among the twelve machines reaching the prompt, and gate_asan_sweep.sh&#x27;s AB_EXEMPT lists alpha/3000/300 and NOT alphabook1.  Guest VA is K0SEG (paddr | 0xfffffc0000000000, memory_alpha.c), with the ptbr==0 identity path as a second route.  A guest `stq a1,0(a0)&#x27; = 0xB6300000 reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) via LS_GENERIC_N -&gt; memory_rw.
regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\codex_assess_20260908.err:167:.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\codex.err:5459:tools/pipeline/matrix.html:3984:construction rather than by an oracle.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** THIS ITEM NOW ARRIVES WITH ITS ROWS ALREADY BUILT AND MEASURED, which changes it from a hunt into an implementation. ***  E2 (`|=` -&gt; `=` on a latch) and E3 (widened guard) are STATEMENT defects, not constant-expression ones -- so unlike E1 they cannot be closed by a declaration, and the seat gave the reason: no constant expression can observe a statement, so the by-construction route is exhausted at E1.  A latch HELPER MACRO does not close E2 either: a macro-level `=` still escapes all 20 rows, because every row exercises one latch in isolation.  MEASURED against the ask-again&#x27;s surviving binaries, one frozen instrument: base 22/22 (negative control -- neither row manufactures a failure), `E2_assignA` 21/22 with `latch keep` moving (got 2, want 1), `E3_widenA` 21/22 with the two-site row moving (got 0,1 want 1,1).  Both had previously escaped at 20/20, and the two are ORTHOGONAL -- each is green under the other&#x27;s mutant.  `latch keep` = TOC_SHORT + BAD_FMT + TOC_SHORT, counting the #489 TOC diagnostic; its channel is a latch OTHER THAN the one Site A owns, which is exactly why all 20 shipped rows were blind.  The two-site row = BAD_CMD + BAD_FMT: both existing two-site rows fix FMT-then-CMD, where `warned` is 0 at Site A, so a widened guard is indistinguishable -- ONE REVERSED SESSION covers the whole order-asymmetry class.  *** RENAME BEFORE SHIPPING: the seat&#x27;s own name `both sites rev` is exactly 14 chars = the gate&#x27;s column width, so it renders with a trailing space and the gate anchor would miss it.  Use `rev sites`.  The seat found this by noticing its own summary table printed blank for that row while the FAIL list caught it. ***  NOT added to #490: E2/E3 were adjudicated coverage debt rather than floor breaches, and 20 -&gt; 22 rows moves every score in a 13-build table.  More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds. — evidence: [panel_20260829_133206]  measured, supp_p2_20260829/probe_{base,E2_assignA,E3_widenA}.log</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">batteryblindstart</span><span class="ttl">The battery&#x27;s pre-flight git status is a multi-minute BLIND WINDOW</span><span class="tip">The battery&#x27;s pre-flight git status is a multi-minute BLIND WINDOW — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td clas