regress\artifacts\pcc2defaultkill\pass2_seats\ollama_glm.burn.txt:1:{"model": "glm-5.3", "created_at": "2026-09-05T11:03:03.773130624Z", "response": "", "thinking": "Let me work through this carefully. I'm a packet-fed seat: I cannot read files, run commands, or check the tree. My job is to audit the premises as stated in the packet, reason from the inlined text, and produce a verdict.\n\nLet me first parse what the packet claims, then check internal consistency.\n\n**The core fix (diff):**\n\nBefore:\n```c\ndefault:\n    debug(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented %s offset 0x%x\", ...);\n    if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)\n        debug(\": 0x%x\", (int)idata);\n    debug(\" ]\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n```\n\nAfter:\n```c\ndefault:\n    /* comment */\n    if (!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << relative_addr))) {\n        d->warned_unimpl |= 1ULL << relative_addr;\n        if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)\n            fatal(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented write to offset 0x%x: 0x%x -- declined.  (once) ]\\n\", ...);\n        else\n            fatal(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented read from offset 0x%x -- served 0x%02x.  (once) ]\\n\", relative_addr, d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]);\n    }\n    break;\n```\n\nWait. There's a serious issue here. The design premise 6 says: \"WRITE side member 3 -- DECLINE + latched `fatal()` `(once)`\". Hmm, \"fatal()\" in gxemul typically... Actually, in gxemul, `fatal()` is a function that prints an error message and **exits**! Let me think about this.\n\nIn gxemul source, `fatal()` is defined in `include/misc.h` or similar: it's a printf-like function that prints to stderr and calls `exit(1)`. Actually, let me recall: gxemul has `debug()` which prints only if verbose flag set, and `fatal()` which prints and exits.\n\nFrom gxemul's `src/include/misc.h`:\n```c\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...);\n```\nAnd in `misc/misc.c`:\n```c\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n    va_list ap;\n    va_start(ap, fmt);\n    vfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n    va_end(ap);\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, actually I believe gxemul's `fatal()` does exit. Let me think again. In gxemul, there are:\n- `debug()` \u2014 prints only when debugging enabled\n- `fatal()` \u2014 prints and exits with error\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident `fatal()` in gxemul calls `exit(1)`. From `src/misc/misc.c`:\n\n```c\n/*\n *  fatal():\n *\n *  Prints an error message and exits.\n */\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n\tva_list ap;\n\n\tva_start(ap, fmt);\n\tvfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n\tva_end(ap);\n\tfprintf(stderr, \"\\n\");\n\texit(1);\n}\n```\n\nWait, but actually \u2014 hold on. Let me reconsider. In gxemul, `fatal()` is used all over the place for e.g. \"unknown cpu type\" and those exit. Yes, `fatal()` exits.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the design here says \"DECLINE + latched `fatal()` `(once)`\" and \"the house `(once)` shape\". Hmm, maybe this fork has a different fatal() \u2014 maybe fatal() in this fork prints and does NOT exit? The phrase \"the house `(once)` shape\" suggests there's a house pattern of \"latched fatal() (once)\". If fatal() exited, then the \"decline\" wouldn't decline \u2014 it would kill the host on the FIRST write (though latched, so only once... but the process would already be dead).\n\nHmm, but the witness shows post-fix: \"ieicr st.b survived\" and \"ieicr st.b msg 1\" \u2014 the message was printed AND the guest survived. So in this fork, `fatal()` evidently does NOT exit \u2014 or the fork changed fatal() to not exit, or there's a `fatal()` variant.\n\nHmm wait. Actually, let me reconsider. In gxemul, I recall:\n\n```c\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n    ...\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but the witness claims post-fix host SURVIVED a guest st.b to IEICR with the \"unimplemented write ... declined. (once)\" line printed. If fatal() exits, the host would exit(1) and the witness row would be \"host exit1\", not \"survived\". The witness post-fix says survived. So either:\n(a) fatal() in this fork doesn't exit (was changed), 