2\u226023. So the prediction's arithmetic is off by one somewhere (16 red + 7 green = 23, but they wrote 8/23 and \"fifteen\"). The measured 7/23 matches the named 16-red list exactly. So arguably the prediction's LIST matches the measurement, and only the count \"8/23\"/\"fifteen\" is a slip. Hmm, interesting. So M_EXIT: predicted 8/23 but enumerated 16 reds; measured 7/23 = 16 reds; the enumerated set matches exactly. So the \"contradiction\" is arithmetic vs enumeration within the prediction itself, resolved in favor of enumeration by the measurement. Not a substantive unsafe miss.\n\nLet me check the other mutants:\n\nM_UNLATCH: predicted 21/23 red: latch sameoff 2, latch rw one 2. Measured 21/23: latch sameoff, latch rw one. Match (21 green = 2 red).\n\nM_ONELATCH: predicted 19/23 red: named wr msg 1, named rd msg 1, named rd srvd 1, latch twooff 1 \u2014 four red \u2192 19 green. Measured 19/23: named wr msg, named rd msg, named rd srvd, latch twooff. Match.\n\nM_BACKED: predicted 21/23 red: ieicr rdawr 0x5a, ieerr rdawr 0x5a. Measured 21/23: ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr. Match.\n\nM_RDKILL: predicted 18/23 red: five rows \u2014 named rd, named rd msg n/a, named rd srvd n/a, dir rd text n/a, latch rw one \u2014 that's 5 red \u2192 18 green. Measured 16/23 \u2192 7 red: named rd, named rd msg, named rd srvd, ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr, latch rw one, dir rd text. So measured has TWO MORE red than predicted: ieicr rdawr and ieerr rdawr went red (host exit on the read in that session), which the prediction missed. Predicted red set \u2282 measured red set; extras: ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr. Is that a \"safe\" miss? The rule: \"A miss in the SAFE direction is recorded; one in the UNSAFE direction (a predicted red that stays green) stops the round.\" Predicted reds that stayed green: none \u2014 all five predicted reds are in the measured red list. The extras (ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr red) are predicted-green-turned-red = safe miss, recorded. But wait \u2014 why did the prediction miss them? Under M_RDKILL (kill on reads), the rdawr sessions (store then load in ONE session) \u2014 the LOAD would kill the host. So ieicr rdawr should obviously be red under M_RDKILL. The prediction said rdawr rows green \u2014 that seems like a prediction error. Why would the predictor think ieicr rdawr survives under M_RDKILL? If M_RDKILL kills on READ accesses, the rdawr session does store (declined, announce) then load \u2192 host dies \u2192 row red. The prediction listed \"latch rw one\" as red \"(the read in that session exits: n/a)\" \u2014 acknowledging a session with read+write dies. But ieicr rdawr is also a session with a read! Inconsistent prediction: it forgot the rdawr rows. Measurement corrected it. Safe direction. Recorded, doesn't stop the round.\n\nM_DIRSWAP: predicted 20/23 red: dir wr text 0, dir rd text 0, named rd srvd 0 \u2014 three red \u2192 20 green. Measured 20/23: named rd srvd, dir wr text, dir rd text. Match.\n\nSo: predictions vs table \u2014 M_EXIT count slip (8/23 vs 7/23, enumeration matches), M_RDKILL two extra reds (safe-direction miss, consistent with the rule). No unsafe misses. Good.\n\nSecond part of Q2: does the detector pin DECLINE against BACKED ACCEPT by a channel other than survival? Yes \u2014 the read-back VALUE channel: ieicr rdawr / ieerr rdawr want 0x0 (decline) vs 0x5a (backed accept); M_BACKED turns exactly those two rows red (0x5a), while sccerr rdawr (cased control) stays green 0x0. So yes: value read-back, one session store-then-load, with the cased-sibling control attributing the delta. That's a channel other than survival. Good \u2014 that's the disposition-pinning claim, and the table supports it: M_BACKED 21/23 with exactly ieicr rdawr + ieerr rdawr red.\n\nNow back to the fatal() issue, because that's potentially the big catch. Let me re-examine.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the fictional fork uses a non-exiting \"fatal\". The phrase \"the house `(once)` shape\" \u2014 and the mutants: M_EXIT \"restores exit(1)\". If fatal() already exited, M_EXIT (restoring exit(1) after the fatal?) would be nearly identical to BASE behavior... no wait, M_EXIT is a MUTANT of the FIXED code: it presumably replaces the fix's behavior with exit(1) \u2014 i.e., reintroduces the kill. If fatal() exited, M_EXIT \u2248 fixed code (both kill), and M_EXIT's 16 red rows would ALSO be red under the fix \u2014 but BASE (the fix) is 23/23 green. So within the fiction, fatal() does not exit; exit(1) does. The fiction is internally consistent on that point ONLY IF fatal() is non-exiting in the fork.\n\nSo the question becomes: is \"fatal() is non-exiting on this fork\" a stated premise? It is NOT stated anywhere in the packet. The packet's premises 1-9 don't mention fatal() semantics. The diff comment says \"the house `(once)` shape\" as if established. The witness post-fix shows survival.\n\nNow, from my knowledge of actual gxemul: fatal() = print + exit(1). If this fork is gxemul-based (it is: src/devices/dev_pcc2.c, DEVICE_ACCESS macro, MEM_WRITE, machine_mvme88k.c, if_ie.c \u2014 these are real gxemul paths), then fatal() exits unless the fork changed it. Changing fatal() to not exit would break... hmm, actually would it? Every existing call site of fatal() in gxemul expects termination. If the fork neutered fatal(), the emulator would limp on after fatal errors \u2014 a massive behavioral change that surely would have been a previous round's issue (there's no mention).\n\nHold on, maybe I should double check my memory of gxemul's fatal(). Actually... let me think about dev_pcc2.c itself. The pre-fix code in the diff: uses `debug()` + `exit(1)`. If fatal() were non-exiting, the natural pre-fix code would use fatal(). The original upstream dev_pcc2.c default arm \u2014 hmm, what does upstream actually have? I recall upstream gxemul dev_pcc2.c:\n\n```c\ndefault:\n    debug(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented %s offset 0x%x\",\n        writeflag == MEM_WRITE? \"write to\" : \"read from\",\n        (int)relative_addr);\n    if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)\n        debug(\": 0x%x\", (int)idata);\n    debug(\" ]\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n```\n\nHmm, that matches the diff's minus lines! Actually yes \u2014 I believe upstream gxemul's dev_pcc2.c really does have `exit(1)` in the default arm. Hmm wait, does it? The diff shows those as the removed lines. If that's upstream verbatim, then upstream dev_pcc2 uses debug+exit \u2014 NOT fatal. Why would upstream use exit(1) directly if fatal() exits? Because they wanted the message under debug (verbose) control, maybe. Or... because fatal() doesn't exit and they wanted termination!\n\nHmm! That's a point IN FAVOR of fatal() being non-exiting... no wait. Let me think. If fatal() exits (my 90% memory), why does upstream dev_pcc2 default arm use debug+exit(1) instead of fatal? Possibly to keep the message verbose-gated. But it's a bit odd.\n\nAlternatively, if fatal() doesn't exit, upstream uses exit(1) for the kill and debug for the message \u2014 consistent.\n\nHmm hmm. Let me try to recall gxemul misc.c definitively. I've definitely read this code at some point. \n\n```c\n/****************************************************************************/\n\n\n/*\n *  fatal():\n *\n *  ...\n */\nvoid fatal(const char *fmt, ...)\n{\n\tva_list ap;\n\n\tva_start(ap, fmt);\n\tvfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n\tva_end(ap);\n\n\texit(1);\n}\n```\n\nI keep reconstructing the same thing with exit(1). Also I recall devices like `dev_bt431.c` or... hmm.\n\nLet me think of another angle: gxemul's `dev_fb.c` (framebuffer) \u2014 on unknown machine it might fatal. `machine.c`: \"fatal: unknown machine 'foo'\"? When you run `gxemul -M unknown`, the output is:\n\n```\nfatal: Unknown machine type 'unknown'\n```\n\nHmm and then it stops. If fatal didn't exit, main() would continue and crash. So fatal exits. Also `emul.c`: `fatal(\"not enough memory\")` etc.\n\nOK here's another very concrete one. In gxemul, when a device access hits something like `dev_ether`: hmm.\n\nI'm going to commit: upstream gxemul `fatal()` prints to stderr and calls exit(1). I'm ~85-90% on this. The docstring I remember: \"This should be used for fatal errors, and the emulator will exit.\" Something like that.\n\nHmm, but hold on, one more consideration. Maybe in this FICTION, the house has already established non-exiting fatal usage. The comment \"the house `(once)` shape\" suggests previous rounds used `fatal(... \"(once) ]\\n\")`. E.g., pcc2widthkills (#119): the width-guard blocks use `warned` bits \u2014 presumably with latched fatal announces. And that passed gates (251 rows green). If those fatal() calls exited the host, the width gate would be all red. So within the fiction, fatal() is established as a non-exiting announce channel \u2014 \"the house shape\".\n\nHmm OK. So now I have a dilemma: as a blind seat, the packet never defines fatal(). Real gxemul's fatal() exits (my memory). The fiction's internal consistency (witness, kill table, gate 14, width kills) requires fatal() to be non-exiting. Two possibilities:\n\n(a) The fork redefined fatal() somewhere (previous round), and the packet just doesn't say so. Then the fix is fine, but there's an UNSTATED PREMISE: \"fatal() does not terminate the host\" \u2014 which is contrary to upstream semantics and must be stated/settled. The premise is checkable in one line (read misc.c / the fork's fatal definition). As a blind seat: name it.\n\n(b) The fork did NOT redefine fatal(), and the whole pass-2 record (witness fixed 13/13, kill table 23/23, gate 14) is internally inconsistent with the diff \u2014 i.e., the record can't be about the diff shown. That's the harshest reading: the diff calls fatal() which exits, so post-fix witness should show host exit1, not survived. The witness inlined says survived. Contradiction between two inlined artifacts.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 hmm, hold on. Let me reconsider (b). If fatal() exits, post-fix witness \"ieicr st.b\" would be host exit1 = FAIL, but the inlined witness says \"survived ... ok\". So the witness and diff can't both be right. Unless the built binary differed from the diff. The kill-table protocol says \"each mutant compile-checked before the table and proven by a diff count\" \u2014 mutants proven, but BASE? BASE 23/23 with fatal() exiting would be... the read rows: \"named rd\" \u2014 a guest read to an uncased offset prints fatal and exits \u2192 row red. So BASE would not be 23/23. Everything in the record screams \"fatal() doesn't exit in the world where these measurements were taken\".\n\nSo the record is self-consistent under \"fatal() doesn't exit\" and wildly inconsistent under \"fatal() exits\". Since the record was produced by seats that CAN read the tree, and none of the nine seats flagged fatal() \u2014 either the fork redefined fatal() (most likely, in a prior round, as \"the house shape\"), or the entire record is confabulated.\n\nHmm wait, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider once more. Actually \u2014 maybe gxemul's fatal() really doesn't exit! Let me think about `main.c` error handling one more... \n\nYou know what, let me look at this from the angle of gxemul's actual dev_pcc2.c, which I might remember. Upstream dev_pcc2.c (Anders Gavare):\n\n```c\n/*\n *  dev_pcc2:\n */\n...\nDEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)\n{\n\t...\n\tswitch (relative_addr & 0x3f) {\n\t...\n\tdefault:\n\t\tdebug(\"[ pcc2: unimplemented %s offset 0x%x\",\n\t\t    writeflag == MEM_WRITE? \"write to\" : \"read from\",\n\t\t    (int) relative_addr);\n\t\tif (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)\n\t\t\tdebug(\": 0x%x\", (int)idata);\n\t\tdebug(\" ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);\n\t}\n\treturn 1;\n}\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 actually, I genuinely recall many gxemul devices ending with `exit(1)` or `fatal(...)` in default arms. E.g., dev_8253? dev_mc146818? I recall `fatal(\"dev_mc146818: unimplemented ...\")` hmm.\n\nAnd what about the specific semantics \u2014 let me recall `src/misc/misc.c` one more time. There are functions: `debug`, `fatal`, `my_execfile`? no... `gettimeofday`... `xlate`? Hmm.\n\nI recall this specific comment block in misc.c:\n\n```c\n/*\n *  fatal():\n *\n *  A fatal error. The message is printed to stderr, and gxemul exits.\n */\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident about exit. Let me also sanity check with a usage that would be weird otherwise: `cpu.c`: `fatal(\"nregs reset with non-zero ...\\n\")` hmm.\n\n`emul.c`:\n```c\nif (... == NULL)\n    fatal(\"out of memory\\n\");\n```\nIf fatal didn't exit, this would null-deref right after. gxemul code is full of such patterns: `X = fatal-check; if (!X) fatal(\"oom\");` and then proceeds to deref X. So fatal must exit. Yes. I'm now ~95%: gxemul's fatal() = print stderr + exit(1).\n\nOK so here's my analysis: The single most important premise in this whole pass \u2014 so basic nobody thought to state it \u2014 is that `fatal()` in THIS FORK prints without exiting. Upstream it exits. The diff's entire claim (\"declines writes\", \"keeps served reads\", \"survived\") hinges on it. The packet never states it; the \"house `(once)` shape\" phrase implies prior rounds established it, and the measurements (witness 13/13, kill table BASE 23/23, gate 14 green) corroborate that in the measured world fatal() behaves non-exiting. So the premise is probably TRUE on the fork (else every measurement would be red), but it is NOT STATED as a premise, and if it were false the fix would be a no-op swap of exit(1)\u2192fatal()\u2192exit(1).\n\nHmm wait, but hold on \u2014 actually, is that right that the measurements corroborate? The measurements were run by the panel seats on the actual built binary. If fatal() exited, BASE would show host exits on default-arm accesses. The witness post-fix shows survival. The witness was run on \"label=fixed-501\" binary. So the binary's fatal() didn't exit when reaching that arm. Therefore the fork's fatal() is non-exiting (or the binary differs from the diff \u2014 a worse hypothesis). So from the packet alone, the premise \"fatal() does not exit on this fork\" is CORROBORATED by the inlined witness \u2014 I can treat it as READ (from the packet) in a weak sense: the witness outcome implies it.\n\nBut \u2014 CRITICAL \u2014 if fatal() was redefined to not exit, that's a change to a function used at every fatal error site in the emulator. gxemul calls fatal() for OOM, unknown machine, bad config, etc. If the fork neutered it, those error paths no longer terminate \u2014 a fork-wide hazard. Hmm, but wait: would the fork do that? That seems like a terrible idea. Alternatively the fork introduced a different function... but the diff literally says `fatal(`. Hmm.\n\nOR: maybe the house redefined fatal() ONLY as a macro in some devices... no.\n\nOK, alternative resolution: maybe the house convention is that `fatal()` exits, and the FIX intends for the latched fatal() to... kill?? Re-read the design premise 6: \"WRITE side member 3 -- DECLINE + latched `fatal()` `(once)`\". And the witness post-fix: \"ieicr st.b survived\". If fatal exits, st.b wouldn't survive. So no.\n\nSo the packet forces: fatal() = non-exiting on this fork. Fine. As a blind auditor, my job: NAME the premise, note that it's contrary to upstream gxemul (where fatal() prints to stderr and exits), note that the packet corroborates it via the witness only if the witness is trusted, and that the witness is the same artifact whose pre-fix version the pass-1 panel already caught problems with... hmm, the witness was disposition-blind but not accused of fabrication.\n\nHmm, hmm, wait. Actually now I want to reconsider the direction of this audit. The three questions:\n\n**Q1. Is the fix right... and which accepted disposition is wrong, by number?**\n\nThe prompt hints: \"be harder on the accepted dispositions than the rejected ones; a seat whose position was adopted (Opus's fix A) scrutinises it MORE.\" So they expect me to find something wrong among dispositions 1-12. Let me audit each:\n\n1. De-fatalise the arm; keep served reads. \u2014 Directionally right (the exit(1) on any uncased access, reads included, is a host-kill DoS from guest). But the implementation uses fatal()... if fatal() exits, this disposition is NOT actually implemented. Also: is \"keep served reads\" right? The memcpy above serves reads for... hmm, wait. Premise 2: \"Reads are served BEFORE the switch by the memcpy (with the #119 end-span guard: a read crossing 0x40 is zero-filled, not served)\". So a guest read to offset 0x2a gets served pcctwo_reg[0x2a] (0x0 default) then falls into default: which previously exit(1)'d. Post-fix: prints \"served 0x%02x\" and breaks. OK.\n\n2. Member 3 for writes, not member 6. \u2014 Reasonable per the warrants. But note: \"DECLINE\" here means... the write is dropped? The diff: on MEM_WRITE, print fatal \"declined\", break, return 1. Hmm \u2014 return 1 means SUCCESSFUL transaction (premise 8: return 0 = failed transaction). So a declined write returns 1 = \"transaction succeeded\" while the write was silently dropped. Is that \"decline\"? The announcement says declined but the return code says success. Hmm. For MEM_WRITE the memcpy already... wait, does the memcpy apply to writes too? In gxemul device access pattern, typically:\n\n```c\nif (writeflag == MEM_READ)\n    memcpy(idata, d->pcctwo_reg + relative_addr, len);\n```\n\nor maybe the memcpy serves both directions into a shadow? Premise 2 says \"Reads are served BEFORE the switch by the memcpy\" \u2014 reads only. So a write to offset 0x2a is dropped (never stored), announced \"declined\", and returns 1. Returning 1 for a dropped write: the bus cycle completes without error. Is that right? On real hardware, these registers exist; a write would be accepted. The model declines (doesn't store) but reports success. That's a design choice \u2014 member 3 \"DECLINE\". Fine. But is \"declined + return 1\" coherent? The guest can't tell. It's \"ignore the write, announce once\". OK, defensible, disclosed.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 actually now think about the READ side and return 1: read is served from the array. Fine.\n\n3. Detector rdawr rows pin the disposition \u2014 supported by M_BACKED result (exactly those two rows red under backed accept, sccerr control green). OK.\n\n4. if_ie never attaches; re-based ranking. \u2014 Can't check from packet (needs tree); but the packet gives the chain: machine_mvme88k.c:106 unreadable at 0xfff46000, if_ie.c:407-409 badpaddr() match failure, ie_setupram early return :561-564 before the PCC2 store :609. Internal question: if if_ie never attaches, then no guest driver writes IEICR \u2014 so what GUEST actually triggers the default arm on this model? The severity ranking \"HIGH rests on the witness and severity\" \u2014 the witness is a synthetic probe, not an attached driver. If no real guest reaches the offsets, is the bug rung-3 (host kill from any guest byte access) still HIGH? The witness demonstrates host-kill via guest code \u2014 but the packet says no driver in the shipped configs reaches these offsets (OpenBSD 2.2's if_ie doesn't attach). Hmm, but \"the guest source is _guest-src-openbsd22/\" \u2014 OpenBSD 2.2 on MVME187 with... other drivers might touch PCC2 offsets. E.g., the MVME187 kernel's clock driver (mc146818 via PCC2?), zsc? The pcc2 has lots of functions. An OpenBSD kernel probing devices might touch IEICR even without if_ie attaching? E.g., an intree probe loop over register offsets? Can't settle from packet. The re-based ranking: HIGH on witness+severity \u2014 \"any guest byte access kills the host\" is a guest-triggerable host kill; in emulator-security terms that's typically HIGH regardless of whether a known driver hits it, since guest code is untrusted. Defensible. But note the tension: they say \"no guest reaches these offsets on this model\" in the diff comment (as a warrant against member 6), AND rank it HIGH. If no guest reaches, the practical severity is lower; the witness makes it reachable by ANY guest code (trivial). So HIGH stands on the witness. OK.\n\n5. Alias documented (Table 6-1) \u2014 fine, premise upgraded with the manual line quoted (\"$FFF42040-$FFF42FFF PCCchip2 Registers Repeated\"). Reasonable.\n\n6. \"cheapest witness: put b\" measured FALSE \u2014 fine, control confirmed by put b rows (msg 0 both pre and post).\n\n7. IEBERR/IEERR row measures IEBERR only; detector has one session per direction across all 18 named offsets \u2014 hmm wait: \"the detector has one session per direction across all 18 named offsets, valid because the fix has no per-offset branch (disclosed)\". Hmm, but the fix DOES have per-offset state: warned_unimpl latch per offset. The announce COUNT could differ per offset... no wait, the latch is per offset, so a session that walks all 18 named offsets gets 18 announces (one per offset) \u2014 one session per DIRECTION means: a write session touching all 18 named offsets (18 announces) and a read session touching all 18 (18 announces). Valid because the fix treats all uncased offsets identically (no per-offset branch in behavior \u2014 the only per-offset state is the latch bit, which is exactly what the latch rows test). OK, disclosed. Fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me reconsider: \"one session per direction across all 18 named offsets\" \u2014 in ONE session, writes to 18 named offsets: each gets its own latch bit, 18 announces. Under M_ONELATCH (one latch for the whole site), only the FIRST offset announces \u2192 \"named wr msg 1\" red. Yes, matches M_ONELATCH red rows: named wr msg 1, named rd msg 1... wait, predicted \"named wr msg 1, named rd msg 1, named rd srvd 1, latch twooff 1\". Hmm, \"named rd srvd 1\"? Under M_ONELATCH, why would named rd srvd go red? M_ONELATCH = one latch for the whole site, \"writes only, reads keep the old debug()\" \u2014 that's the codex alternative! \"The codex seat's alternative -- ONE latch for the whole site, writes only, reads keep the old `debug()`\". So M_ONELATCH models the REJECTED alternative: reads use debug() (verbose-gated, maybe not captured?) \u2192 \"named rd srvd 1\" hmm. The prediction says M_ONELATCH red: named wr msg 1 (only one announce instead of 18), named rd msg 1 (read announces via debug not fatal? if the harness captures stderr only... hmm, \"msg\" count presumably counts the fatal lines; debug lines maybe not captured \u2192 1 vs expected 18? or 0?), named rd srvd 1 (reads keep old debug \u2192 the served-value announce text differs \u2192 \"srvd 1\"?? Hmm, \"named rd srvd\" is the served-VALUE channel \u2014 expected 18 rows with served 0x00? Under M_ONELATCH reads keep old debug, so the \"served 0x%02x\" announce is absent \u2192 srvd count 1? I can't fully reconstruct the instrument, fine.) and \"latch twooff 1\" (one latch for whole site \u2192 two different offsets announce only once \u2192 count 1 instead of 2). Measured M_ONELATCH: 19/23 with exactly those four red. Consistent with modeling codex's alternative. Good \u2014 the detector pins the per-offset choice against the recorded alternative. \n\n8. IACKs declined; c040kill first. Cross-round obligation. OK.\n\n9. Premise 2 wording L4s. OK.\n\n10. return 0 = failed transaction. OK.\n\n11. Per-offset announce adopted; codex per-site recorded. OK (bounded, 64 bits covers 0..0x3f \u2014 fine since relative_addr folded to 0..0x3f... wait, is it? Premise 6: \"`relative_addr` is folded to 0..0x3f above the switch so the shift is defined\"). Hmm \u2014 \"the shift is defined\" \u2014 with uint64_t and relative_addr in 0..0x3f, 1ULL << 0..63 is defined. OK. But WAIT: is relative_addr really folded to 0..0x3f ABOVE the switch, or is the fold part of the switch expression? The premise says folded above the switch. If instead the switch were on `relative_addr & 0x3f` while relative_addr itself were larger (e.g., 0x6a from an access at 0xfff4206a), then `1ULL << relative_addr` with relative_addr=0x6a would be UB (shift \u2265 64) \u2014 the comment in the struct says \"relative_addr is folded to PCC2_SIZE above the switch, so the shift is always defined\", asserting the fold. Also `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` as the array index in the read announce \u2014 if relative_addr weren't folded, 0x6a would read OUT OF BOUNDS of pcctwo_reg[0x40]! And the alias row \"alias 0x6a\" tests exactly this: \"the %= PCC2_SIZE fold: 0x6a aliases IEICR\". Post-fix witness: \"alias 0x6a survived\" and kill table \"alias 0x6a\" red under M_EXIT, green under BASE. And \"alias msg\" row. So the detector covers it. But the premise \"the fold is above the switch\" is a SOURCE fact I cannot check from the packet \u2014 the diff doesn't show the fold! The diff only shows the default arm. The fold (relative_addr %= PCC2_SIZE) is asserted in premises 1/6 and the struct comment, and corroborated by... the witness alias row (0x6a aliases IEICR \u2014 pre-fix host exit1 on 0x6a shows 0x6a reached the default arm; post-fix survived). Hmm, but wait: pre-fix \"alias 0x6a host exit1\" \u2014 that shows 0x6a reached default:. If the fold is above the switch, then the announce for 0x6a says \"offset 0x2a\" (folded) \u2014 and pcctwo_reg[0x2a] \u2014 in bounds. If there were NO fold above the switch (fold only inside the switch expression), then post-fix the read announce would do pcctwo_reg[0x6a] \u2014 OOB read of the struct \u2014 which might not crash (UB, but likely reads adjacent bytes) and the latch 1ULL << 0x6a \u2014 UB shift, typically masked by x86 to 0x6a&0x3f=0x2a... on x86 the shift is mod 64, so 1ULL<<0x6a == 1ULL<<0x2a \u2014 silently \"works\". The detector's \"alias msg\" row checks the announce TEXT \u2014 expected \"offset 0x2a\" if folded. If the fold were only in the switch, msg would say 0x6a \u2192 red. Under BASE it's green, so the fold is real per the instrument. OK \u2014 so the packet corroborates the fold via the alias rows. Good. But strictly: the fold location (\"above the switch\") is not in the diff; it's asserted and corroborated. I'll note it as a premise corroborated by the alias rows (READ: the rows exist; the source fact itself not inlined \u2014 but the diff context line \"@@ -658,13 +662,40 @@ DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)\" shows only the default arm).\n\n12. Two fresh sessions can't establish per-instance latch scope; debt filed. OK.\n\n13-15 rejections: member 6 rejected \u2014 good; member 5 rejected \u2014 \"these are documented, present registers\" \u2014 faulting every access would break guests that legitimately read unimplemented-modeled registers; rejection reasonable. 15: no offset-range predicate \u2014 reasonable.\n\nSo which accepted disposition is WRONG? The prompt for Q1 asks \"which accepted disposition is wrong, by number?\" \u2014 implying at least one is. Candidates:\n\n(a) None of the 12 dispositions is substantively wrong, but the IMPLEMENTATION has the fatal() problem. Hmm, but the question insists \"which accepted disposition is wrong, by number?\" Maybe the answer: disposition 1 and 2 are wrong AS IMPLEMENTED because the implementation uses fatal() which (upstream) exits \u2014 i.e., the adopted design \"DECLINE + latched fatal()\" is self-contradictory if fatal() exits. \"DECLINE + fatal()\" is a contradiction in terms in upstream gxemul: you cannot decline and fatal. If the fork redefined fatal, the disposition rests on an unstated premise.\n\n(b) Or maybe there's another flaw I should find. Let me re-scan.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the READ announce: `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]` printed as \"served 0x%02x\". Premise 6 says \"it is the array's default, NOT a computed value: not member 4 of the option set\". The memcpy above serves the read into idata from the same array \u2014 so the announce prints the same byte the guest got. Consistent. But WAIT: the memcpy serves reads BEFORE the switch \u2014 for BYTE reads at uncased offsets AND for WIDE reads that SPAN into uncased territory? E.g., a 4-byte read at offset 0x28 (IEERR): IEERR uncased \u2192 served 4 bytes of array defaults. Fine. What about a wide read that straddles a CASED register and uncased bytes \u2014 e.g., 4-byte read at 0x26 covering 0x26,0x27 (cased?) and 0x28 (IEERR, uncased)? The memcpy serves ALL 4 bytes; then the switch dispatches on relative_addr 0x26 \u2192 the cased arm for 0x26 handles it; the uncased bytes 0x28 within the span get served silently WITHOUT the announce (default: never reached). Hmm \u2014 that's an asymmetry: the announce is per-access-entry (switch dispatch offset), not per-byte-served. A wide read at 0x26 gets uncased bytes served with NO announce and no latch. Is that a defect? It's consistent with \"the memcpy serves reads before the switch\" (premise 2) \u2014 the arm only sees the dispatch offset. The detector: \"pcc2 width rows emitted 251, green 251\" \u2014 the width chain (#119) covers spans. The kill table has \"t1cmp word rt\" and \"inword wr\". Hmm \u2014 \"inword wr\" is a write INSIDE a counter word (0x05) \u2192 uncased \u2192 declined+announce. What about a WIDE write at 0x04 covering the whole T1CMP word? If T1CMP (0x04..0x07) has a case at 0x04 (the word base), the wide write dispatches to the T1CMP case \u2192 handled as implemented. If the wide write is at 0x05 (offset alignment...) \u2014 relative_addr 0x05 \u2192 default \u2192 declined. OK.\n\nSo straddling reads: served bytes from uncased offsets without announce \u2014 disclosed? The design says \"one latched announce per offset per instance\" \u2014 but a straddling read serves uncased bytes WITHOUT the latch being set or the announce printed. So \"per offset per instance\" is not exactly true for bytes served via spans whose dispatch offset is cased. Is that worth flagging? It's a READ though \u2014 reads are served and announced as \"served\" \u2014 the guest GETS the bytes either way; the announce is informational. The latch would matter only for announce spam. Minor.\n\nHmm, but here's a subtler one: the WRITE side. A WIDE write (e.g., 4-byte st) at an uncased offset: the memcpy \u2014 does it apply to writes? Premise 2 says reads are served by the memcpy. For writes, gxemul's typical pattern is that the switch arm copies idata into the register array for the cased arms. The default arm (old) didn't copy \u2192 write dropped. New arm: still dropped, announced. So a wide write spanning from an uncased offset (e.g., st at 0x28, 4 bytes covering 0x28-0x2b including IEBERR at 0x2b) \u2014 dispatch at 0x28 \u2192 default \u2192 the whole span declined, including the IEBERR byte. Announce says \"write to offset 0x28\" only. Fine \u2014 per-offset announce means per-dispatch-offset. Disclosed as \"in-word bytes are the interior of IMPLEMENTED counters \u2014 declined here as a width matter\". OK.\n\nNow \u2014 bigger question \u2014 the WRITE side and the memcpy: premise 2 says the memcpy serves READS. What about writes to cased registers \u2014 handled in arms. OK.\n\n(c) Let me now scrutinize the latch guard vs body: \"GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD \u2014 the guard reads warned_unimpl (bit relative_addr); the body reads writeflag, idata, pcctwo_reg[relative_addr]. They disagree only in the latch's own state\". The guard: `!(d->warned_unimpl & (1ULL << relative_addr))`. Body: writes vs read; write uses idata; read uses pcctwo_reg. Latch mutants: M_UNLATCH removes the latch (announce every access): red latch sameoff (2 announces instead of 1? \"latch sameoff 2\" predicted \u2014 same offset twice \u2192 2 announces expected 1), latch rw one (read then write same offset \u2192 2 announces expected 1). Measured M_UNLATCH 21/23: exactly latch sameoff, latch rw one red. Consistent.\n\nM_ONELATCH: one latch for whole site, writes only, reads old debug: red named wr msg 1, named rd msg 1, named rd srvd 1, latch twooff 1. Measured 19/23 exactly those four. Consistent.\n\n(d) Now the RDKILL mutant: predicted 18/23, measured 16/23 with two extra reds (ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr). Safe miss, recorded. Fine.\n\n(e) M_DIRSWAP: dir wr text 0, dir rd text 0, named rd srvd 0 \u2014 direction swapped (writeflag test inverted): write announce prints \"read from ... served 0x%02x\" reading pcctwo_reg on a WRITE (and declines on READS?). Red: dir wr text, dir rd text, named rd srvd. Measured 20/23: named rd srvd, dir wr text, dir rd text. Consistent.\n\nSo the detector table is internally consistent with predictions modulo two recorded safe misses (M_EXIT arithmetic slip, M_RDKILL extras). \n\nNow Q2 answer: no UNSAFE contradiction; two safe-direction deviations recorded (M_RDKILL +2 red: ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr; M_EXIT's count said 8/23 but its own enumeration matches the measured 16 red \u2014 arithmetic slip, not a channel miss). And yes, DECLINE vs BACKED is pinned by the VALUE channel (read-back 0x0 vs 0x5a in one session) with sccerr rdawr as the cased control \u2014 M_BACKED's red set is exactly the two rdawr rows.\n\nWait, actually let me double-check M_BACKED: measured 21/23, red: ieicr rdawr, ieerr rdawr. Predicted same. And sccerr rdawr stays green (0x0) under M_BACKED \u2014 because SCCERR is cased, write-to-clear, unaffected by the default-arm mutant. Good \u2014 control holds.\n\nHmm, and under M_BACKED, what about \"named rd srvd\"? Backed accept stores the written byte into the array; a subsequent READ of the same offset would serve the STORED byte. The rdawr rows capture that (store 0x5a then load in ONE session, want 0x0 \u2014 backed gives 0x5a \u2192 red). The \"named rd srvd\" row is presumably a fresh-session read without prior write \u2192 array default 0x0 \u2192 green under M_BACKED. Right, that's why the ONE-session rdawr rows were needed (disposition 3) \u2014 fresh sessions can't distinguish (array reset per instance). Consistent with disposition 12 (fresh sessions = new process... wait, \"each is a new process\" \u2014 for the LATCH scope; but for backed-accept the array is per-INSTANCE \u2014 within one emulator run, one instance. Two sessions = two processes = two fresh instances \u2192 the stored byte wouldn't