31742:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while checking [[alphapfnoffset]]'s reachability rather than by any seat.\n\n    if (vaddr == 0xfffffffd80000000ULL) {\n            fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**A guest-chosen virtual address ends the emulator process.**  It sits inside the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap arm,\nso it is reached BEFORE virtual memory is configured -- i.e. early in a boot, on an address the kernel\nchooses, not one a user program supplies.\n\nIt is the same de-fatalisation class this project has worked through repeatedly in devices, arriving in a CPU\nmemory path where nobody has been looking.  **The disposition is NOT obvious, which is why this is filed\nrather than fixed:** declining leaves the guest with a wrong translation and it will fault or hang somewhere\nless legible, so this may be one of the rare sites where a loud stop is genuinely better than continuing --\nand if so the round's product is a RECORD saying so, plus a latch, not a removal.\n\nMED: reachable by construction, but only on a machine whose Alpha VM support is already declared incomplete\nseven TODOs over.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : replace the terminal path with the architecturally appropriate outcome\n    one oracle     : that outcome is observed for the affected address class\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** grok ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- the sixth and last Alpha row.  THE CLUSTER IS NOW FULLY ASSESSED. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE BUT NARROWER THAN FILED: it holds for kernel mode with PTBR == 0 on that exact\nVA.**  There are TWO hits on `0xfffffffd80000000` in `memory_alpha.c` and **only one terminates**;\nthe other is a bare `fatal(\"AYONA3\n\")` -- a debug string left in the tree.  `fatal()` only prints;\nthe termination is the `exit(1)` immediately after, with nothing recovering in between.\n\n## THE CENSUS I ASKED FOR, and it changes the framing\n\n| walker | unmodelled / stub region | ordinary translation miss |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alpha | this one VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)`; other PTBR==0 VAs **ACCEPT** identity | `fatal` + **`return 0`**, no guest exception (`#194` removed the abort here) |\n| MIPS | -- | `mips_cpu_exception`, `return 0` |\n| ARM | unimplemented descriptor type: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `arm_exception`, `return 0` |\n| PPC | 64-bit translation: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `ppc_exception`, `return 0` |\n| M88K | internal inconsistency: `exit(1)` | `m88k_exception`, `return 0` |\n| SH | unimplemented kernel VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)` (**honours FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS**) | `sh_exception`, `return 0` |\n\n**The kill is an outlier in BOTH in-file directions** -- it is neither the PTBR==0 siblings' ACCEPT\nnor the walk's own `return 0`, which this project already chose over host death in `#194`.\n\n## *** ACCEPT IS REFUTED ON A PREMISE, AND THIS IS THE SHARPEST PART OF THE ANSWER ***\n\nDeleting the `exit(1)` looks like member 4, because `*return_paddr` is already computed above it.\n**But that value is the comment's own \"UGLY hack\": a K0SEG mask applied to a K1SEG address**, giving\na physical address near 4 TB against a default 128 MB of RAM.  Data then takes the unmapped-physical\npath -- **zeros returned, writes dropped, and the access reported SUCCESSFUL.**\n\n> *\"That is the silent bogus map the landmine exists to refuse.\"*\n\n**So a bare de-fatalisation here would be WORSE than the kill it removes**, which is the opposite of\nthis queue's usual shape and is exactly the kind of thing the option-set rule exists to surface.\n\n## DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, `return 0`\n\n* **5 FAULT is architecturally right and NOT AVAILABLE.**  Choosing it obliges building delivery\n  first: a PAL frame, `kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a\n  redirected PC.  **That is a new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.**  *My brief's caution\n  that FAULT may be unavailable on Alpha is confirmed, with the bill itemised.*\n* **7 IMPLEMENT does not apply** -- its tell is \"no predicate, whole path stubbed\"; this is a\n  one-address predicate, and K1SEG translation IS the walk once PTBR is set.\n* **2 silent DECLINE drops the only signal the author planted.**\n\n## SCOPE: STILL SIX SEPARATE ROUNDS\n\nI asked whether the shared missing MM-dispatch now meets codex's bar for merging this with\n`alphafaultclaim`.  **It does not:** *\"missing MM-dispatch is a CARRIER, not a shared repair.\"*  A\ncommon blocker is not one fix design and one oracle.\n\n## SEVERITY: **med**, explicitly BELOW `alphatbinegsel`\n\nRaised from unset to `med` on grok's argument: this needs a guest already executing privileged code\nwith PTBR == 0 at one specific address, whereas `alphatbinegsel` is a documented primitive silently\nunder-flushing during ordinary operation.  *The ranking is the seat's and I am adopting it rather\nthan substituting my own.*",
31764:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the codex reachability pass (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt).\n\ncodex: *\"The fork's claim that a failed Alpha walk causes the caller to raise a fault is unsupported by this\npath.  The translator returns zero, the memory worker returns failure, and the Alpha data instruction\nabandons the access without delivering an Alpha exception.\"*\n\n*** THIS IS A WRONG-RECORD (L4) CANDIDATE IN A COMMENT THE FORK ADDED ITSELF. ***  `memory_alpha.c` carries\na `#194: (Codex/Fable) a failed guest page-table walk must not ...' note at its `not_found' label -- so a\nprevious round reasoned about this path and recorded a conclusion about fault delivery.  If the delivery\ndoes not happen, that record is false and a future round will transfer it.\n\n**AND IT CORROBORATES A STANDING CARRIER CLAIM FROM A NEW DIRECTION.**  The option-set rule already records\nthat member 5 (FAULT) *\"is NOT universally available -- there is no Alpha equivalent\"* of the MIPS/M88K\nexception dispatch.  That was derived from `memory_rw.c`; this is the same conclusion reached from the\nAlpha instruction side.  **Any round reaching for FAULT on an Alpha device now has two independent reasons\nnot to.**\n\nNOT YET VERIFIED BY ME -- recorded as the seat stated it, with the two citations it gave, because the\nverification is a reading of `cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c`'s failure path and belongs to the round that\ntakes it.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : if it is only a false comment, document actual fault ownership\n    one oracle     : the documented claim is supported by the return/caller control flow\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED 2026-09-08 BY A SEAT THAT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT THIS ROW. ***\n\ncodex, answering the `alphapteprot` brief (`regress/artifacts/alphapteprot/codex_assess_20260908.txt`):\n\n> *\"There is no implemented Alpha MM-fault dispatch here.  `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` and `ALPHA_MMCSR_*`\n> have definitions but no consumers; `kentry` entries are initialized and assigned, never used to\n> dispatch.  The comments claiming the translator or caller 'already signalled' a fault are\n> unsupported by the implementation.\"*\n\n**That is this row's claim, reached from the other direction** -- it was tracing whether a\nwrite-protected page could fault, and found the fault machinery is definitions only.  *Raised under\nthe found-something-else licence, on a brief about the page walk's return value.*\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR SCOPE:** this row and `alphapteprot` are now coupled by a shared prerequisite --\n**neither can deliver an architectural fault, because nothing dispatches one.**  codex's earlier\ncluster ruling said they may be grouped *\"only after proving they share a file, one fault-handling\nrepair, and the same fault-delivery oracle.\"*  **The shared missing machinery is evidence toward\nexactly that**, and it is the question to put to the adjudication rather than to settle here.\n\n*** A THIRD INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION, 2026-09-08, again from a seat not asked about this row. ***\ncodex, on the `alphactxfail` brief: *\"The comment saying the translator already signalled a fault is\nalso unsupported by the inspected Alpha failure path.\"*  **Three briefs, three directions, one\nconclusion** -- the tbi callback trace, the page-walk protection review, and now the context-switch\nerror handling.  *None of the three was asked about this row; all three arrived at it while tracing\nwhether something could fault.*",
31768:   "id": "alphaswpctxreg",
31774:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the codex reachability pass (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt), raised under the\nfound-something-else licence on a brief about something unrelated.\n\ncodex: *\"`swpctx` writes the old context into **a0**, although the supplied ABI wrapper returns **v0**.\nThis independently contradicts the handler's contract.\"*\n\nIf correct, a guest calling the PALcode `swpctx` gets its previous context pointer in the wrong register --\nthe caller reads `v0` and finds whatever was there.  That is a silent wrong value rather than a crash, which\nis the harder kind to notice.\n\n*(SUPERSEDED WITHIN THE HOUR, kept because the record should show the order.  This paragraph read:\n**\"NOT VERIFIED BY ME.  Both citations are given and the check is two file reads; it is filed rather\nthan confirmed because the Alpha queue is already three rows deep tonight and none of them has a\nwitness.  A round taking any Alpha item should settle this one first -- it is the cheapest of the four\nto check.\"**  It WAS the cheapest, it WAS two file reads, and both were done immediately below.)*\n\n*** VERIFIED BY ME 2026-09-07, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN THE SEAT STATED: THE CODE CONTRADICTS THE COMMENT\nDIRECTLY ABOVE IT. ***\n\n    /*  Return old context in v0.  */\n    cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n\nThe comment says v0; the store is to ALPHA_A0.  **ALPHA_V0 exists and is used correctly elsewhere in the same file** -- the PAL dispatch a hundred\nlines up does `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = a3;' -- so this is not a missing symbol or a naming\nconvention; it is one wrong identifier.  And the ABI wrapper confirms the intent independently:\n`alpha_pal_swpctx' in `src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h' declares its return register as\n`__asm(\"$0\")' (v0), takes the new context in `__asm(\"$16\")' (a0), and ends `return (v0);'.\n\n**RAISED LOW -> MED and this is now the CHEAPEST CERTAIN FIX ON THE BOARD** -- cheaper than\n[[sgirergbroundtrip]], because the intent is stated on the line above the defect rather than inferred from a\nconsumer.  One token, ALPHA_V0'.\n\n**Its REACHABILITY is still bounded by the same open question as the other Alpha rows** ([[alphapfnoffset]]'s\nboot question) -- a guest must reach a context switch.  But unlike those, the DEFECT is certain without any\nwitness: a comment and its code cannot both be right.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : return the old context through the ABI-defined result register\n    one oracle     : returned v0 equals the outgoing context address\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** FIX DESIGN COMPLETE, codex 2026-09-08 -- AND IT REFUTED THE PREMISE I PUT IN THE BRIEF. ***\n\n**MY Q2 PREMISE WAS WRONG.**  I asked whether this is ONE defect or TWO, suggesting the store to\n`r[ALPHA_A0]` does two wrong things: fails to place the result where the caller reads it, AND\nclobbers the caller's `a0`.  **The clobber is not a defect.**  The wrapper is:\n\n    static __inline unsigned long\n    alpha_pal_swpctx(unsigned long ctx)\n    {\n            register unsigned long a0 __asm(\"$16\") = ctx;\n            register unsigned long v0 __asm(\"$0\");\n            __asm volatile(\"call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx\"\n                    : \"=r\" (a0), \"=r\" (v0)\n                    : \"i\" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), \"0\" (a0)\n                    : \"$1\", \"$22\", \"$23\", \"$24\", \"$25\", \"memory\");\n            return (v0);\n    }\n\n**`a0` is an OUTPUT tied to an input, so its incoming value need not survive** -- the wrapper\ndiscards that output and returns `v0`.  So it is ONE defect with two register-state effects, and the\none-character edit repairs it without any separate preservation handling.\n\n*And my reading of `\"0\"` was wrong in the other direction too: it refers to output OPERAND ZERO,\nwhich here occupies `$16` (`a0`) -- it does NOT select physical `$0`.  `ALPHA_V0 = 0` and\n`ALPHA_A0 = 16` in `cpu_alpha.h`, so the two happen to look alike in a way that invites exactly this\nconfusion.*\n\n## THE FIX -- one character, and it matches FOUR in-tree siblings\n\n    -\t\tcpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n    +\t\tcpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n\n**The house idiom was found by grepping the fork rather than by reasoning**, as the option-set rule\nrequires.  The closest sibling is `PAL_OSF1_swpipl` (case 0x35) -- *\"a0 = new ipl, v0 = return old\nipl\"* -- which writes the old state into `ALPHA_V0` and then installs the new state from the copied\nlocal `a0`, the identical shape.  `rdmces`, `rdps` and `whami` all return through `ALPHA_V0` as\nwell, as does `PROM_R_PUTS` in `alpha_prom_call()`.\n\n**Q4 -- no in-tree dependency.**  The local `a0` stays live through `cpu->cd.alpha.ctx = a0`, so the\nload-new-context step is unaffected; the emulator caller checks status and PC and never reads\n`r[ALPHA_A0]`; and `src/` contains no calls to the wrapper at all.  *An external guest relying on\nthe accidental old-context value in `a0` would observe a change -- but the wrapper provides no such\nguarantee, so that is not a contract this round breaks.*\n\n## *** THE ORACLE IS DEBUGGER-VISIBLE, WHICH MAKES THE WITNESS CHEAP ***\n\n    observable   existing code   fixed code\n    v0           P (stale)       C  (the outgoing context)\n    a0           C               N  (the incoming argument)\n\n**The oracle is `v0_after == ctx_before`**, and the debugger's `reg` command reaches the Alpha\nregister dump, which prints both registers by name -- **so guest code need not consume the return\nvalue for the two builds to be distinguishable.**  That is a materially easier witness than\n[[alphatbinegsel]]'s, which needs a cached mapping, a PTE remap and everything inside one `step N`.\n\n*** THE VACUITY CONDITION, and it is the same shape as the page-0 trap on the sibling row: the\nobservation requires `P != C` -- the pre-call `v0` must differ from the outgoing context, or a stale\n`v0` happens to look correct. ***  The probe must set `v0` to a distinctive value before the call.\n\n**Q6 -- KEEP the comment, and it owes a row.**  *\"Return old context in v0\"* asserts an observable\npostcondition (the value AND its register), so under this project's rule it is a claim that must be\npinned, not a description.  It makes no claim about preserving `a0`, which is consistent with the\nfinding above.\n\n**STATUS: ready to implement.**  One-character product change, four sibling precedents, a named\noracle with its vacuity condition, and no in-tree dependency.  Nothing has been built or run --\nthis was a source review and the seat said so.\n\n*** THE WARRANT IS STRONGER THAN THE SEAT STATED, AND I RE-DERIVED IT RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBING. ***\n\ncodex named four sibling precedents.  **THE DERIVER, so the next reader re-runs it instead of\ntrusting a literal:**\n\n    grep -n 'ALPHA_V0\\] =' src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n\nOn 2026-09-08 that printed EIGHT return-value sites in this one file -- :118 (PROM_R_PUTS),\n:204 (rdmces), :306 (sysvalue), :339 and :344 (swpipl, rdps), :356 (usp), :361 (whami), :383 (a\nsuccess code).  The same grep for ALPHA_A0 finds the swpctx line under review.\n\n> **SO swpctx IS THE LONE OUTLIER AMONG NINE RETURN-VALUE SITES IN ITS OWN FILE.**  That is a far\n> stronger warrant than \"four siblings agree\", and it is the shape the option-set rule already\n> records from sgireunmapped: *the arm under review was the file's own outlier, and one grep showed\n> it.*\n\nALPHA_V0 is 0 and ALPHA_A0 is 16 (src/include/cpu_alpha.h), so the two constants look confusable in\na way that plausibly explains the original slip -- and explains my own misreading of the \"0\" asm\nconstraint as selecting physical $0.\n\n*The count is written with its deriver rather than as a bare literal, per the counts rule: the\nrounds that consult a number here are the rounds that change it.*\n\n*** A TOOLING NOTE FROM WRITING THIS ENTRY, because it is the fifth instance in one session:\nBACKTICKS IN RECORD PROSE MUST NOT GO THROUGH `python -c`. ***  Bash performs command substitution\non them before python ever sees the string -- this attempt emitted a screenful of\n`:118: command not found` and then a SyntaxError, having also executed lines out of two unrelated\nfiles it happened to glob.  **No write occurred, because python died before reaching it.**  The\nremedy is recorded and I keep not applying it: *record prose goes in a script FILE, written with the\nWrite tool, never inlined into a shell command.*\n\n*** RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-08 WITH A CENSUS, AND THE CENSUS IS WHAT MAKES THIS UNARGUABLE: `:251` IS A\nPOPULATION OF ONE AGAINST A POPULATION OF EIGHT. ***\n\n    grep -n \"ALPHA_V0\\]\" src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n        118  204  306  339  344  356  361  383        <- EIGHT handlers return via V0\n\n    grep -n \"r\\[ALPHA_A0\\] =\" src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n        251                                           <- EXACTLY ONE.  This line.\n\n**So it is not a local convention, not a naming difference and not a missing symbol.**  It is one\nidentifier, in the one place the file does it differently, under a comment that says the opposite.\n\n*** AND THE ABI WRAPPER SETTLES THE INTENT INDEPENDENTLY -- IT DECLARES a0 AS THE REGISTER THE\nGUEST MUST DISCARD. ***  `alpha_cpu.h:449-459`:\n\n    register unsigned long a0 __asm(\"$16\") = ctx;\n    register unsigned long v0 __asm(\"$0\");\n    __asm volatile(\"call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx\"\n        : \"=r\" (a0), \"=r\" (v0)\n        : \"i\" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), \"0\" (a0)\n        /* clobbers t0, t8..t11, a0 (above) */\n\n`v0` is `$0` and is an OUTPUT; `a0` is an IN-OUT operand the comment explicitly calls clobbered.\n**So the model writes its result into the register the caller is told to throw away, and leaves\nuntouched the one the caller reads.**  A correct guest cannot recover the value at all.\n\n**ORDERING IS CORRECT AND IS NOT PART OF THIS DEFECT** -- `:251` publishes the OLD `ctx` before\n`:255` overwrites it with the new one.  Recorded so a fix does not \"helpfully\" reorder anything.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each rather than asserting it:** whether the handler\nshould ALSO clobber a0 for fidelity (or whether that is unobservable by construction, since no\ncorrect caller may read it); REACHABILITY -- I have NOT shown any guest this project boots calls\n`swpctx` and consumes the return, and the wrapper existing in the OS header is a different claim;\nand the witness shape, which must seed `v0` with a distinguishing value or \"the handler did nothing\nto v0\" and \"the handler wrote the right thing\" are indistinguishable.\n\n**A CROSS-ROW HAZARD CARRIED FROM `alphatbinegsel`'s panel, recorded on BOTH rows:** `:296` in this\nsame arm is `invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, 0, INVALIDATE_ALL)` under `// TODO: Don't\ninvalidate EVERYTHING!`, and glm warned that **swpctx IS the context-switch PAL, so whichever of\nthe two rows lands SECOND must re-baseline its witness with the other's fix present** or a TBIAP on\nthe switch path confuses attribution between them.  The pass-1 brief asks explicitly whether that\nTODO belongs in this round or is a separate row, under the one-site rule.\n\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/PASS1_brief.md`, fired at codex.  Written in neutral vocabulary\nand checked against the `codexcyberflag` trigger list before firing -- zero hits.",
31784:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/codex_fixdesign_20260908.txt",
31796:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **RAISED BY THE CODEX SEAT** under the found-something-else licence, on a brief that asked\nonly whether Alpha machines can run anything (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt) -- and VERIFIED by reading before\nfiling.\n\n`PAL_OSF1_swpctx' loads a new context and assigns it into the pcb.  **`apcb_ptbr' is assigned TWICE and\n`apcb_flags' is never assigned at all:**\n\n    // Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously:\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ksp    = new_ksp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_usp    = new_usp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_ptbr;      <- correct\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_cpc    = new_cpc;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_asn    = new_asn;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_unique = new_unique;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_flags;     <- *** should be apcb_flags ***\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv0 = new_decrsv0;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv1 = new_decrsv1;\n\n*** WHY THIS OUTRANKS THE OTHER ALPHA ROWS: `apcb_ptbr' IS THE PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER. ***\n`alpha_translate_v2p()' opens with `uint64_t base = cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr << ALPHA_PAGESHIFT;' -- so\nafter ANY `swpctx' the page-table walk reads its tables from an address derived from the FLAGS word.  Every\ntranslation after a context switch walks garbage.\n\n**And `apcb_flags' keeps the OUTGOING context's value**, because nothing ever writes it -- a second, quieter\ndefect from the same line.\n\n**THE COMMENT DIRECTLY ABOVE ASSERTS WHAT THE CODE DOES NOT DO:** *\"Update all variables in the pcb\nsimultaneously\"*.  Two of the nine are wrong -- one clobbered, one never set.  That is the second time in\nthis one function that a comment states an intent the code contradicts; the other is [[alphaswpctxreg]].\n\n**FIX: one token** -- the second `apcb_ptbr' becomes `apcb_flags'.  As with its sibling, the intent is not in\ndoubt: `new_flags' is loaded from the context block and would otherwise be dead, and `apcb_flags' exists in\nthe struct and would otherwise never be written.\n\n**REACHABILITY: cold-debugger reachable, per the same pass** -- `swpctx' is an active handler reached by\nordinary guest instruction dispatch with no OS dependency.  A witness plants a `call_pal 0x30' and observes\n`apcb_ptbr' afterwards.\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH [[alphapfnoffset]] IS WORTH STATING, because it changes how that round should be\ndesigned: this defect CORRUPTS THE WALK'S BASE, and that one CORRUPTS THE WALK'S RESULT. ***  A witness for\neither must control for the other -- a probe that does a `swpctx' and then observes a bad translation cannot\nattribute it without pinning which of the two produced it.  **Fix this one first**: it is certain, one token,\nand it removes a confound from the harder row.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).  THE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED AND TWO OF MY\nSUPPORTING PREMISES WERE WRONG.  Corrected here. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"A and B are defects, and both proposed corrections are right.  They are TWO changes under your\nreopening rule.  `apcb_flags is never written' is false; no third destination typo was found.\"*\n\n**CORRECTION 1 -- `apcb_flags' IS WRITTEN, and I filed the opposite as fact.**  This row said it *\"is never\nassigned at all\"*.  `grep -rn apcb_flags src/' returns `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:221' -- **`wrfen' assigns it**\nfrom `r[ALPHA_A0]' and then writes it back to PCB memory, and `swpctx' itself READS the field when saving the\noutgoing context.\n\n**The honest statement is narrower and still a defect:** `swpctx' never assigns `apcb_flags' WHEN LOADING A\nNEW CONTEXT, so the incoming context's flags are silently dropped and the outgoing context's value persists\nacross the switch.  That is a real defect; *never written anywhere* was not.\n\n**CORRECTION 2 -- `new_flags' is NOT dead, and the distinction matters.**  I wrote that it *\"would otherwise\nbe dead\"*.  codex: it *\"has exactly one use after its declaration: the erroneous PTBR assignment.  It is\notherwise unused, but **not dead in the current program**: it changes PTBR.\"*  My phrasing implied the\ncompiler would flag it; it would not, because the erroneous line consumes it.\n\n**A QUALIFICATION ON THE SIBLING [[alphaswpctxreg]] THAT BOUNDS ITS IMPACT:** the handler captures its input\ninto a LOCAL `a0' before dispatch, so overwriting `r[ALPHA_A0]' does **not** change which context is loaded.\nThat defect is therefore purely a wrong RETURN VALUE -- it does not corrupt the switch itself.  I had not\nestablished that, and it is the difference between a reporting bug and a state-corruption bug.\n\n**SCOPE: TWO CHANGES, NOT ONE.**  I intended to fix both in one diff -- same function, same class.  codex\nrules they are two under the reopening rule.  **Accepted**: the observables differ (a guest register versus\ninternal PCB state reachable only through a translation), so one test cannot cover both without a new\nobservation, which is exactly what that rule forbids.\n\n**MY Q3 HYPOTHESIS WAS REFUTED, and recording that matters as much as a finding.**  I said a third\nwrong-destination assignment was *\"more likely than not\"* given two in one function, and asked for the whole\nhandler to be read.  It was, and there is no third.  **A predicted class that does not materialise is a\nresult** -- and it stops the next round hunting for one.\n\n*** READ-ONLY AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_read_20260907.txt`).\nTHE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED FIELD BY FIELD, THE FIX IS ONE TOKEN, AND A PREMISE OF MINE ABOUT\nREACHABILITY WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN THE WITNESS. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"`swpctx` assigns `apcb_ptbr` twice, leaves `apcb_flags` unchanged, and uses all nine\nlocals.  Its return line writes `a0`.  Opcode-0 dispatch has no privilege check, but\n`alpha_palcode()` itself checks user mode before the switch.\"*\n\n**CONFIRMED, from the seat's own nine-row assignment table rather than from my description:**\n`apcb_ptbr` is assigned twice, `apcb_flags` never, and **all nine locals are used** -- so the defect\nis precisely a mis-typed destination, not a dropped load.  **The fix is ONE IDENTIFIER TOKEN on ONE\nLINE** (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`), which the seat states explicitly.  The `a0`-for-`v0` return is\nconfirmed as a second, separate defect in the same handler.\n\n*** THE CORRECTION, AND IT IS A HARNESS-BREAKER RATHER THAN A RECORDS NICETY. ***  I wrote -- in the\nbrief AND in the first draft of the Alpha harness -- that `call_pal` dispatch is unconditional.\n**Dispatch INTO `alpha_palcode()` is; the function then GATES ITS OWN SWITCH** at\n`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:183`:\n\n    uint64_t userMode = cpu->cd.alpha.ps & ALPHA_PSL_USERMODE;      /* 0x0008 */\n    if (userMode && !(palcode == 0x80 || 0x81 || 0x83 || 0xae ||\n                      0xaa || 0x86 || 0x9e || 0x92 || 0x9f)) {\n            fatal(\"[ Privileged Alpha PALcode called from user mode: TODO ]\");\n            cpu->running = 0;\n            return;\n    }\n\n**`0x30` is not on that whitelist**, so in user mode `call_pal 0x30` does not reach swpctx -- it\nHALTS THE CPU.  **That failure presents as a probe whose step never completes**, i.e. as a broken\nharness rather than as a mode problem, which is the worst kind of failure to debug.  Verified by\nreading the lines rather than taken on the seat's word.\n\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ps` has NO explicit initialiser anywhere in `src/`, so it is zero from the zeroed cpu\nstruct and USERMODE is clear -- kernel mode, and the gate does not fire.  **That is a derivation, and\nthe harness turns it into a measurement**: `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` now carries a `palgate`\nrow asserting the diagnostic is absent, and a `palalive` row asserting the machine survives a swpctx\ncall at all.\n\n*Note the shape: the premise that was wrong is the one I did NOT think to question -- I checked the\ninstruction decoder carefully and never looked inside the function it dispatches to.  That is the\nthird instance this week and it is the subject of the queued rule question\n[[sgilinearnegidxpostmortem]], which asks whether the premise-audit instruction needs a\nself-directed twin.  **This is its third data point and the first where a seat caught it before it\ncost anything.***\n\n**AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THIS WAS THE SECOND ATTEMPT.**  The first -- carrying a four-step witness\nDESIGN -- was REFUSED by the same seat as chain-shaped content (see [[codexbriefscoping]]).  The\nchain-free re-ask, which requests a field-by-field audit and names no procedure, was answered in\nfull.  **The design question went to grok instead**, so neither question was dropped.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN WAS REFUTED, BY THE SEAT THE REFUSED BRIEF WAS ROUTED TO.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_witness_20260907.txt`, 13.5 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"YES, a rung-3 witness is buildable on the three constructing Alpha machines -- but the\nproposed 'no MMU at all, two `swpctx`, read `A+16`' design is unsound as written if `A.flags` is\nnonzero.  After the first switch, `apcb_ptbr` IS the flags word, the identity map dies, and the\nsecond `call_pal` (and the save that is supposed to publish the bug) can fail to land or can\n`exit(1)` the host.  Use K0SEG for code and both PCBs, or keep `flags==0` and accept a weaker\noracle.  The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous LHS.\"*\n\n*** THE DESIGN WAS SELF-DEFEATING AND I HAD NOT SEEN IT: THE DEFECT DESTROYS THE PATH THE WITNESS\nNEEDS TO OBSERVE IT. ***  Verified in `memory_alpha.c` rather than taken on the seat's word, and the\nsource is sharper than the verdict:\n\n  * **K0SEG is checked BEFORE `base` is used at all** (`:62`): `if (!userMode && vaddr >=\n    ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE && vaddr <= ALPHA_K0SEG_END) { *return_paddr = vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff; return\n    2; }`.  A K0SEG access never consults `apcb_ptbr`, so grok's remedy is sound.\n    `ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE = 0xfffffc0000000000`; `ALPHA_PHYS_TO_K0SEG(x) = x | BASE`.\n  * **The identity map is an explicit 'UGLY hack' conditioned on `base == 0`** (`:73`).  That is the\n    ONLY reason a naive harness works at all -- `ptbr` is zero at reset.  **The moment the buggy\n    assignment makes `apcb_ptbr` nonzero, `base != 0` and every later access falls into a real\n    page-table walk over garbage**, with an `exit(1)` sitting on one vaddr at `:75-78`.\n  * **AND THE POLARITY TRAP grok did not state, which kills the 'keep flags==0' fallback too:** if\n    the marker is put in `A.ptbr` instead, then under the *FIXED* build `apcb_ptbr` becomes that\n    nonzero marker and the identity map dies on the FIX rather than on the defect.  **Neither\n    polarity survives with a nonzero value anywhere in that field.**  So K0SEG is not the better\n    option, it is the only one.\n\n**THE CORRECTED DESIGN, and it needs no 64-bit immediates:** `lda`/`ldah` build 32-bit constants\nonly, and a K0SEG address needs bit 63..42 set.  The way through is to **load the pointers BEFORE the\nfirst `swpctx`, while the identity map is still alive**: place the 64-bit K0SEG pointers in\nidentity-mapped scratch with the debugger's `put q`, have the guest `ldq` them into registers, and\nonly then issue the calls.  Every access after that carries a K0SEG address in a register and is\nimmune to whatever `apcb_ptbr` becomes.\n\n**A SEPARATE TRAP FOUND WHILE CHECKING THIS, which the harness avoids only by luck:** `memory_alpha.c:87`\nsilently remaps `(vaddr & ~0x1fff) == 0x20000000` to `PROM_ARGSPACE_PADDR` (0x12000).  The harness's\ncode at `0x20010000` and scratch at `0x20020000` fall OUTSIDE that 8 kB window -- but nothing said\nso, and a probe placed 64 kB lower would have had its stores quietly land in PROM argument space.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED:** *\"The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous\nLHS.\"*  Both seats agree the correction is `apcb_flags` at `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`.\n\n*** THE ROUTING WORKED AND IS THE REUSABLE LESSON. ***  codex REFUSED this brief as chain-shaped;\ngrok answered it in full, read the handler, the dispatch path and the existing harness, and refuted\nthe design on a mechanism I had missed.  **The chain-free re-ask to codex and the chain-shaped\noriginal to grok together cost one extra invocation and lost nothing** -- which is the argument the\n2026-09-09 adjudication is asked to rule on under [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** CODE REVIEW OF THE HARNESS, BEFORE ITS FIRST RUN.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_harness_20260907.txt` -- codex REFUSED this brief too (sixth\nrefusal, chain/witness shape), so it is grok's third answer to a brief codex would not take. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DO NOT TRUST THE FIRST RUN.  The lda/ldah/ldq bootstrap and the swpctx SAVE/RESTORE\nwalk are right.  The encodings that this file actually emits match the decoder.  `put d` is the right\ndebugger command.  What is wrong is the ADDRESS PLAN, and it makes three rows able to go green while\nmeasuring 'unmapped reads return 0', not the handler.\"*\n\n*** FINDING 1 -- FATAL, AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT AND GREEN. ***  The machine is built `-M 64`\n= 64 MB and every address in the file sat at `0x200xxxxx` (512 MB+).  K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff`, so **the K0SEG idea survives contact with `alpha_translate_v2p` and the\nPHYSICAL BACKING does not.**  And the failure mode is silent:\n`memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` only halts when `halt_on_nonexistant_memaccess` is set, i.e.\nunder `-T`.  **Without it, writes succeed and are DISCARDED and reads RETURN ZEROS** -- and an\ninstruction fetch of zeros is a real instruction, `call_pal 0`.\n\n**MY ORACLE MADE THAT UNDETECTABLE.**  It asserted `PCB_A+16 == 0` for the correct case, and 0 is\nalso what an unmapped read returns.  **The witness would have gone green having written nothing,\nread nothing, and executed `call_pal 0` off a zero-filled code page.**\n\n**REPAIRED, and the repair is better than moving the addresses:** K0SEG means translation never\nconsults `apcb_ptbr`, so `apcb_ptbr` may now hold a distinctive NONZERO value -- which the FIRST\ndesign could not allow, because a nonzero ptbr killed the identity map.  So:\n\n    correct handler  -> PCB_A+16 == PTBR_VAL  (0xaaaa1111)\n    the defect       -> PCB_A+16 == FLAGS_VAL (0xbbbb2222)\n    anything broken  -> 0, which is NEITHER, so BOTH rows red\n\nplus a third row `swpctx mapped` asserting the value is not 0 -- **because without it a probe whose\naddresses drifted back outside RAM would fail one row and pass the other, which reads exactly like a\nreal defect.**  Addresses moved inside 64 MB with an `assert` that says so.\n\n*** FINDING 2 -- `palalive` IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, AND I WROTE IT WHILE APPLYING THE RULE\nAGAINST THAT. ***  *\"`alive` is 'the pty is still readable'.  `alpha_palcode`'s default arm does\n`cpu->running = 0` and stays in the debugger because `-V` is single-step.  The emulator does not\nexit.  `palalive` stays green.\"*  **Kept as a LABELLED NON-ORACLE rather than deleted**, so the next\nreader sees why it is there and does not re-add it as a real row; the mode question is answered by\n`palgate`, which greps the diagnostic text.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- the encoder self-test does not test the decoder.**  It re-runs `load_const`'s own\n32-bit wrap masked with `& 0xffffffff`, while LDAH sign-extends through an `(int32_t)` cast --\n`0xdeadbeef` would be `0xffffffffdeadbeef` in a register and the self-test would still print ok.\n**It is sound only because every value this file uses has bit 31 clear.**  Correct by luck; recorded\nin the file as such rather than quietly fixed.\n\n**FINDING 4 -- `put q` is not merely 128-bit, it is UNIMPLEMENTED** (`\"quad-words: TODO\"`).  The\npremise was true and understated.\n\n**CONFIRMED, so the design work was not wasted:** encodings match the decoder; the bootstrap method\nis valid and nothing between the `put d` writes and the first `call_pal` touches `apcb_ptbr`; K0SEG\nis checked before `base`; PCB offsets match `struct alpha_pcb`; `ps` is zero from `zeroed_alloc`;\n`0x30` is not on the unprivileged whitelist.  It also supplied two facts I did not have:\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ctx` starts at `0x10100`, and PROM setup (on by default) sets `a0 = 2048`.\n\n*** THE REVIEW COST ONE INVOCATION AND SAVED A GREEN WITNESS THAT PROVED NOTHING. ***  The lifecycle\nsays ask a seat to try a rung-3 probe; this says ask a seat to READ the probe before running it, and\non this evidence the second is worth as much as the first.\n\n*** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND RUN, 2026-09-07.  THE DEFECT REPRODUCES ON THE COMMITTED BUILD. ***\n\n    row              got          want              \n    encoder          ok           ok                load_const sign correction, 9 vectors, offline\n    live             0x5a5a5a5a   0x5a5a5a5a        LIVENESS: guest store then load through RAM\n    exec             0x1234       0x1234            a guest LDAH/LDA pair reached a register\n    palgate          not gated    not gated         call_pal 0x30 is privileged; we are in kernel mode\n    palalive         alive        alive             NON-ORACLE, labelled as such\n    swpctx alive     alive        alive             the guest survives two swpctx calls in K0SEG\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111        FAIL -- the defect\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222    FAIL -- the defect's exact signature\n    swpctx mapped    0xbbbb2222   NOT 0x0           ok  -- the memory WAS there\n    ALPHAHARNESS_RESULT=7/9\n\n**THE MECHANISM, confirmed by the measurement rather than only by reading:** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`\ncase `0x30` LOADs the new context, sets `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr`, and then **overwrites it with\n`new_flags` a few lines later**.  The probe stages PCB_A with ptbr `0xaaaa1111` and flags\n`0xbbbb2222`, swaps A in, swaps B in (whose SAVE writes A's context back to `PCB_A+16`), and reads\nthat cell.  It holds the FLAGS.\n\n*** `swpctx mapped` IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PHANTOM, and it exists\nbecause a code review demanded it. ***  The grok harness review found that **every address in the\nfirst draft was planted past the end of RAM** -- `-M 64` gives 64 MB and the structures sat at\n`0x20010000`+ (512 MB) -- where writes are discarded and reads return zeros with no diagnostic.\nThree rows would have gone GREEN while measuring *unmapped memory returns 0*.  The repair moved\neverything below 64 MB **and added `assert max(P_CODE, P_PTRV, P_PCB_A, P_PCB_B) < 64*1024*1024`**,\nso the class cannot be reintroduced silently.  `swpctx mapped` then pins the same property at run\ntime: a `0` readback is reported as a SETUP FAILURE, not as a result.\n\n> **THAT IS THE THIRD DISTINCT ROW-CLASS THIS SESSION WHOSE WHOLE PURPOSE IS TO SEPARATE 'MEASURED\n> NOTHING' FROM 'MEASURED A ZERO'** -- the UBSan in-band control, the kill-table NUL check, and this.\n> They were built independently and they are all the same rule: *an apparatus that cannot fail\n> loudly will fail quietly and be believed.*\n\n**TEST-FIRST IS SATISFIED:** the witness shows the symptom on the committed build BEFORE any edit,\nwhich is what licenses a fix.  The probe exits non-zero on a red row, so `rc=1` here is the correct\noutcome and not an error.\n\n**AND IT UNBLOCKS [[alphapfnoffset]]**, which is the reason this row was worked first: that row's\nwitness needs a non-zero `apcb_ptbr` and a valid page table, and *this* defect clobbers `apcb_ptbr`\nwith the flags word -- so any translation probe built before this was fixed could not attribute a\nbad translation to the walk rather than to the swap.  The confound is now measured rather than\nsuspected.\n\n*** A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THIS ROW DOES NOT RECORD, found by COUNTING the assignments rather than\nreading them.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nOver `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:274-292`, the load block's field assignments are:\n\n    apcb_ksp 1   apcb_usp 1   apcb_cpc 1   apcb_asn 1   apcb_unique 1\n    apcb_decrsv0 1   apcb_decrsv1 1\n    apcb_ptbr  **2**        <- :278 = new_ptbr, :282 = new_flags\n    apcb_flags **0**        <- never assigned at all\n\n**So `:282` is a field-name transposition: it should be `apcb_flags = new_flags`.**  The value IS\nloaded -- the SAVE block stores `apcb_flags` to `ctx + 40` and the loader reads `new_flags` from\n`ctx + 40` -- and then lands in the wrong field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE 1 is the clobber the witness measures: `apcb_ptbr` ends up holding the flags word.**\n> **CONSEQUENCE 2 is new: `apcb_flags` is NEVER LOADED, so a context switch silently drops the\n> incoming context's flags.**  The row's title names only the first.\n\n*And the block's own comment at `:275` reads **\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"** --\nwhich is exactly the property it fails to have.  A comment asserting completeness directly above an\nincomplete list is the shape this project keeps finding: the claim is the thing least likely to be\nre-checked, because it reads as a description of the code below it.*\n\n**The counting is what found it.**  Reading the block, `:282` looks like an ordinary assignment in a\ncolumn of ordinary assignments; the eye supplies the field name it expects.  A `grep -o 'pcb\\.apcb_[a-z0-9]*\n*=' | sort | uniq -c` over the block makes the 2-and-0 unmissable -- **a census of a nine-line block,\nwhich is not where anyone thinks to run one.**\n\n*Fix design out to grok (`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/FIX_brief.md`) before any edit: whether\none token repairs both consequences, what reads `apcb_flags` today and might rely on it being stale,\nand whether the witness then goes 9/9 or only 8/9.*\n\n*** THE GROK FIX REVIEW RATIFIED THE ONE-TOKEN EDIT AND REFUSED TO RATIFY IT AS THE ROUND --\nbecause the witness was BLIND to the second consequence.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n*\"Both red rows read the same `got': A+16.  A build that only stops clobbering PTBR -- delete\n`:282', or assign new_flags to any other field -- also scores 9/9.  Consequence 2 is unpinned.\"*\n\n**That is a clause-(d) gap in my own witness**, and the repair it named is one readback on a page the\nprobe had already proved was backed.  Added as `swpctx flags' (PCB_A + 40) and MEASURED:\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111       FAIL   the clobber\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222   FAIL   the clobber's signature\n    swpctx flags     0x0          0xbbbb2222       FAIL   apcb_flags NEVER LOADED\n    HEAD 7/9 -> 7/10\n\n**Three red rows now pin three distinct consequences instead of one cell read twice.**\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, and a THIRD consequence found:**\n  * `apcb_flags` IS written globally -- by `wrfen` at `:221`.  My \"never assigned at all\" is true\n    only inside the load block, and I had stated it without that scope.\n  * *\"whatever the previous context left\"* is wrong: `swpctx` has never loaded that field from any\n    PCB, so the in-cpu value is **last `wrfen`, or 0 from the zeroed cpu struct**.\n  * **THIRD CONSEQUENCE:** today the SAVE at `:243-244` publishes that never-loaded word into the\n    OUTGOING PCB -- so a context switch *wipes the flags the guest planted*.  The bug propagates\n    outward into guest memory, not just inward into the cpu struct.\n\n**Every `apcb_flags` site in `src/` was enumerated and none relies on the stale value** (`wrfen`\nwrites without reading; the SAVE publishes; the header constants and `ALPHA_IF_CODE_FEN` are unused;\n`memory_alpha.c:46` reads `apcb_ptbr`, not flags).  *Sticky-FEN-across-`swpctx` is a PAL bug, not a\nbehaviour a reader is entitled to.*  **A separate finding volunteered in passing: `PAL_clrfen`\n(`0xae`) is named and user-mode-whitelisted but has NO `case`, so it reaches the default arm and\nstops the CPU.**\n\n*** `#507` APPLIED.  THE CENSUS THAT FOUND THE DEFECT IS THE CENSUS THAT CONFIRMS THE FIX. ***\n\n    before   apcb_ptbr 2   apcb_flags 0   (every other field 1)\n    after    apcb_ptbr 1   apcb_flags 1   (every field exactly 1)\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt, two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  *The witness re-run is in flight; its result is\nNOT recorded here until it lands, because a number written before the run that produces it cannot be\ntold from a number fitted to the answer.*\n\n*** `#507` MEASURED: 7/10 -> 10/10, SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx flags     0x0        -> 0xbbbb2222     <- consequence 2, pinned by the row grok demanded\n\nSix controls green on both runs.  Clause (i) discharged by the pair.  **Nothing committed** -- the\nFable hold stands and this waits as finished work rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n                                   contains the `#507' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
31807:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the `swpctx' design pass (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt), raised under the\nfound-something-else licence.\n\ncodex enumerated the comparison rather than reasoning about it: *\"Local `a0' is unsigned.  Enumerating the\ncomparison gives `-2 -> VADDR', `-1 -> VADDR', `0 -> ALL', `1 -> VADDR', `2 -> VADDR', `3 -> VADDR'.  The\nheader's independent definitions require all-entry and all-per-process invalidation for the negative\nselectors.\"*\n\nSo `tbi(-1)' and `tbi(-2)' -- the OS's way of saying *invalidate everything* / *invalidate all\nper-process entries* -- are treated as *invalidate one virtual address*.  **A TLB flush that silently\nflushes almost nothing is the dangerous direction**: the guest believes stale translations are gone.\n\nNOT INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED BY ME.  The citation is given and the check is one comparison plus the header's\nselector definitions.  Filed rather than confirmed because the Alpha queue is now six rows deep and this\none needs the header read alongside the code.\n\n*** VERIFIED IN PART, 2026-09-08, BY codex -- AND IT REFUSED TO VERIFY THE HALF MY BRIEF HAD MADE\nUNANSWERABLE. ***\n\n**THE DISPATCH IS CONFIRMED.**  `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`, `case 0x33 /* PAL_OSF1_tbi */`:\n\n    if (a0 >= 1)\n            cpu->invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, a1, INVALIDATE_VADDR);\n    else\n            cpu->invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, 0, INVALIDATE_ALL);\n\n`a0` is a `uint64_t` local copied from `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0]`, itself `uint64_t`\n(`src/include/cpu_alpha.h`).  **There is no active signed cast** -- the only cast is inside a\ncommented-out `debug()`, which has no effect.  So `-2` is `0xff..fe`, `a0 >= 1` is TRUE, and both\nnegative selectors take the VADDR branch.  All six enumerated: `-2, -1, 1, 2, 3 -> VADDR`, `0 -> ALL`.\n\n**THE HEADER DEFINITIONS ARE REAL AND THEY ARE THE POINT** -- `src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h`:\n\n    #define ALPHA_TBIA()     alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)     /* all TB entries */\n    #define ALPHA_TBIAP()    alpha_pal_tbi(-1, 0)     /* all per-process */\n    #define ALPHA_TBISI(va)  alpha_pal_tbi(1, (va))\n    #define ALPHA_TBISD(va)  alpha_pal_tbi(2, (va))\n    #define ALPHA_TBIS(va)   alpha_pal_tbi(3, (va))\n\nSo the two selectors meaning *invalidate broadly* are exactly the two the comparison misroutes.\nSelector `0` is NOT defined by the header, so its fallback to INVALIDATE_ALL is not a requirement.\n\n*** WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, AND THE SEAT WAS RIGHT TO SAY SO: WHETHER THE TWO BRANCHES\nDIFFER IN EFFECT. ***  *\"The handler proves that different callback arguments are selected.  It does\nnot prove what the callback does with them ... I would not promote this to a verified functional\ninvalidation defect yet.\"*\n\n**THAT GAP IS MY BRIEF'S FAULT.**  I scoped its reading to the PALcode file and the header -- a habit\nadopted to keep the codex content filter from refusing -- and that scope EXCLUDES\n`invalidate_translation_caches`, which is the only thing that can answer the question.\n\n> *** THE TENSION IS WORTH NAMING BECAUSE IT WILL RECUR: NARROWING codex's READING SCOPE TO AVOID\n> THE CONTENT FILTER CAN STARVE THE QUESTION. ***  The scoping remedy in [[codexsubjectrefusal]] is\n> about which CORPUS trips the filter; it is not free, and here it cost the answer.  The seat named\n> the missing scope precisely rather than guessing, which is the behaviour to want.  Re-fired with\n> the widened scope as `codex_q3_20260908.txt`.\n\n**PROPOSED REPAIR (conditional on that answer):** `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)`, which sends both\nnegative selectors to the existing INVALIDATE_ALL path.  **And the seat flagged the disposition\nrisk:** if the callback ignores the flags or full-flushes either way, the source is still wrong but\nthe defect is UNOBSERVABLE -- documented-only, not a behavioural round.  A dispatch oracle (assert\nthe callback's `(address, flags)` for the six selectors) is decisive about DISPATCH and says nothing\nabout guest effect.\n\n*** Q3 IS SETTLED AND THE ANSWER IS (a): THE TWO BRANCHES DIFFER, AND `ALPHA_TBIA()` -- THE OS's\n\"INVALIDATE EVERY TLB ENTRY\" PRIMITIVE -- CURRENTLY INVALIDATES **VIRTUAL PAGE ZERO** AND NOTHING\nELSE.  codex, 2026-09-08, with the widened reading scope it asked for. ***\n\n**THE TRACE, end to end, each hop quoted in the answer:** `cpu_alpha.c` binds the callback to\n`alpha_invalidate_translation_caches`; `tmp_alpha_tail.c` generates it via\n`#define DYNTRANS_INVALIDATE_TC ... #include \"cpu_dyntrans.c\"`; and the two branches in\n`cpu_dyntrans.c` are genuinely different code:\n\n    VADDR   for (r=0; r<...; r++)\n                if (entry[r].valid &&\n                    (entry[r].vaddr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask) == (addr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask))\n                        INVALIDATE_TLB_ENTRY(...)          <- ADDRESS PREDICATE\n\n    ALL     if (flags & INVALIDATE_ALL) {\n                for (r=0; r<...; r++)\n                    if (entry[r].valid)\n                        INVALIDATE_TLB_ENTRY(..., 0);      <- NO ADDRESS PREDICATE\n                return;\n            }\n\n*** THE ARITHMETIC THAT MAKES IT BITE. ***  `ALPHA_TBIA()` is `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)` -- selector\n`-2`, **vaddr `0`**.  The unsigned comparison sends it to the VADDR branch, which then matches on\n`a1 == 0`.  Alpha uses 8 KiB pages and ordinary CPU construction leaves `vaddr_mask` ALL-ONES, so\nthe predicate matches **cached virtual page zero only -- and nothing at all if that page is not\ncached.  Every other mapping survives a flush the guest OS believes cleared the whole TLB.**\n\n**THE GUEST-VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE IS ESTABLISHED, not inferred:** a previously cached, subsequently\nremapped nonzero virtual page can still reach its OLD physical backing, because the Alpha load/store\nfast path consumes the surviving `host_load`/`host_store` pointers that\n`DYNTRANS_INVALIDATE_TLB_ENTRY` would have NULLed.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED THE ESCAPE HATCH I OFFERED. ***  My brief invited *\"if Alpha has no bootable\nin-tree rig that exercises paging, say so -- documented-only is a real answer.\"*  The seat declined\nit and gave three reasons: `memory_alpha.c` carries an ACTIVE three-level page-table walker (the\n\"Not yet implemented\" diagnostic is inside a specific zero-PTBR bootstrap case, not the walker);\nAlphaBook 1 IS registered in `machine_alpha.c`; and `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` and\n`alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py` already drive the ordinary Alpha machine with liveness and PAL-dispatch\ncontrols.  **\"Neither inherent unobservability nor absent paging support justifies closing it as\ndocumented-only.\"**\n\n**HONEST STATUS, in its words: \"source-confirmed functional defect; witness unvalidated.\"**  It ran\nno before/after comparison and found no dedicated test.  So the round still owes the rung-3\ndifferential -- but the basis for building one exists and is named.\n\n*** SEVERITY RAISED med -> high. ***  A documented OS primitive for *invalidate everything* clears\none page or none, silently, while the guest proceeds on the assumption that it worked -- and the\nconsequence is a guest reaching stale physical backing through the fast path.  That is the same\nclass as the other HIGH rows here (silent wrong data reaching the guest), not a cosmetic one.\n**This is a judgment and it is flagged for the adjudication rather than treated as settled**: the\nunderlying facts are codex's, verified by citation; the ranking is mine.\n\n> *** THE CLUSTER ORDERING NEEDS REVISITING, AND codex'S OWN CAVEAT SAYS WHY. ***  It ranked\n> `alphaswpctxreg` FIRST and labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the\n> greatest potential severity\"* -- explicitly because `alphatbinegsel` had \"confirmed dispatch\n> behavior but unverified functional consequences.\"  **Those consequences are now verified.**  The\n> ordering input has changed since the ranking was given, so the ranking should not be quoted as\n> though it still holds.\n\n**REPAIR, unchanged and now on a settled basis:** `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)`, sending both defined\nnegative selectors to INVALIDATE_ALL.  codex supplied the accurate CHANGELOG claim and its limit:\n\n> *\"Correct Alpha PAL `tbi` handling of negative selectors so broad invalidation clears all cached\n> address mappings.\"*\n\nIt **retains coarse full flushing for `-1`** and does NOT implement `ALPHA_TBIAP()`'s selective\npreservation of global (ASM=0) entries -- so the round must claim the coarse repair and disclose\nthat, rather than claiming the architectural semantics.\n\n*One stale in-tree comment fell out of the same trace and is worth a separate look: a comment\nclaiming the VADDR path \"clears only the quick array\" is stale for this implementation -- the\n64-bit entry helper NULLs `host_load`, `host_store`, `phys_addr` and `phys_page` and clears the TLB\nentry's `valid` flag.*\n\n*** WITNESS DESIGN: codex REFUSED IT AT THE PROMPT (2026-09-08), and the refusal is informative\nrather than merely inconvenient. ***\n\n`codex_witness_20260908.txt` is 0 bytes; its `.err` is 6,291 bytes with **zero file-read lines** --\nso it refused before opening anything, which makes this the CHAIN-SHAPE trigger recorded in\n[[codexsubjectrefusal]] rather than the archive-reading one.  The same seat answered three other\nbriefs on this very defect tonight, including the callback trace that settled Q3.\n\n**The distinction is between ANALYSING existing code and CONSTRUCTING a guest sequence that reaches\nthe defect.  Witness design is inherently the second**, so codex is the wrong seat for it -- not a\ndegraded one.  Re-fired the identical brief, byte-for-byte, at grok.\n\n*** THE WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE, AND grok SUPPLIED IT.  2026-09-08, on the brief codex refused at\nthe prompt -- reused BYTE-FOR-BYTE, so this is a seat-capability result and not a rewrite. ***\n\n**RUNG 3, unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.**  `machine_alpha.c` already `device_add`s LCA for\n`ST_ALPHABOOK1`, so nothing is added to reach PAL 0x33 -- which is the ladder's clause (iii).\n\n**THE OBSERVABLE IS A STALE DYNTRANS HIT AFTER A PTE REMAP, not a dispatch spy.**  That is the\nguest-visible channel codex could not name from its narrower scope: fill a mapping, remap the PTE\nbehind it, `ALPHA_TBIA()`, then load and see the OLD value.\n\n*** THE CONSTRAINT THAT DECIDES WHETHER THE ROW IS REAL OR VACUOUS, and it is a probe-design trap I\nwould have walked into: THE DEBUGGER PROMPT ALL-FLUSHES BETWEEN INTERACTIONS. ***  `debugger.c`,\nwhen `debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction` reaches 0, calls `cpu_create_or_reset_tc` and\n`invalidate_translation_caches(..., INVALIDATE_ALL)`.\n\n> **So the FILL, the REMAP, the `tbi` and the OBSERVING LOAD must all be inside ONE `step N`.  Split\n> them across two `step`s and BOTH builds re-walk after the remap -- the row goes GREEN ON HEAD and\n> the witness proves nothing.**  Every existing probe in this tree is written as a sequence of\n> debugger interactions, so the obvious construction is the broken one.\n\n**AND THE OBVIOUS SUBSTRATE CANNOT WITNESS IT EITHER:** an identity map (`ptbr == 0`) has no PTE to\nchange, and **K0SEG is checked BEFORE `ptbr`** in `memory_alpha.c` -- so a K0SEG address never\nconsults the page table at all.  The witness needs a real three-level walk, which is why it reuses\n`alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py`'s page-table substrate rather than starting from scratch.\n\n**THE SEQUENCE** (one session, one `step N`), with the walk planted through K0SEG while `ptbr` is\nstill 0:\n\n    ldq  a0, k0(PCB)\n    call_pal 0x30              # swpctx: arm the walk, and it ALL-flushes\n    lda  t0, 0x2000            # a NON-ZERO VPN -- see the page-0 constraint\n    ldq  t1, 0(t0)             # FILL: cold walk, host_load -> P_OLD\n    ldq  t4, planted PTE(P_NEW)\n    stq  t4, 0(k0(L3+8))       # remap via K0SEG, NOT via the walked VPN\n    lda  a0, -2(zero)          # a0 = 0xfffffffffffffffe\n    lda  a1, 0(zero)           # TBIA's a1 -- must NOT be the test VPN\n    call_pal 0x33              # PAL_OSF1_tbi\n    ldq  t2, 0(t0)             # OBSERVE: VAL_OLD on HEAD, VAL_NEW after the fix\n\n**Q3 (page-0) answered and extended:** the buggy arm invalidates virtual page 0, so a test VPN of 0\nwould pass on BOTH builds -- vacuous by address choice.  grok adds a second constraint I had not\nasked for: **`a1` must not be the test VPN either**, or the VADDR arm matches it and the row goes\ngreen for the wrong reason.\n\n**AN INSTRUMENT CONTROL IT VOLUNTEERED:** `lda a0, -2(zero)` really does sign-extend (`lda_0` does\n`(int64_t)(int32_t)arg[2]`, and LDA sign-extends its int16), **but print `a0` and `a1` after the\nprogram anyway** -- a truncated `0xfffe` would ALSO take the VADDR arm on HEAD and the ALL arm under\nthe fix, so the row would still move while no longer testing `TBIA`.  *That is a control against the\ninstrument rather than against the emulator, and it is exactly the hand-assembled-encoding trap this\nproject has recorded four times.*\n\n**IT ALSO CONFIRMED ALL SIX PREMISES AND CORRECTED A STALE IN-TREE COMMENT** -- the comment claiming\nthe VADDR path \"only clears the quick array, not `vph_tlb_entry[]`\" is stale: the 64-bit helper does\nNULL `host_load`/`host_store`/`phys_addr`/`phys_page` and clear `valid`.  **The bug is WHICH entries\nare selected, not that the helper is inert.**  It adds that the ALL arm does not implement ASM=0\neither, *\"extra, not a contradiction\"* -- consistent with codex's caveat that the proposed repair is\ncoarse and does not give `ALPHA_TBIAP()` its selective semantics.\n\n**REMAINING: build it.**  The design is complete enough to implement; nothing here has been run, so\nthe row's status stays *source-confirmed functional defect; witness DESIGNED, not yet measured*.\n\n*** THE DEBUGGER ALL-FLUSH IS VERIFIED BY ME, not taken from the seat.  The anchor is\n`grep -n debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction src/debugger/debugger.c` -- note the path is\n`src/debugger/`, NOT `src/core/`, which is where my first grep looked and found nothing. ***\n\n    :781  if (debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction > 0) {\n    :782          debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction --;\n          } else {\n    :793          cpu_create_or_reset_tc(debugger_machine->cpus[i]);\n    :795          invalidate_translation_caches(\n\nSo the flush fires on the branch taken when the counter is NOT positive -- i.e. whenever the\ndebugger is about to interact.  **The one-`step N` constraint is real.**\n\n*A file-not-found from a wrong path is the safe direction: it is loud.  Had I grepped a path that\nEXISTS but is the wrong copy, I would have gotten a confident empty result instead -- which is the\npristine-`src/` trap this project has recorded, and the reason the citation-form rule prefers a\nverified grep anchor over a bare line number.*\n\n*** THE ASSESS STAGE HERE IS NOT A PANEL, AND THE GATE IS RIGHT TO SAY SO.  2/9. ***\n\n`check_stage_panels` reports this stage as having moved on without the full panel -- missing\nfable5, opus5, agy, kimi, glm, deepseek and minimax.  **That is accurate.**  What ran was TWO\nTARGETED BRIEFS, not a panel: codex on the dispatch and the callback trace, then grok on the\nwitness design after codex refused it.\n\n**I am recording this rather than backfilling.**  The owner's rule is FULL PANEL ON EVERY STAGE and\nSTOP AND ASK if a seat cannot be run -- *not degrade quietly* -- and quietly is exactly how a\ntwo-seat investigation becomes a \"panel\" in the record.\n\n*** THE THREE SEATS THAT COULD NOT HAVE RUN, by name and reason: opus5 and fable5 are PRESERVED for\nthe 2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive, and agy is the MEASUREMENT RESERVE by the\n2026-08-30 ruling.  The four packet-fed transports COULD have run and were not fired -- that is a\nchoice I made, not a constraint. ***\n\n**So this row's assess stage owes a real panel before it is treated as adjudicated**, and the\nfindings it produced -- the dispatch defect, the callback trace, the witness design -- stand on\ntheir own as two seats' work, correctly attributed.\n\n*** THE ASSESS PANEL FIRED (four packet-fed seats) AND THREE OF FOUR FOUND THE SAME DEFECT -- IN MY\nBRIEF, NOT IN THE CODE. ***\n\n**THE SEVERITY RAISE AND THE COARSE REPAIR ARE CONFIRMED 4/4.**  glm, kimi, minimax and deepseek all\nsustain med -> high and all accept shipping `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)` with the ASM=0 limit\ndisclosed.\n\n*** DEFECT 1, FOUND INDEPENDENTLY BY glm, kimi AND minimax: MY POLARITY WAS INVERTED. ***  The brief\nsaid *\"VADDR keeps an entry only when `(vaddr_page & vaddr_mask) == (addr_page & vaddr_mask)`\"*.\n**The VADDR branch INVALIDATES the matching entry; it does not keep it.**  Verified by opening\n`cpu_dyntrans.c`: `if (flags & INVALIDATE_VADDR)` runs a loop whose own comment reads *\"they must be\ninvalidated based on the bits that matter\"*.\n\n**The CONCLUSION is unaffected** -- with `a1 = 0` the arm invalidates page zero and leaves every\nother mapping, which is the defect as filed -- **but the sentence stating the mechanism contradicted\nits own THEREFORE**, and glm said so in exactly those terms.  *A packet-fed seat can only reason\nfrom the packet, so an inverted premise there is the one error they cannot route around; that three\nof four caught it anyway is the premise-audit instruction working.*\n\n*** DEFECT 2, FOUND BY glm AND minimax: \"THE CONSEQUENCES ARE NOW VERIFIED\" OVERCLAIMS. ***  codex's\nown status line was *\"source-confirmed functional defect; witness UNVALIDATED\"*, and I compressed\nthat to \"verified\" when asking whether the ordering should change.  **What is verified is the\nDISPATCH.  The witness is DESIGNED, NOT BUILT.**  glm: *\"'witness constructible, shape known' is not\nverification -- do not promote this row to first on the packet as written.\"*\n\n**SO THE ORDERING QUESTION IS NOT SETTLED AND MUST NOT BE RECORDED AS IF IT WERE.**  kimi says this\nrow goes first; glm says not on the evidence as stated.  **Both are answering honestly and they\ndisagree because my premise was wrong.**  It goes to the adjudication as a live question with the\noverclaim corrected.\n\n**RESIDUALS WORTH KEEPING:**\n* **kimi's adversarial re-break: `a0 + 2 <= 5`** -- an unsigned shifted-window test that re-admits\n  the negative selectors while looking like a range check.  deepseek's is\n  `if (a0 != 0 && a0 != (uint64_t)-1)`.  **Both belong in the round's mutant list.**\n* **the designed witness is BLIND to stale STORES** (a same-VA store then load reads back green on a\n  broken build) **and to permission-only remaps** -- kimi.  A round building it owes those channels\n  or a disclosure.\n* deepseek, Q4: the witness is vacuous when the old and new data happen to match -- so the remap\n  must change the observed bytes, not merely the mapping.\n\n*** THE agy SEAT ANSWERED AND IT IS THE STRONGEST ANSWER ON THIS ROW: A FILE-READING SEAT THAT\nRE-DERIVED EVERY PREMISE FROM SOURCE RATHER THAN RATIFYING THE PACKET. ***\n\n**It confirms Q1 high and Q2 the coarse repair, and it re-verified the six inlined premises with\nfile and line** -- the uint64_t declaration, the two OS wrappers, the two dyntrans branches,\nvaddr_mask, the 8 KiB page size, the debugger ALL-FLUSH, the K0SEG early return, and the\nMEM_MIPS/MEM_M88K-only device-failure arms with ALPHA_KENTRY_MM having zero consumers.\n\n*** IT IS THE FOURTH INDEPENDENT STATEMENT OF THE CORRECT POLARITY, and it did not have to be\ntold. ***  Unprompted, it writes that INVALIDATE_VADDR *\"invalidates only entries matching\n(vaddr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask) == (addr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask)\"* -- which is the opposite of my\nbrief's *\"keeps an entry only when\"*.  glm, kimi and minimax caught the inversion from the packet\nalone; agy simply states the truth from the file.\n\n**Q2 gained a REASON the packet-fed seats could not supply**, and it is the reason that makes the\ncoarse repair right rather than merely acceptable: *\"GXemul's translation cache does not track ASM\nbits or ASN tags per entry in vph_tlb_entry / l1_64 at all\"*, so ASM=0 selectivity is not a finer\nsemantics this round declined to implement -- **it has no representation to be implemented in.**\nFlushing everything on ALPHA_TBIAP() is conservative and correct against that structure.\n\n*** FOUR ADVERSARIAL RE-BREAKS FOR Q3, AND THE FOURTH IS A CLAUSE-(d) NULL-REPAIR. ***\n\n    (uint32_t)a0 >= 1        looks like the 32-bit PALcode argument convention; 0xfffffffe >= 1\n    a0 > 0                   looks like a clean equivalent of a0 >= 1; -2 as uint64_t is > 0\n    a0 >= 1 || a0 <= 3       a one-character typo that is true for every 64-bit value\n    else -> INVALIDATE_VADDR the GUARD is correctly fixed and the ELSE ARM still says VADDR\n\n**The fourth is the one a mutant table would most likely miss**, because every row aimed at the\nGUARD sees a correct guard.  It is clause (d) exactly: keep the repair, null its effect -- and it\nbelongs in the round's mutant list beside kimi's `a0 + 2 <= 5` and deepseek's\n`a0 != 0 && a0 != (uint64_t)-1`.\n\n*** FIVE WITNESS BLIND CHANNELS, AND TWO ARE NEW AND SHARPER THAN THE THREE ALREADY FILED. ***\n\n* **TEST VPN = PAGE 0 IS SELF-DEFEATING.**  HEAD's defective arm flushes page zero, so a witness\n  that happens to pick VPN 0 goes GREEN ON THE DEFECTIVE BUILD.  *The defect is that only page 0\n  is flushed, so page 0 is the one page that cannot witness it.*\n* **A POLLUTED a1 MASKS IT.**  If a1 is not explicitly zeroed before `call_pal 0x33`, HEAD flushes\n  whatever a1 holds -- so if a1 happens to carry the test VPN the broken build looks correct.\n* the other three restate what kimi and deepseek already gave: value collision, split debugger\n  steps, and K0SEG or ptbr == 0 bypassing the page table.\n\n**Q5 ORDERING: agy says THIS ROW FIRST, and it is careful about why.**  It does NOT repeat my\nwithdrawn overclaim -- it writes *\"witness FEASIBILITY\"*, which is what is actually established,\nand it grounds the ranking on a severity comparison it made itself: `alphaswpctxreg` is *\"medium\nseverity (writing the return value to a0 rather than v0)\"*, and swpctx's own context arming via\napcb_ptbr works regardless of which register receives the old context address.\n\n*** I WROTE \"THE ORDERING NOW READS 3-1\" AND IT READS 2-3 THE OTHER WAY.  Corrected the same\nhour, from the answers, before this record shipped. ***\n\nI tallied from my own summary instead of from the five files, and got both numbers wrong --\nincluding deepseek, whose closing sentence is *\"if this row is high and verified, it may go\nfirst, but that is not established from the packet.\"*  **That is a tally of seats written from\nmemory, which is the exact class this project keeps paying for.**  Read from the artifacts:\n\n    kimi        THIS ROW FIRST    \"the ranking variable was uncertainty; it is now zero\"\n    agy         THIS ROW FIRST    on a severity comparison it made itself from source\n    glm         NOT YET           \"constructibility is not verification; no executed differential\"\n    deepseek    NOT ESTABLISHED   cannot rank without alphaswpctxreg's severity\n    minimax     NOT YET           \"mark ready to elevate once the witness actually runs\"\n\n> **BUT THE COUNT IS THE LEAST INFORMATIVE THING HERE, AND THE ARGUMENTS CONVERGE COMPLETELY.**\n> All three NOT-YET seats give ONE reason and it is the same reason: **the witness has not been\n> RUN.**  None of them disputes the severity or the mechanism.  So this is not a 2-3 split to be\n> resolved by weight -- it is a unanimous statement of the CONDITION under which the ordering\n> flips, and that condition is exactly what opus5's hold is for.  *Count the arguments, not the\n> seats.*\n\n**minimax put the trap in the sharpest form:** *\"The previous ranking was honest.  Flipping it on\nthe strength of 'constructible in principle' is the same shape of error in reverse.\"*\n\n*** FOUR FINDINGS THAT WERE SITTING BELOW THE VERDICT LINES, AND I HAD READ ONLY THE TAILS. ***\n\n* **glm: THE THEREFORE NAMES ONLY `ALPHA_TBIA()`, AND `ALPHA_TBIAP()` IS EQUALLY BROKEN.**\n  `-1` misdispatches identically, so **the CONTEXT-SWITCH primitive is broken too** -- which glm\n  says *\"strengthens Q1 and matters for Q5\"*.  agy's verdict line independently names both\n  wrappers.  My brief's conclusion sentence understated the defect's own reach.\n* **glm: MY SEVERITY ARGUMENT HAS AN UNESTABLISHED PREMISE -- \"during ordinary operation\".**\n  Nothing shown demonstrates a supported guest executing `tbi` with a negative operand during\n  normal running; the wrappers existing in the OS header is not the same claim.  **Settling fact\n  named: a trace of PAL 0x33 invocations with their `a0` values during an ordinary boot.**  glm\n  confirms high on per-invocation consequence regardless, but flags that if the rubric wants\n  demonstrated in-guest incidence, that trace is the missing artifact.  *That is a premise audit\n  landing on the one judgment in this row that is mine.*\n* **glm: A CROSS-ROW ATTRIBUTION HAZARD.**  This repair changes context-switch flush behaviour and\n  `swpctx` IS the context-switch PAL -- so **whichever of the two lands SECOND must re-baseline its\n  witness with the other's fix present**, or a TBIAP on the switch path confuses attribution\n  between the two rows.  Neither row records this today.\n* **kimi's Q2 open question was CLOSED BY agy, and neither seat knew.**  kimi: *\"the one fact that\n  would change this answer: do tc entries carry an ASN/ASM field?  Not stated; if they do not, the\n  fine semantics are not even implementable here.\"*  agy read the source: **they do not** --\n  `vph_tlb_entry` and `l1_64` carry no ASM/ASN.  So Q2's answer is not *coarse repair accepted with\n  a disclosed limit* but the stronger **the finer semantics have no representation to be\n  implemented in**, and the disclosure should say that instead.\n\n**AND kimi's Q3 CARRIES A REVIEWER INVARIANT WORTH MORE THAN THE MUTANT:** its `a0 + 2 <= 3 + 2`\nadmits exactly {-2,-1,0,1,2,3} by mod-2^64 wrap, so **every one of the five documented wrappers\ntakes the identical branch it took on the broken build** and any test authored against the\ndefective behaviour stays green.  The honest look-alike `(a0 - 1) <= 2` is CORRECT -- which is\nprecisely why the `+2` form survives review.  kimi's invariant: *\"the true-branch preimage over\nall 2^64 inputs must be exactly {1,2,3}; any test expressed as addition or subtraction on the\nunsigned a0 is a red flag.\"*\n\n**A SECOND ROW GAINS AN UNSOLICITED SEVERITY READ:** `alphaswpctxreg` medium, argued from the\nregister semantics.  It is carried to that row rather than acted on here.\n\n*** Q2 IS NOW SETTLED FROM PRIMARY SOURCE PLUS A GREP, AND THE ANSWER IS STRONGER THAN THE\nQUESTION I ASKED. ***  The 21066 manual arrived on a DIFFERENT row (`lcaconfbus1kill`, fetched at\ncodex's citation) and `machine_alpha.c:61` does `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`, so it is the right\nmanual for this machine's chipset.\n\n**WHAT THE HARDWARE DOES** -- `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:1484-1486` (ITB) and `:1684-1686`\n(DTB), read locally, not taken from a seat:\n\n> *\"The ITBs support a single address space number (ASN) through the PTE ASM bit.  Each PTE entry\n> in the ITB contains an ASM bit.  **Write operations to the ITBASM register invalidate all entries\n> that do not have the ASM bit set.**\"*\n\nThat is exactly `ALPHA_TBIAP()`'s selectivity, documented, and it confirms the round was right to\ncall the repair COARSE rather than complete.\n\n**WHAT THE TREE CAN REPRESENT** -- and this is the half that decides the disposition:\n\n    grep -rn \"ALPHA_PTE_ASM\" src/     ->  ONE hit: the #define itself, alpha_cpu.h:223\n    grep -rn \"apcb_asn\"      src/     ->  written by swpctx, read by a debug print, and\n                                          consulted by NO translation lookup\n\n**`ALPHA_PTE_ASM` IS DECLARED AND HAS ZERO CONSUMERS** -- the same shape as `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM`,\nwhich the round already noted is defined with nothing using it.\n\n> **SO THE HONEST DISCLOSURE IS NOT \"WE SHIPPED A COARSE REPAIR AND DEFERRED THE FINER\n> SEMANTICS\".  IT IS: THE FINER SEMANTICS HAVE NO REPRESENTATION IN THIS EMULATOR'S TRANSLATION\n> CACHE, AND IMPLEMENTING THEM IS A SEPARATE ROUND THAT MUST FIRST ADD THE PLUMBING.**  Over-\n> invalidation is conservative in the safe direction: it costs re-walks, never stale data -- the\n> exact opposite of the defect being fixed.\n\n*This is kimi's open question closed from the other side.  kimi named the deciding fact -- \"do tc\nentries carry an ASN/ASM field?  If they do not, the fine semantics are not even implementable\nhere\" -- agy answered it from the source, and the manual now supplies what the hardware would have\ndone.  **Three seats and a primary source, none of which saw the others.***\n\n**AND A CAUTION ON MY OWN CHECK, because the first grep I would have written is wrong:** the ASM\nbit IS present in the tree as a PTE constant, so a census keyed on *\"does the tree know about\nASM?\"* answers YES and would have contradicted agy.  The right question is *\"does anything CONSUME\nit?\"* -- which is the carrier's own deriver rule: **key on what makes a member a member, not on a\ncorrelate of it.**",
31851:     "hold_question": "`alphatbinegsel` is the one row I RAISED IN SEVERITY MYSELF this session (med -> high), and that ranking is the single judgment in it that is not a measurement.  THE FACTS ARE TWO SEATS' AND WERE RE-VERIFIED BY ME: `ALPHA_TBIA()` is `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)`, `a0` is a uint64_t with no signed cast, so `a0 >= 1` is TRUE for -2 and the call takes the VADDR arm with a1 = 0 -- **the OS primitive meaning 'invalidate every TLB entry' invalidates cached virtual page ZERO and nothing else.**  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) SEVERITY: is high right?  A documented primitive silently under-flushing during ordinary operation, with the fast path then consuming surviving host_load/host_store pointers into stale backing -- I put that with the other HIGH rows; confirm or correct, and note the raise is MINE, not a seat's.  (2) CLAIM SCOPE: the proposed repair `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)` is COARSE -- it fixes the misdispatch and does NOT give `ALPHA_TBIAP()` its ASM=0 selectivity.  Ship the coarse repair with the limit disclosed, or does the round owe the finer semantics?  (3) ORDERING: a file-reading seat ranked `alphaswpctxreg` FIRST and labelled that 'a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential severity' -- explicitly because THIS row's consequences were then unverified.  **They are now verified.**  Does the input to that ranking having changed move this row first?",
73:	exit(1);
85:	exit(1);
155:		    fatal()/exit(1) and substitutes `dev = 0' instead, and `lcabadval'
162:			exit(1);  */
