regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:174:print("alpha_harness_probe -- binary=%s label=%s machine=%s" % (BIN, LABEL, MACH))
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:180:#  CONTROL 1 -- LIVENESS.  A known value through a REAL guest store and a REAL guest load.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:181:#  If the scratch address is not writable RAM this row is RED, which is the point: it tells us
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:182:#  the address is wrong rather than letting a later row blame the emulator.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:183:#  REGISTER NUMBERS, with their DEBUGGER NAMES beside them.  The two are offset by one and
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:184:#  that offset has already produced one wrong row in this file: register 1 prints as "t0",
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:185:#  register 2 as "t1", register 3 as "t2" (ALPHA_REG_NAMES, cpu_alpha.h).
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:189:#  PRINT THE REGISTER THE LOAD ACTUALLY TARGETS.  T2 is register NUMBER 3, and
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:190:#  ALPHA_REG_NAMES calls register 3 "t2" -- an earlier draft printed "t1" (register 2), which
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:191:#  the load never writes, so the row would have been reading an untouched register.  That is
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:192:#  the hand-assembled-encoding trap in its purest form: a wrong register field yields a value
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:193:#  no oracle here would have questioned.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:198:#  CONTROL 2 -- did the CPU execute at all?  A register written by a guest instruction and
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:199:#  nothing else.  It separates "the store/load path is wrong" from "no instruction ran", which
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:200:#  the liveness row alone cannot distinguish -- both present as a wrong value.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:205:#  CONTROL 3 -- THE PALCODE MODE GATE.  `call_pal 0x30' is privileged: in user mode
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:206:#  alpha_palcode() prints "Privileged Alpha PALcode called from user mode" and sets
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:207:#  cpu->running = 0 BEFORE its switch, so the handler never runs.  This row proves we are on the
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:208:#  kernel-mode side of that gate, by asserting the diagnostic is ABSENT and the machine is still
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:209:#  alive after issuing the call.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:210:#
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:211:#  *** IT IS A CONTROL ON A PREMISE THAT WAS ALREADY WRONG ONCE. ***  I wrote in this file's own
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:212:#  header that dispatch has "no gating of any kind"; a seat read alpha_palcode() and corrected
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:213:#  it.  A harness that assumed kernel mode and hit the gate would report a dead machine and look
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:214:#  like a pty or encoding fault -- so the assumption is measured here instead of trusted.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:219:#  *** `palalive' IS VACUOUS AND IS KEPT ONLY AS A LABELLED NON-ORACLE. ***  `alive' means the pty
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:220:#  is still readable.  `alpha_palcode's default arm sets `cpu->running = 0' and STAYS IN THE
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:221:#  DEBUGGER, because -V single-steps -- the emulator does not exit.  So this row cannot distinguish
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:222:#  a served call from a refused one and would stay green either way.  The mode question is answered
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:223:#  by `palgate', which greps for the diagnostic text instead.
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:227:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the swpctx witness
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:228:#  *** THE FIRST DESIGN WAS SELF-DEFEATING AND A SEAT REFUTED IT.  The defect DESTROYS THE PATH
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:229:#  THE WITNESS NEEDS TO OBSERVE IT, and that is why everything below lives in K0SEG. ***
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:230:#
regress/alpha_harness_probe.py:231:#  The naive design -- put both PCBs in ordinary RAM, swpctx twice, read ctx_A+16 -- fails because
